ion; it's a fact that the argument uses to support its own conclusion. So B doesn't seem right.\n\nC) The first is a prediction that, if accurate, would support the main conclusion; the second is a conclusion drawn to support the main conclusion. This makes sense because the first part is a prediction (high school is lower) that, if true, would support the conclusion that CEHE will drop. The second part is the conclusion itself.\n\nD) The first is a prediction supporting the main conclusion; the second is the main conclusion. This is similar to C, but it says the second is the main conclusion, which it is. So both C and D seem possible.\n\nWait, let me think again. The first bolded part is a statement that the argument uses to support its conclusion. So it's a premise that leads to the conclusion. Therefore, the first is a premise (a prediction) that supports the conclusion, which is the main point. So D says the first is a prediction supporting the main conclusion, and the second is the main conclusion. That fits.\n\nBut C says the first is a prediction that, if accurate, would support the main conclusion, and the second is a conclusion drawn to support the main conclusion. So both are correct in a way, but D is more direct because it states that the second is the main conclusion.\n\nWait, but the question is about the roles. The first is a prediction (a premise) that supports the conclusion, which is the main conclusion. So D is correct because it directly states that the second is the main conclusion, and the first supports it.\n\nAlternatively, C is also correct because the first is a prediction that supports the conclusion, and the second is the conclusion drawn. But D is more precise because it explicitly states that the second is the main conclusion.\n\nI think D is the better answer because it directly links the second part as the main conclusion, whereas C is a bit more about the first being a prediction that supports the conclusion, which is also true but D is more specific.\n",
