  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_7450",
    "context": "The literary works of the Chinese writer Mo Yan have made the hidden Chinese vernacular concerned by the readers of the world and recognized by the international literary world, which has once again proved that this judgment is that only the national is the world.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, based on this, which of the following options is correct?",
    "options": [
      "A) It's national, it's world",
      "B) If it is not national, it is not world",
      "C) As long as the nation is, it is the world",
      "D) Only the world is national"
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nThe literary works of the Chinese writer Mo Yan have made the hidden Chinese vernacular concerned by the readers of the world and recognized by the international literary world, which has once again proved that this judgment is that only the national is the world.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, based on this, which of the following options is correct?\n# Options:\nA) It's national, it's world\nB) If it is not national, it is not world\nC) As long as the nation is, it is the world\nD) Only the world is national\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\n1. The context states that Mo Yan's works have made the Chinese vernacular known to the world, and this recognition is only possible through the nation. This implies that the world's recognition is equivalent to the nation.\n\n2. Translating this into logic: If something is recognized by the world (World), then it must be national (Nation). Symbolically, World \u2192 Nation.\n\n3. The contrapositive of this statement is: If it is not national (~Nation), then it is not recognized by the world (~World). Symbolically, ~Nation \u2192 ~World.\n\n4. Option B states exactly this contrapositive, making it the correct answer.\n\nAnswer: B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this question about Mo Yan's literary works. Let me read the context again carefully. It says that Mo Yan's works have made the hidden Chinese vernacular known to the world and recognized by the international literary world. The judgment mentioned is that only the national is the world. Hmm, that's a bit abstract, but I think I get the gist.\n\nSo, the context is talking about how Mo Yan's literature has brought China's vernacular culture to the world, and this achievement is seen as proof that only the national (which I think refers to the nation or national identity) is considered the world. So, the idea is that the national is the ultimate or the only thing that can be recognized as the world.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options A to D is correct based on this information. Let me look at the options again:\n\nA) It's national, it's world\nB) If it is not national, it is not world\nC) As long as the nation is, it is the world\nD) Only the world is national\n\nI need to translate the context into logical statements and see which option aligns with it. The context seems to suggest that the recognition by the world (world view) is only possible through the nation (national identity). So, if something is recognized by the world, it must be national. Alternatively, if it's not national, it can't be recognized by the world.\n\nLet me break it down. The context says that Mo Yan's works have made the Chinese vernacular known to the world, and this is because only the national is the world. So, the connection is that the world (recognition) is tied to the nation (Chinese identity). So, if something is recognized by the world, it must be national. That would mean that the world only recognizes the nation, so the world is tied to the nation.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A: It's national, it's world. So, if it's national, then it's world. That seems to fit because the context says that the world is recognized only through the nation. So, if something is national, it's world.\n\nOption B: If it is not national, it is not world. This is the contrapositive of option A. If something isn't national, then it can't be world. That also makes sense because the world only recognizes the nation.\n\nOption C: As long as the nation is, it is the world. This seems similar to option A but phrased differently. It suggests that nation implies world, which is correct.\n\nOption D: Only the world is national. This seems a bit confusing. It suggests that the only thing that can be national is the world, which doesn't quite fit because the context is about the world recognizing the nation, not the nation being the world.\n\nWait, maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me think again. The context says that the recognition by the world is only possible through the nation. So, the world (recognition) is equivalent to the nation. So, if something is recognized by the world, it must be national, and if it's national, it's recognized by the world.\n\nSo, the correct logical statement would be: If something is world (recognized by the world), then it is national. Or, if it's not national, then it's not world. Both A and B seem to fit, but let me check the options again.\n\nWait, the options are:\n\nA) It's national, it's world. So, if it's national, then it's world.\n\nB) If it is not national, it is not world. So, if not national, then not world.\n\nC) As long as the nation is, it is the world. So, if nation, then world.\n\nD) Only the world is national. So, the only thing that can be national is the world.\n\nWait, but the context says that the world is recognized because of the nation. So, the world (recognition) is a result of the nation. So, the correct statement would be that the world is equivalent to the nation, meaning that if something is world, it's national, and if it's national, it's world.\n\nSo, both A and B are correct in a way, but looking at the options, A is \"It's national, it's world,\" which is a direct translation of the context. B is the contrapositive, which is also correct. But in logic, both are equivalent.\n\nWait, but the options are separate, so which one is correct? Let me think about the structure. The context says that the recognition by the world is only possible through the nation. So, the world (recognition) is a result of the nation. So, if something is recognized by the world, it must be national. So, the statement would be: If it's world, then it's national. But that's not one of the options.\n\nWait, maybe I'm misunderstanding. The context says that the recognition by the world is only possible through the nation, meaning that the world's recognition is equivalent to the nation. So, the world is the same as the nation in terms of recognition. So, if something is recognized by the world, it's national, and if it's national, it's recognized by the world.\n\nSo, the correct options would be A and B, but since they are separate, I need to see which one is correct.\n\nWait, the options are:\n\nA) It's national, it's world.\n\nB) If it is not national, it is not world.\n\nC) As long as the nation is, it is the world.\n\nD) Only the world is national.\n\nSo, A and C are similar, both saying that nation implies world. B is the contrapositive of A, which is also correct. D is the opposite, saying that only the world is national, which is incorrect because the context says that the world is recognized by the nation, not the other way around.\n\nSo, the correct options would be A and B, but since the question is asking for one correct option, I need to see which one is the best fit.\n\nWait, the context says that the recognition by the world is only possible through the nation, meaning that the world is equivalent to the nation. So, the correct statement would be that if something is world, it's national, and if it's national, it's world. So, both A and B are correct, but since they are separate options, I need to choose the one that directly reflects the context.\n\nOption A directly states that if it's national, then it's world, which aligns with the context. Option B is the contrapositive, which is also correct but might be a bit more abstract.\n\nWait, but the question is asking which option is correct based on the information. So, both A and B are correct, but perhaps the intended answer is A because it's more straightforward.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct answer is B because it's the contrapositive, which is a logical equivalent. But I'm not sure. Let me think again.\n\nThe context says that the recognition by the world is only possible through the nation. So, the world (recognition) is a result of the nation. So, if something is recognized by the world, it must be national. So, the statement would be: If it's world, then it's national. But that's not an option. The options are about the relationship between nation and world.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct answer is B because it's the contrapositive. If it's not national, then it's not world. That makes sense because if something isn't national, it can't be recognized by the world. So, B is correct.\n\nAlternatively, A says that if it's national, then it's world, which is also correct. So, both A and B are correct, but perhaps the question expects one answer. Let me check the options again.\n\nWait, the options are:\n\nA) It's national, it's world.\n\nB) If it is not national, it is not world.\n\nC) As long as the nation is, it is the world.\n\nD) Only the world is national.\n\nSo, both A and B are correct, but perhaps the intended answer is B because it's the contrapositive, which is a more formal logical statement. Alternatively, A is more direct.\n\nWait, the context says that the recognition by the world is only possible through the nation, meaning that the world is equivalent to the nation. So, the correct statement would be that if something is world, it's national, and if it's national, it's world. So, both A and B are correct, but since the options are separate, perhaps the answer is B because it's the contrapositive, which is a more precise logical statement.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is A because it's more straightforward. I'm a bit confused now.\n\nWait, let me think about the logical structure. The context can be translated as: If something is recognized by the world, then it is national. So, World \u2192 Nation. The contrapositive would be: If it's not national, then it's not world. So, ~Nation \u2192 ~World, which is option B.\n\nSo, the correct answer is B because it's the contrapositive, which is logically equivalent to the original statement. Therefore, the correct option is B.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
