  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_3551",
    "context": "National pricing: refers to the commodity prices and charging standards set by the government price department and the competent business department in accordance with the authority of the state.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following is not subject to national pricing?",
    "options": [
      "A) A well-known shopping mall has newly imported a batch of high-end cashmere sweaters. for the credibility of the shopping mall, the boss decided to sell the new products at a 30% discount in the first three days of the market.",
      "B) On the eve of the start of school in the spring of 2005, the education bureau of a certain city issued a notice about the tuition and miscellaneous fees of various schools in the new semester, and asked the school to announce it.",
      "C) On the eve of the spring festival, in order to safeguard the interests of the general public and stabilize the order of the holiday market, the municipal price bureau approved and announced the maximum prices of some commodities such as poultry eggs, vegetables, and aquatic products.",
      "D) The charging standards for buses in nanjing are clearly: 1 yuan per ride for ordinary unmanned ticket vending vehicles, 2 yuan per ride for air-conditioned unmanned ticket vending vehicles, and 20% off for passengers with bus cards."
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nNational pricing: refers to the commodity prices and charging standards set by the government price department and the competent business department in accordance with the authority of the state.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is not subject to national pricing?\n# Options:\nA) A well-known shopping mall has newly imported a batch of high-end cashmere sweaters. for the credibility of the shopping mall, the boss decided to sell the new products at a 30% discount in the first three days of the market.\nB) On the eve of the start of school in the spring of 2005, the education bureau of a certain city issued a notice about the tuition and miscellaneous fees of various schools in the new semester, and asked the school to announce it.\nC) On the eve of the spring festival, in order to safeguard the interests of the general public and stabilize the order of the holiday market, the municipal price bureau approved and announced the maximum prices of some commodities such as poultry eggs, vegetables, and aquatic products.\nD) The charging standards for buses in nanjing are clearly: 1 yuan per ride for ordinary unmanned ticket vending vehicles, 2 yuan per ride for air-conditioned unmanned ticket vending vehicles, and 20% off for passengers with bus cards.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n1. National pricing involves government or business regulations.\n2. Options B, C, and D involve government-set standards (tuition, food prices, bus fares).\n3. Option A is a mall's pricing decision, not necessarily government-regulated.\n4. Therefore, A is not subject to national pricing.\n\nThe correct option is: A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option is not subject to national pricing based on the given context. Let me start by understanding what the context says. National pricing refers to the commodity prices and charging standards set by government or business departments. So, anything that's regulated by these standards would be subject to national pricing.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A: A shopping mall sells new cashmere sweaters at a 30% discount. The boss did this for credibility. I'm not sure if mall sales fall under government regulation. Maybe they have their own pricing, but the context doesn't mention malls being subject to national pricing.\n\nOption B: Education bureau issued tuition notices. Education is a public service, so it's likely regulated by national pricing.\n\nOption C: Price bureau approved food commodities. This directly mentions the price bureau, so definitely subject to national pricing.\n\nOption D: Bus charging standards are clearly defined. This is a clear example of government-set standards, so subject to national pricing.\n\nSo, the odd one out is Option A because it's a shopping mall, which might not be under government regulation. The others are all examples of government-set prices or regulations.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_11377",
    "context": "In mountainous regions, the timberline is the highest altitude at which trees grow. In the Rocky Mountains, the current timberline is at the altitude above which growing season temperatures remain cooler than 10 degrees centigrade. Fossilized remains of trees that grew 10, 000 years ago have been found 100 meters above the current Rocky Mountain timberline. Clearly, ttherefore, the climate of the Rocky Mountains is cooler now than it was 10, 000 years ago.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following is an assumption on which the argument relies?",
    "options": [
      "A) In the past 10, 000 years, the only trees to have grown above today's timberline are the trees whose fossilized remains been found.",
      "B) The climate of the rocky mountains has never been significantly warmer than during the lifetime of the trees whose fossilized remains have been found.",
      "C) The fossils are not of species of trees that were able to tolerate cooler growing temperatures than are the species that currently grow near the timberline.",
      "D) No trees grew 10, 000 years ago at altitudes higher than the ones at which fossilized tree remains have been found."
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nIn mountainous regions, the timberline is the highest altitude at which trees grow. In the Rocky Mountains, the current timberline is at the altitude above which growing season temperatures remain cooler than 10 degrees centigrade. Fossilized remains of trees that grew 10, 000 years ago have been found 100 meters above the current Rocky Mountain timberline. Clearly, ttherefore, the climate of the Rocky Mountains is cooler now than it was 10, 000 years ago.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is an assumption on which the argument relies?\n# Options:\nA) In the past 10, 000 years, the only trees to have grown above today's timberline are the trees whose fossilized remains been found.\nB) The climate of the rocky mountains has never been significantly warmer than during the lifetime of the trees whose fossilized remains have been found.\nC) The fossils are not of species of trees that were able to tolerate cooler growing temperatures than are the species that currently grow near the timberline.\nD) No trees grew 10, 000 years ago at altitudes higher than the ones at which fossilized tree remains have been found.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe argument assumes that the fossilized trees could not have tolerated cooler temperatures than those currently present near the Rocky Mountain timberline, which supports the conclusion that the climate has become cooler. \n\nAnswer: C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read through the context and the question again to make sure I understand everything.\n\nThe context says that in mountainous regions, the timberline is the highest altitude where trees grow. In the Rocky Mountains, the current timberline is at an altitude where the growing season temperatures are cooler than 10 degrees Celsius. They found fossilized tree remains 100 meters above the current timberline, and the argument concludes that the Rocky Mountains' climate is cooler now than it was 10,000 years ago.\n\nThe question is asking which assumption the argument relies on. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nFirst, I need to break down the argument. The main point is that because trees from 10,000 years ago grew 100 meters higher than the current timberline, the climate must be cooler now. So the argument is inferring that the current environment's conditions (temperature) have changed enough to allow trees to grow higher.\n\nNow, looking at the options, I need to find which one is an assumption without which the argument wouldn't hold.\n\nOption A says that the only trees above today's timberline are the ones whose fossils have been found. Hmm, but the argument is about the climate being cooler now, not necessarily about the types of trees. So maybe this isn't the main assumption. It seems more like a statement about what's been found, not an assumption needed for the argument.\n\nOption B states that the climate has never been significantly warmer than during the trees' lifetimes. If the climate had been significantly warmer in the past, those trees might not have grown that high. So this seems relevant because it's assuming that the current conditions are the only ones allowing the trees to grow higher. But I'm not sure if this is the exact assumption or if there's another better fit.\n\nOption C claims that the fossils are not of trees that could tolerate cooler temperatures than those currently near the timberline. This is important because if those trees could grow at higher altitudes in the past, then the argument's conclusion that the climate is cooler now wouldn't hold. So this seems like a strong candidate because it directly addresses whether the trees could have grown where they did without the current conditions.\n\nOption D says no trees grew 10,000 years ago at higher altitudes than the ones found. If trees did grow higher in the past, that would weaken the argument's conclusion. So this is another possible assumption because it's assuming that the trees found are the highest ones from that time, making the argument's point valid.\n\nNow, I need to determine which one is the main assumption. The argument is saying that because trees from 10,000 years ago grew 100 meters above the current timberline, the climate is cooler now. The key here is that the trees from the past couldn't grow that high unless the climate was cooler. So the argument assumes that the trees from the past couldn't grow above the current timberline unless the climate was cooler. \n\nLooking at the options, Option C is about whether the fossils are of trees that could tolerate cooler temperatures. If those trees could have grown at higher altitudes even without a cooler climate, then the argument wouldn't hold. So Option C is directly addressing that concern, making it the assumption the argument relies on.\n\nOption D is about whether any trees from the past grew higher than the ones found. If they did, then the argument's conclusion wouldn't be valid. But the argument only mentions the fossilized remains found at a certain altitude, not whether there were others higher. So while D is relevant, it's not as directly tied to the argument's conclusion as C is.\n\nTherefore, the correct assumption is that the fossils are not of species that could tolerate cooler temperatures than those currently growing near the timberline.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_6657",
    "context": "Scientists found that men who underwent the same surgery and took the same painkillers experienced less pain relief and were less effective for less time than women.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, according to the above text, which of the following is the meaning of expression?",
    "options": [
      "A) Some painkillers only work on women, not men",
      "B) Men who took the same painkillers after surgery felt pain more quickly than women",
      "C) Men are less able to tolerate pain than women",
      "D) The same painkiller works better for women than for men"
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nScientists found that men who underwent the same surgery and took the same painkillers experienced less pain relief and were less effective for less time than women.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above text, which of the following is the meaning of expression?\n# Options:\nA) Some painkillers only work on women, not men\nB) Men who took the same painkillers after surgery felt pain more quickly than women\nC) Men are less able to tolerate pain than women\nD) The same painkiller works better for women than for men\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nThe context indicates that men experienced less pain relief and were less effective with the painkillers compared to women. This suggests that the painkillers are more effective for women, meaning the same painkiller works better for women than for men.\n\nThe correct option is: D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out the answer to this question based on the given context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context says that scientists found that men who underwent the same surgery and took the same painkillers experienced less pain relief and were less effective for less time than women. Hmm, so both men and women had the same surgery and took the same painkillers, but the outcome was different. The men had less pain relief and were less effective for less time compared to women.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options (A, B, C, D) correctly interprets the meaning of the expression in the context. Let me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"Some painkillers only work on women, not men.\" Wait, the context doesn't say that the painkillers don't work on men. It just says that men experienced less relief. So maybe the painkillers do work on men, but just not as effectively. So A might not be correct because it's making a stronger statement than what's given.\n\nOption B: \"Men who took the same painkillers after surgery felt pain more quickly than women.\" The context says men had less pain relief and were less effective for less time. So if they felt pain more quickly, that would mean they had more pain sooner, which might lead to less relief. But the context doesn't specify the timing of when they felt pain, just that they were less effective overall. So I'm not sure if B is correct. It's possible, but I'm not certain.\n\nOption C: \"Men are less able to tolerate pain than women.\" The context mentions less pain relief and less effectiveness, which could imply that men are less able to tolerate pain. That seems plausible. If they can't tolerate it as well, they wouldn't feel the relief as effectively.\n\nOption D: \"The same painkiller works better for women than for men.\" This seems similar to C. The context says the painkillers worked less effectively on men, so maybe they're less effective, meaning they work worse for men. So D could be correct, but I need to think about the wording.\n\nWait, the context says men had less pain relief and were less effective for less time. So the painkillers worked better for women. So D is saying the painkillers work better for women, which aligns with the context. But C is about men being less able to tolerate pain, which is a different statement. The context doesn't directly say that men can't tolerate pain, just that they had less relief.\n\nSo between B, C, and D. Let me think again. The question is about the meaning of the expression, which is the context provided. The context is about the effectiveness of the painkillers. So the correct interpretation would be that the painkillers are less effective for men, meaning they work better for women. So D seems correct.\n\nBut wait, option B is about feeling pain more quickly. The context doesn't mention anything about the timing of when they felt pain, just that they were less effective overall. So B might not be correct because it's not stated that men felt pain more quickly, just that they had less relief overall.\n\nOption C is about men being less able to tolerate pain. The context doesn't provide information about their ability to tolerate pain, just that they had less relief. So C might not be correct because it's inferring something not directly stated.\n\nSo the correct answer should be D: The same painkiller works better for women than for men.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_14023",
    "context": "A survey of address changes filed with post offices and driver' s license bureaus over the last ten years has established that households moving out of the city of Weston outnumbered households moving into the city two to one. Ttherefore, we can expect that next year' s census, which counts all residents regardless of age, will show that the population of Weston has declined since the last census ten years ago.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which one of the following, if true, most helps strengthen the argument?",
    "options": [
      "A) Many people moving into weston failed to notify either the post office or the driver's license bureau that they had moved to the city.",
      "B) Most people moving out of weston were young adults who were hoping to begin a career elsewhere, whereas most adults remaining in or moving into the city had long-standing jobs in the city.",
      "C) Over the past century any census of weston showing a population loss was followed ten years later by a census showing a population gain.",
      "D) Most adults moving out of weston were parents who had children living with them, whereas most adults remaining in or moving into the city were older people who lived alone."
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nA survey of address changes filed with post offices and driver' s license bureaus over the last ten years has established that households moving out of the city of Weston outnumbered households moving into the city two to one. Ttherefore, we can expect that next year' s census, which counts all residents regardless of age, will show that the population of Weston has declined since the last census ten years ago.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which one of the following, if true, most helps strengthen the argument?\n# Options:\nA) Many people moving into weston failed to notify either the post office or the driver's license bureau that they had moved to the city.\nB) Most people moving out of weston were young adults who were hoping to begin a career elsewhere, whereas most adults remaining in or moving into the city had long-standing jobs in the city.\nC) Over the past century any census of weston showing a population loss was followed ten years later by a census showing a population gain.\nD) Most adults moving out of weston were parents who had children living with them, whereas most adults remaining in or moving into the city were older people who lived alone.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\nThe argument states that the population of Weston has declined because more people moved out than moved in over the past ten years. To strengthen this argument, we need to consider why the population decline is expected.\n\nOption D explains that most people moving out are parents with children, who may have a more significant impact on the city's population, while those moving in are older adults living alone. This suggests that the population decline is more pronounced because the people moving in are less impactful, supporting the argument that the population has indeed declined.\n\nAnswer: D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option would best strengthen the argument presented in the context. The context says that over the last ten years, more households moved out of Weston than moved in, specifically a two to one ratio. The argument then concludes that the population will decline next year based on the census.\n\nFirst, I need to understand what the argument is saying. It's using the fact that more people left than came in over ten years to predict a decline in population. But wait, population change isn't just about moving in and out; it's also about the number of people moving in and out. So, if more people left, the population would go down, right?\n\nNow, the question is asking which option would strengthen this argument. So, I need to find an option that provides additional support for why the population decline is expected.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A says that many people who moved into Weston didn't notify the post office or driver's license bureau. Hmm, so this means that the data about incoming residents might be incomplete. If they didn't notify, maybe they didn't actually move, or maybe they moved but didn't register. But how does this affect the population count? If they didn't notify, the number of people moving in might be underestimated. So, if the census counts all residents regardless of age, and if some people moved in but didn't notify, the population might not decrease as much as the argument suggests. Wait, but the argument is that the population has declined because more left than came in. If the number of people coming in is underestimated, then the actual number of people moving in is higher, which would mean the population might not decline as much. So, this might actually weaken the argument because it's suggesting that the population decline isn't as bad as it seems. So, A might not be the right answer.\n\nOption B states that most people moving out were young adults hoping to start a career elsewhere, while most people staying or moving in were adults with long-term jobs. This seems to talk about the reasons for moving in and out. But how does that strengthen the population decline? It might explain why the moving out is happening, but it doesn't directly address whether the number of people moving in is sufficient to offset the out-migration. So, maybe B doesn't directly strengthen the argument about population decline.\n\nOption C says that over the past century, any census showing a population loss was followed by a gain ten years later. This seems to suggest that population trends might reverse over time. But does that strengthen the argument? The argument is about the last ten years, and this option is about a longer-term trend. It might imply that even if the census shows a loss now, it could reverse, which would actually weaken the argument because it suggests the population might not decline as expected. So, C might not be the right choice.\n\nOption D mentions that most adults moving out were parents with children, while most remaining or moving in were older adults living alone. This seems relevant because if the people moving out are parents with children, they might have a different impact on the population compared to adults moving in. For example, parents with children might be contributing more to the local community, like through schools or community activities, which could offset the population decline. Alternatively, if the people moving in are older adults living alone, they might not be contributing as much to the local economy or community, which could mean the population decline is more significant. Wait, but the argument is that the population has declined because more left than came in. If the people moving in are older adults, maybe they're more likely to stay in the city, so the population decline might not be as severe. But I'm not sure. Alternatively, if the people moving out are parents with children, they might be taking their kids out of the city, which could affect the population more directly. Hmm, I'm a bit confused here.\n\nWait, let me think again. The argument is that the population has declined because more people left than came in. To strengthen this, we need to ensure that the number of people moving in is not significantly higher than those moving out. Option D suggests that the people moving out are parents with children, which might mean they have a larger impact on the city's population because they might be more likely to have children staying in the city. On the other hand, the people moving in are older adults living alone, which might not affect the population as much because they're staying in the city but not contributing as much to the local dynamics. So, this could mean that the population decline is more pronounced because the people moving in are less impactful, thus supporting the argument that the population has declined.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the people moving in are older adults, so they might not be contributing as much to the city's economy or community, making the population decline more significant. So, D could be the right answer because it provides a reason why the population decline is expected.\n\nWait, but I'm not entirely sure. Let me compare the options again. Option A suggests that the number of people moving in is underestimated, which would mean the population decline isn't as bad. So, A would weaken the argument. Option B is about reasons for moving, which doesn't directly address the numbers. Option C is about a trend that might reverse, which weakens the argument. Option D is about the types of people moving in and out, which could mean that the population decline is more significant because the people moving in are less impactful, thus strengthening the argument.\n\nSo, I think D is the correct answer because it provides a reason why the population decline is expected based on the types of people moving in and out.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_6259",
    "context": "A report by American scientists pointed out that the comprehensive value created by the coastal wetland ecosystem per hectare is 4025 dollars per year, which is equivalent to twice the tropical rain forest in the same area, or 13 times that of other forests, 17 times that of grassland, or 44 times that of farmland!",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following is not the correct understanding of value in the sentence?",
    "options": [
      "A) Coastal wetlands are rich in biodiversity.",
      "B) Special ecosystem coastal wetland plays an irreplaceable role in global ecological balance.",
      "C) Coastal wetlands have high biological productivity and important coastal protection functions.",
      "D) Coastal wetlands can create high national economic value for coastal areas."
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nA report by American scientists pointed out that the comprehensive value created by the coastal wetland ecosystem per hectare is 4025 dollars per year, which is equivalent to twice the tropical rain forest in the same area, or 13 times that of other forests, 17 times that of grassland, or 44 times that of farmland!\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is not the correct understanding of value in the sentence?\n# Options:\nA) Coastal wetlands are rich in biodiversity.\nB) Special ecosystem coastal wetland plays an irreplaceable role in global ecological balance.\nC) Coastal wetlands have high biological productivity and important coastal protection functions.\nD) Coastal wetlands can create high national economic value for coastal areas.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n1. The context highlights the high value of coastal wetlands compared to other ecosystems but doesn't mention their irreplaceable role globally.\n2. Option B introduces a global ecological balance aspect not covered in the context.\n3. Therefore, B is the incorrect understanding.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option is not a correct understanding of the value mentioned in the context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it properly.\n\nThe context says that a coastal wetland ecosystem creates a comprehensive value of 4025 dollars per year per hectare. It's twice the value of a tropical rainforest in the same area and 13 times that of other forests, 17 times that of grassland, and 44 times that of farmland. So, the wetlands are significantly valuable compared to other ecosystems.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options is not a correct understanding of the value mentioned. Let's go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Coastal wetlands are rich in biodiversity. Hmm, the context doesn't explicitly mention biodiversity, but it does talk about the value created. I know that biodiversity is often a component of ecosystem value, so maybe this is inferred. But I'm not sure if it's directly stated or just an assumption.\n\nOption B: Special ecosystem coastal wetland plays an irreplaceable role in global ecological balance. The context doesn't talk about global ecological balance, only about the value per hectare compared to other ecosystems. So this might be stretching it. It's possible that the wetlands are important, but the context doesn't specify their irreplaceable role globally.\n\nOption C: Coastal wetlands have high biological productivity and important coastal protection functions. The context mentions that the value is high, which often relates to productivity and ecological functions. So this seems correct because high value usually comes from high productivity and important functions like protection.\n\nOption D: Coastal wetlands can create high national economic value for coastal areas. The context does say that the value is 4025 dollars per year per hectare, which relates to economic value. So this is definitely correct.\n\nNow, looking back at the options, A and C are likely correct because they relate to the value mentioned. D is correct because it's directly stated. B, on the other hand, is about global ecological balance, which isn't mentioned in the context. The context only compares wetlands to other ecosystems in the same area, not globally. So B is not supported by the given information.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be B.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_12793",
    "context": "Essayist: Some researchers criticize British governmental security agencies for not releasing enough information about sightings of unidentified flying objects (UFOs) made by these military and intelligence agencies. Requests for information by civilian researchers are brushed aside. This leads one to suspect that there have been spacecraft sighted near Earth that are extraterrestrial in origin.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which one of the following, if true, would most strengthen the essayist's argument?",
    "options": [
      "A) The british government would deny the requests by civilian researchers to have access to the data only if this government had something to hide.",
      "B) The british government is less trusting of civilian researchers than it is of military researchers.",
      "C) The british government would withhold information pertaining to ufos only if it were established that they were from other planets.",
      "D) The british government has always attempted to deny the existence of ufos."
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nEssayist: Some researchers criticize British governmental security agencies for not releasing enough information about sightings of unidentified flying objects (UFOs) made by these military and intelligence agencies. Requests for information by civilian researchers are brushed aside. This leads one to suspect that there have been spacecraft sighted near Earth that are extraterrestrial in origin.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which one of the following, if true, would most strengthen the essayist's argument?\n# Options:\nA) The british government would deny the requests by civilian researchers to have access to the data only if this government had something to hide.\nB) The british government is less trusting of civilian researchers than it is of military researchers.\nC) The british government would withhold information pertaining to ufos only if it were established that they were from other planets.\nD) The british government has always attempted to deny the existence of ufos.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe reasoning is that Option C directly links the withholding of information to the UFOs being from other planets, which supports the essayist's argument that the lack of information is evidence of extraterrestrial origins.",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option would most strengthen the essayist's argument. The essayist is saying that because British government security agencies aren't releasing information about UFO sightings, especially to civilian researchers, it suggests that there might be extraterrestrial spacecraft near Earth. So, the argument is that the lack of information is evidence of something beyond Earth.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options, if true, would most strengthen this argument. Let's break down each option one by one.\n\nOption A says that the British government would deny access to data only if they had something to hide. Hmm, so if they're withholding information, it's because they think they have something to hide. That makes sense because if they had nothing to hide, they might just share the info. This seems relevant because it directly links withholding info to potential hidden motives, which could be extraterrestrial.\n\nOption B states that the government is less trusting of civilian researchers than military ones. That's interesting. If they're less trusting of civilians, that might explain why they're withholding info. But does this directly support the idea of extraterrestrial spacecraft? It does show a pattern, but maybe not as strong as Option A.\n\nOption C claims the government would withhold info only if it's established that the UFOs are from other planets. Wait, that's actually the opposite of what the essayist is arguing. The essayist is suggesting that UFOs are extraterrestrial, so if the government only withholds info when it's from other planets, that would support the argument. But the option says they would withhold info only if it's established that they're from other planets, which might mean they don't withhold info for other reasons. Hmm, maybe this is a bit confusing. If they only withhold info when it's from other planets, that actually supports the idea that UFOs are extraterrestrial because they're withholding info only in that case. So this could be a strong option.\n\nOption D says the government has always denied the existence of UFOs. That's a strong statement, but does it directly relate to the argument? The argument is about why they're withholding info now, not about past behavior. So maybe this isn't the best option because it doesn't explain the current withholding of information.\n\nNow, comparing the options. Option A suggests that withholding info is because of something to hide, which could be extraterrestrial. Option C says they only withhold info if it's from other planets, which also supports the idea. But Option A is more about the reason behind withholding info, while Option C is about the conditions under which they withhold info. \n\nWait, the essayist's argument is that the lack of info is evidence of extraterrestrial origins. So, if the government only withholds info when it's from other planets (Option C), that directly supports the argument because it's a condition tied to extraterrestrial UFOs. On the other hand, Option A is more about the motive (hiding something) rather than the specific reason related to extraterrestrial origins.\n\nSo, I think Option C is the strongest because it directly ties the withholding of information to the UFOs being from other planets, which is exactly what the essayist is arguing. Option A is also relevant but doesn't specifically address the extraterrestrial aspect as directly as Option C.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_6777",
    "context": "At present, Tamil, a specific drug for bird flu, has not been registered by the Ministry of Health in Vietnam, so it is difficult to circulate widely in Vietnam.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following options is the meaning of this sentence?",
    "options": [
      "A) There is a shortage of drugs to treat bird flu in vietnam.",
      "B) There is no medicine for bird flu in vietnam.",
      "C) It will take some time for bird flu specific drugs to circulate widely in vietnam.",
      "D) The ministry of health of vietnam should register tami fluorocarbons as soon as possible."
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nAt present, Tamil, a specific drug for bird flu, has not been registered by the Ministry of Health in Vietnam, so it is difficult to circulate widely in Vietnam.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following options is the meaning of this sentence?\n# Options:\nA) There is a shortage of drugs to treat bird flu in vietnam.\nB) There is no medicine for bird flu in vietnam.\nC) It will take some time for bird flu specific drugs to circulate widely in vietnam.\nD) The ministry of health of vietnam should register tami fluorocarbons as soon as possible.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: A) There is a shortage of drugs to treat bird flu in vietnam.\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\nThe context states that Tamil, a specific bird flu drug, hasn't been registered in Vietnam, making it difficult to distribute widely. This implies a lack of availability, which can be interpreted as a shortage. While option C suggests it will take time, the context doesn't specify time constraints, so A is the best fit.\n\nThe correct option is: A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out the answer to this question based on the given context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context says: \"At present, Tamil, a specific drug for bird flu, has not been registered by the Ministry of Health in Vietnam, so it is difficult to circulate widely in Vietnam.\"\n\nAlright, so Tamil is a drug for bird flu, but it hasn't been registered by the Vietnamese Ministry of Health. Because of this, it's hard to spread it widely in Vietnam. So, what does this mean?\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) There is a shortage of drugs to treat bird flu in Vietnam.\nB) There is no medicine for bird flu in Vietnam.\nC) It will take some time for bird flu specific drugs to circulate widely in Vietnam.\nD) The ministry of health of Vietnam should register Tamil as soon as possible.\n\nI need to determine which option correctly interprets the context.\n\nFirst, let's break down the context. Tamil is a specific drug, so it's a medicine for bird flu. It hasn't been registered, which means it's not officially available or recognized in Vietnam. The fact that it's difficult to circulate widely suggests that it's either not available or there's a problem with distribution.\n\nOption A says there's a shortage of drugs. The context doesn't mention anything about other drugs being short or unavailable. It only talks about Tamil not being registered. So maybe there's a shortage, but the context doesn't explicitly state that. It's possible, but not directly supported.\n\nOption B claims there's no medicine for bird flu in Vietnam. That's not true because Tamil is a specific drug mentioned, so there is at least one medicine available, just not registered. So B is incorrect.\n\nOption C suggests it will take time for the drugs to circulate. The context says it's difficult to circulate widely now, which might imply that it's not yet available or is in the process of being distributed. So maybe it's just not widely available yet, but it doesn't necessarily mean it will take some time. It could be that registration is pending, which would take time, but the context doesn't specify that it's taking time now; it's just difficult to distribute.\n\nOption D says the ministry should register Tamil as soon as possible. The context does mention that Tamil hasn't been registered, so it's logical to infer that registering it would help in distributing it widely. So D seems correct because it's a direct consequence of Tamil not being registered.\n\nWait, but the question is about the meaning of the sentence, not what should be done. So the sentence is explaining why Tamil can't be widely distributed, which is because it's not registered. So the meaning is that the drug hasn't been registered, making it difficult to spread.\n\nLooking back at the options, A talks about shortage, which isn't directly stated. C talks about taking time, which might be a possible reason, but the context doesn't specify that it's taking time now; it's just difficult. D is more about action, which isn't the meaning of the sentence. The sentence is stating a fact: Tamil isn't registered, so it's hard to distribute.\n\nSo the correct interpretation is that Tamil isn't registered, making distribution difficult. That aligns with option C, which says it will take some time for the drug to circulate. Wait, but the context doesn't say it will take time; it's just difficult now. So maybe C is not the best answer.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct answer is A because the lack of registration could imply a shortage. But the context doesn't mention other drugs being unavailable. So maybe A is not correct.\n\nAlternatively, the correct answer might be C because the fact that it's difficult to circulate widely could mean that it's not yet available or is in the process of being distributed, which would take time. But the context doesn't explicitly say it's taking time, just that it's difficult.\n\nHmm, I'm a bit confused. Let me think again.\n\nThe sentence is: Tamil hasn't been registered, so it's difficult to distribute widely. So the meaning is that the drug hasn't been registered, leading to distribution difficulties. So the correct option should reflect that the drug isn't registered, making it hard to spread.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Shortage of drugs. Not necessarily stated.\n\nB) No medicine. Incorrect because Tamil is a medicine.\n\nC) It will take time. Maybe, but the context doesn't say it's taking time now, just that it's difficult.\n\nD) The ministry should register. It's a consequence, not the meaning.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct answer is C because the fact that it's difficult to distribute widely could imply that it's not yet available or is in the process of being distributed, which would take time. But the context doesn't specify that it's taking time, just that it's difficult.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct answer is A because the lack of registration could imply a shortage. But I'm not sure.\n\nWait, the context says it's difficult to circulate widely, not that there's a shortage. So maybe the correct answer is C, but I'm not entirely confident.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct answer is A because the drug isn't registered, so there's a shortage. But I'm not sure if that's the case.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct answer is C because the drug hasn't been registered, so it's not available yet, which would take time to get distributed.\n\nI think I'm overcomplicating this. The sentence is stating that Tamil isn't registered, making it difficult to distribute. So the meaning is that there's a lack of registration, leading to distribution difficulties. So the correct option should reflect that.\n\nLooking at the options again:\n\nA) Shortage of drugs. Not necessarily stated.\n\nB) No medicine. Incorrect.\n\nC) It will take some time. Maybe, but the context doesn't say it's taking time now.\n\nD) The ministry should register. It's a consequence, not the meaning.\n\nWait, perhaps the correct answer is A because the drug isn't registered, so there's a shortage. But I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct answer is C because the drug hasn't been registered, so it's not available yet, which would take time to distribute.\n\nI think the correct answer is A because the drug isn't registered, leading to a shortage. But I'm not entirely confident. Alternatively, maybe it's C.\n\nWait, the context says it's difficult to circulate widely, not that it's taking time. So maybe the correct answer is C, but I'm not sure.\n\nI think I'll go with A because the drug isn't registered, implying a shortage. But I'm not 100% sure.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_4259",
    "context": "In order to implement the cultural strategy of strengthening the city, a city built two libraries in 2008 and 2010. At the end of 2008, a total of 70,000 citizens' library cards were issued, and a total of 130,000 citizens' library cards were issued by the end of 2010. In 2011, the city established a third library in the new district, which was completed and opened in early 2012. As of the end of 2012, the city had handled more than 200,000 library cards for citizens. The city government therefore believed that this measure was effective, because in just four years, the number of citizens coming into the libraries has nearly tripled.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which one of the followings, if true, can weaken the above conclusion the most?",
    "options": [
      "A) The library need to constantly purchase new books, and the maintenance cost is also high, which will affect the construction of other cultural facilities in the city.",
      "B) There are two universities in the city, and many students have also applied for library cards for these three libraries.",
      "C) Many citizens who applied for the library card for the first library also applied for the library card for the other two libraries.",
      "D) The construction of the new district of the city was developing rapidly, and a large number of migrants have poured into the new district in 4 years."
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nIn order to implement the cultural strategy of strengthening the city, a city built two libraries in 2008 and 2010. At the end of 2008, a total of 70,000 citizens' library cards were issued, and a total of 130,000 citizens' library cards were issued by the end of 2010. In 2011, the city established a third library in the new district, which was completed and opened in early 2012. As of the end of 2012, the city had handled more than 200,000 library cards for citizens. The city government therefore believed that this measure was effective, because in just four years, the number of citizens coming into the libraries has nearly tripled.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which one of the followings, if true, can weaken the above conclusion the most?\n# Options:\nA) The library need to constantly purchase new books, and the maintenance cost is also high, which will affect the construction of other cultural facilities in the city.\nB) There are two universities in the city, and many students have also applied for library cards for these three libraries.\nC) Many citizens who applied for the library card for the first library also applied for the library card for the other two libraries.\nD) The construction of the new district of the city was developing rapidly, and a large number of migrants have poured into the new district in 4 years.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nThe reasoning is that option D introduces an external factor (migration) that could explain the increase in library card applications without necessarily indicating that the new libraries are attracting more people. This directly weakens the conclusion that the libraries' construction was effective in increasing library usage.\n\nThe correct option is: D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option weakens the conclusion the most. The conclusion is that the city's measure of building three libraries was effective because the number of citizens using the libraries nearly tripled in four years. \n\nLet me break down the context first. In 2008, they built two libraries and had 70,000 cards issued by the end of that year. By 2010, they had 130,000 cards. Then in 2011, they built a third library, which opened early in 2012. By the end of 2012, they had over 200,000 cards. The government concluded that this measure was effective because the number of people coming into the libraries nearly tripled in four years.\n\nNow, the question is asking which option, if true, would weaken this conclusion the most. So I need to find an option that shows the increase in library users isn't because of the new libraries but maybe because of something else.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) The libraries need new books and high maintenance costs, affecting other cultural facilities. Hmm, this is more about the cost and impact on other projects. It doesn't directly relate to the number of people using the libraries. So maybe not the strongest weakener.\n\nB) There are two universities, and many students applied for library cards. So if students from these universities are using the libraries, that could explain the increase. It's possible that the student population grew, leading to more library card applications. This could mean the increase isn't because of the new libraries but because of more students.\n\nC) Many citizens who got the first library card also got cards for the other two. So this is about overlap in card applications. If the same people applied for multiple libraries, the increase in total cards might not mean more unique users. But the government's conclusion is about the number of people coming in, not the number of cards. So if more people got multiple cards, the actual number of unique users might not have tripled as much as the card numbers suggest. This could weaken the conclusion because it's not the same as the number of people visiting.\n\nD) The new district's construction is rapid, and many migrants came in over four years. This would mean that the population in the new district increased, leading to more library card applications. So the increase could be due to more people moving there rather than the libraries attracting them. This would directly affect the conclusion because it's an external factor causing more people to get library cards, not necessarily more people coming to the libraries.\n\nNow, comparing B, C, and D. B talks about students, which is a specific group. C talks about overlapping card applications, which affects the count but not necessarily the unique users. D talks about an influx of migrants, which would increase the population and thus the number of people getting library cards, regardless of whether they use the libraries.\n\nSo D seems like the strongest because it's an external factor that could explain the increase in library card applications without necessarily increasing the number of people using the libraries. Similarly, B could be a factor if the student population increased, but it's more specific and might not account for all the increase.\n\nC is also a possibility because overlapping applications mean fewer unique users, but the government's conclusion is about the number of people coming in, not the number of cards. So if the same people are getting multiple cards, the number of people visiting the libraries might not have tripled as much as the card numbers suggest. But the question is about the effectiveness of the measure, which is about attracting people to the libraries. So if the increase in cards is due to more people getting multiple cards, it might not mean the libraries are attracting more people, just that more people are getting multiple cards.\n\nBut D is more about the population increasing, which would mean more people are getting library cards regardless of the libraries. So D is a stronger weakener because it's an external factor that could explain the increase without the libraries being effective.\n\nSo the answer should be D.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_6001",
    "context": "Confirmation bias refers to the fact that once a belief is established, people try to find examples that are consistent with it and ignore those that are not.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, based on the above definition, which of the following is the most likely the confirmation bias?",
    "options": [
      "A) Despite being told that all kimchi ingredients in the pickle jar are made at the same time, chef xiao huang still thinks that using the kimchi in the yellow jar to cook fish-flavored pork will be more delicious.",
      "B) The stock manager told a client named xiaoming that a stock would go up while telling other clients behind his back that the stock would go down. as a result, the stock went up, so xiaoming trusted the manager very much.",
      "C) Xiaogang was obsessed with the lottery because he thought it would be a windfall one day. though he never won the lottery, he idled around all day and even took out a loan to buy a lottery ticket.",
      "D) Xiaodong often worried when he heard some so called prophet conclude that he was going to be in a car accident. eventually, after a brush with another vehicle, xiaodong trusted the prophet more."
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nConfirmation bias refers to the fact that once a belief is established, people try to find examples that are consistent with it and ignore those that are not.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, based on the above definition, which of the following is the most likely the confirmation bias?\n# Options:\nA) Despite being told that all kimchi ingredients in the pickle jar are made at the same time, chef xiao huang still thinks that using the kimchi in the yellow jar to cook fish-flavored pork will be more delicious.\nB) The stock manager told a client named xiaoming that a stock would go up while telling other clients behind his back that the stock would go down. as a result, the stock went up, so xiaoming trusted the manager very much.\nC) Xiaogang was obsessed with the lottery because he thought it would be a windfall one day. though he never won the lottery, he idled around all day and even took out a loan to buy a lottery ticket.\nD) Xiaodong often worried when he heard some so called prophet conclude that he was going to be in a car accident. eventually, after a brush with another vehicle, xiaodong trusted the prophet more.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nThe reasoning is that in Option A, Chef Xiao Huang ignores the contradictory evidence that all kimchi ingredients are the same, sticking to his belief about the yellow jar, which is a clear example of confirmation bias.",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the given options is the most likely example of confirmation bias based on the provided definition. Let me start by understanding what confirmation bias is. The context says that confirmation bias is when people believe a certain thing and then only look for information that supports their belief, ignoring the contradictory evidence.\n\nSo, the question is asking which option demonstrates this bias. Let me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Chef Xiao Huang thinks using kimchi from the yellow jar will be more delicious, even though he was told all jars have the same ingredients. He ignores the information that they're the same. This seems like ignoring contradictory info, but I'm not sure if it's confirmation bias because he's sticking to his belief without considering the truth.\n\nOption B: The stock manager told a client (Xiaoming) the stock would go up, but told others it would go down. The stock went up, so Xiaoming trusted the manager. Wait, that's interesting. The manager's private info (telling others it would go down) didn't affect the outcome, but Xiaoming trusted based on the public info. So he didn't ignore the contrary info because he was told it would go up. Maybe this isn't a case of confirmation bias because he trusted the info given, not ignoring it.\n\nOption C: Xiaogang thought the lottery would be a windfall, so he kept playing even though he never won. He took a loan to buy a ticket. Hmm, this seems more about perseverance and maybe the belief in luck rather than selectively ignoring contradictory evidence. He didn't ignore the fact that he didn't win, he just kept playing. So probably not confirmation bias.\n\nOption D: Xiaodong trusted a prophet after he had an accident. So, when the prophet predicted an accident, and it happened, he trusted the prophet more. Wait, but the prophet predicted an accident, and it did happen, so he's aligning with the prediction. But confirmation bias is about ignoring contradictory evidence. Here, he trusted the prediction, which is in line with it happening, but I'm not sure if this is confirmation bias because he didn't ignore the contrary evidence; he actually used it to make a decision.\n\nWait, maybe I'm misunderstanding. Let me think again. Confirmation bias is about sticking to your belief and ignoring evidence that contradicts it. So in Option A, he thought the yellow jar's kimchi was better, ignoring that all jars are the same. So he's ignoring contradictory info (that they're the same) to stick to his belief. That fits confirmation bias.\n\nIn Option B, Xiaoming trusted the manager because the stock went up, even though the manager told others it would go down. So he trusted the info given, not ignoring the fact that others were told differently. So it's more about acting on the given info, not ignoring contradictory evidence.\n\nOption D: Xiaodong trusted the prophet because the accident happened, which aligns with the prediction. So he's using the confirmation, but he didn't ignore contradictory evidence because he didn't have any contradictory info; he just trusted the one that came true. So maybe this isn't a case of confirmation bias because he didn't ignore any contradictory info.\n\nSo, putting it all together, Option A seems to be the best example of confirmation bias because the person is ignoring evidence that contradicts their belief (that all jars are the same) to stick with their initial thought about the yellow jar.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_7839",
    "context": "If a child's weight to height ratio exceeds the level of 80% of children in the region, it is called an obese child. According to the survey results over the years, the number of obese children in Linjiang has been steadily increasing for 15 years.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following options is correct according to the above definition?",
    "options": [
      "A) The number of non-obese children in linjiang city has been increasing for 15 years",
      "B) In the past 15 years, the physical exercise of children in linjiang city has become increasingly insufficient",
      "C) The weight of every obese child in linjiang city exceeds the average weight of the city's children",
      "D) In the past 15 years, the number of children underweight in linjiang city has been declining"
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nIf a child's weight to height ratio exceeds the level of 80% of children in the region, it is called an obese child. According to the survey results over the years, the number of obese children in Linjiang has been steadily increasing for 15 years.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following options is correct according to the above definition?\n# Options:\nA) The number of non-obese children in linjiang city has been increasing for 15 years\nB) In the past 15 years, the physical exercise of children in linjiang city has become increasingly insufficient\nC) The weight of every obese child in linjiang city exceeds the average weight of the city's children\nD) In the past 15 years, the number of children underweight in linjiang city has been declining\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n1. The context states that obesity is defined by a weight-to-height ratio exceeding 80% of peers, and obesity rates have increased in Linjiang over 15 years.\n2. Option A is incorrect because the context doesn't provide data on the total number of children or non-obese children, so we can't conclude their trend.\n3. Option B is plausible as increased obesity could indicate reduced physical exercise, aligning with the context.\n4. Option C is incorrect because obesity doesn't mean every child is above average weight.\n5. Option D is unrelated as there's no data on underweight children.\n\nAnswer: B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D is correct based on the given context. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that a child's weight to height ratio exceeding 80% of children in the region is considered obese. It also mentions that the number of obese children in Linjiang has been increasing for 15 years.\n\nNow, the question is asking which option is correct according to this definition. Let's look at each option one by one.\n\nOption A: The number of non-obese children in Linjiang city has been increasing for 15 years. Hmm, wait. If the number of obese children is increasing, that doesn't necessarily mean the number of non-obese is decreasing. It depends on the total population. If the total number of children is also increasing, maybe the non-obese could be increasing, but the context doesn't mention anything about the total population. So I'm not sure about this one.\n\nOption B: In the past 15 years, the physical exercise of children in Linjiang city has become increasingly insufficient. The context says that the number of obese children has been increasing. Obesity is often linked to a lack of physical activity or insufficient exercise. So if more kids are becoming obese, it might imply that their physical exercise levels have decreased. This seems plausible, but I'm not certain if this is the only conclusion we can draw.\n\nOption C: The weight of every obese child in Linjiang city exceeds the average weight of the city's children. Wait, the definition says that a child is obese if their weight to height ratio is above 80% of children. It doesn't necessarily mean that every obese child is heavier than the average. Some might be average or even lighter but still have a higher ratio. So this statement might not be correct because it's too absolute.\n\nOption D: In the past 15 years, the number of children underweight in Linjiang city has been declining. The context doesn't mention anything about underweight children. It only talks about obesity increasing. So there's no information to support or refute this option. It's probably not correct.\n\nSo, considering all this, Option B seems like the most logical conclusion because an increase in obesity could be due to less physical exercise. Option A isn't necessarily true because we don't know the total number of children. Option C is incorrect because not every obese child is above average. Option D is unrelated to the given information.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer should be B.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_2961",
    "context": "According to the role of learning in the formation and development of mobility, human motivation can be divided into primitive motivation and acquisition motivation. Primitive motives are innate motives, which are based on people's instinctive needs; Acquisition motivation refers to all kinds of acquired motivation, that is, all kinds of motivation generated and developed through learning.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following options is most likely to belong to the original motivation?",
    "options": [
      "A) Respect the elderly and parents",
      "B) Beautiful and gentle girls are loved by boys",
      "C) Nothing venture,nothing gain",
      "D) Respect teachers and education, respect literature and martial arts"
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nAccording to the role of learning in the formation and development of mobility, human motivation can be divided into primitive motivation and acquisition motivation. Primitive motives are innate motives, which are based on people's instinctive needs; Acquisition motivation refers to all kinds of acquired motivation, that is, all kinds of motivation generated and developed through learning.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following options is most likely to belong to the original motivation?\n# Options:\nA) Respect the elderly and parents\nB) Beautiful and gentle girls are loved by boys\nC) Nothing venture,nothing gain\nD) Respect teachers and education, respect literature and martial arts\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option belongs to the original motivation based on the given context. Let me start by understanding the context first. The context talks about human motivation being divided into primitive and acquisition motivation. Primitive motives are innate, based on instinctive needs, while acquisition motivation comes from learning.\n\nSo, the question is asking which of the options is most likely to be original motivation. That means I'm looking for something that's instinctive or based on basic needs, not something learned through education or experience.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Respect the elderly and parents. This seems like something people might learn or develop through socialization and understanding family values. It's more of a cultural or acquired motivation.\n\nB) Beautiful and gentle girls are loved by boys. This sounds like a stereotype or a social norm that might be learned or influenced by society. It's not necessarily based on instinct.\n\nC) Nothing ventured, nothing gained. This is a saying that suggests taking risks leads to rewards. It's more of a personal belief or motivation, but I'm not sure if it's innate or learned. It could be learned from experiences or stories.\n\nD) Respect teachers and education, respect literature and martial arts. This definitely seems like something that's learned. People respect teachers and education because they've been taught or exposed to it. Similarly, respecting literature and martial arts comes from learning about them.\n\nWait, but the question is about original motivation, which is innate. So maybe I'm missing something. Let me think again. The options are about what kind of motivation they represent. Primitive motives are based on instinct, so maybe something like respecting parents is more instinctual, but I'm not sure. Alternatively, perhaps the correct answer is something else.\n\nWait, the options are about what kind of motivation they are. The question is asking which option is most likely to belong to the original motivation, which is innate. So, perhaps the correct answer is something that's more instinctual or tied to basic needs.\n\nLooking back, option C) \"Nothing ventured, nothing gained\" is a saying that might be learned from experiences or culture, not necessarily an instinct. Option D) is definitely learned. Option B) is a social norm, so learned. Option A) Respect for parents and elderly is more about family values, which can be learned but might have roots in instinctual family needs.\n\nWait, maybe I'm overcomplicating. The question is about which is most likely to be original motivation, so perhaps the answer is something that's more instinctual. But all the options seem to be learned except maybe C. Or perhaps the correct answer is C because it's a personal belief that might stem from instinctual risk-taking behavior.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct answer is D because respecting teachers and education is more about cultural transmission, which is learning, but respecting literature and martial arts could be more about personal interests, which might be learned but not necessarily original.\n\nWait, I'm getting confused. Let me try to break it down again. Primitive motives are based on instinctive needs, like survival, safety, love, etc. Acquisition motivation comes from learning, like education, socialization, etc.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Respect for elderly and parents: This is more about social roles and family, which are learned through socialization.\n\nB) Beautiful and gentle girls are loved by boys: This is a stereotype that's learned from societal norms.\n\nC) Nothing ventured, nothing gained: This is a personal belief that might be learned from experiences or cultural exposure.\n\nD) Respect teachers and education, respect literature and martial arts: Respect for teachers and education is learned, as is respect for literature and martial arts, which are often learned through exposure.\n\nWait, but maybe the correct answer is C because it's a general belief about risk and reward, which might be more instinctual. Or perhaps it's D because respecting literature and martial arts are more learned interests.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct answer is A because respecting parents is a basic family value, which might be more instinctual. But I'm not sure.\n\nWait, the question is about which is most likely to belong to original motivation. Original motivation is innate, so perhaps the answer is something that's more instinctual. But all the options seem to be learned except maybe C.\n\nWait, maybe the correct answer is C because it's a general principle that might be instinctual in terms of taking risks, but I'm not certain. Alternatively, perhaps the correct answer is D because respecting literature and martial arts are more learned, but respecting teachers and education is also learned.\n\nI'm getting stuck. Let me think differently. The question is asking which option is most likely to belong to the original motivation, which is based on instinctive needs. So, perhaps the answer is something that's more about basic needs, like survival or love.\n\nLooking at the options again:\n\nA) Respect the elderly and parents: This is about family, which is more about social roles, not necessarily basic needs.\n\nB) Beautiful and gentle girls are loved by boys: This is a social norm, not a basic need.\n\nC) Nothing ventured, nothing gained: This is a personal belief about risk and reward, which might be learned from experiences.\n\nD) Respect teachers and education, respect literature and martial arts: This is about cultural transmission and personal interests, which are learned.\n\nWait, maybe the correct answer is C because it's a general belief that might be instinctual in terms of taking risks, but I'm not sure. Alternatively, perhaps the correct answer is D because respecting literature and martial arts are more learned.\n\nWait, I'm overcomplicating. Let me try to think of which option is more about instinct. Respect for parents and elderly (A) is more about family, which is learned through socialization. Respect for teachers and education (D) is learned through formal education. Respect for literature and martial arts (D) is learned through exposure and learning.\n\nSo, perhaps the correct answer is C because it's a general belief about risk and reward, which might be more instinctual. Or maybe it's D because respecting literature and martial arts are more learned.\n\nWait, but the question is about original motivation, which is innate. So, perhaps the correct answer is C because it's a general principle that might be instinctual in terms of taking risks, but I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct answer is D because respecting literature and martial arts are more learned, but respecting teachers and education is also learned.\n\nWait, I'm getting stuck. Let me try to think of the options again.\n\nThe question is asking which option is most likely to belong to the original motivation, which is based on instinctive needs. So, perhaps the answer is something that's more about basic needs, like survival, safety, love, etc.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Respect the elderly and parents: This is about family, which is learned through socialization.\n\nB) Beautiful and gentle girls are loved by boys: This is a social norm, learned.\n\nC) Nothing ventured, nothing gained: This is a personal belief about risk and reward, which might be learned from experiences.\n\nD) Respect teachers and education, respect literature and martial arts: This is about cultural transmission and personal interests, which are learned.\n\nWait, maybe the correct answer is C because it's a general belief that might be instinctual in terms of taking risks, but I'm not sure. Alternatively, perhaps the correct answer is D because respecting literature and martial arts are more learned.\n\nWait, I'm going in circles. Let me try to think of it differently. The original motivation is based on instinctive needs, so perhaps the answer is something that's more about basic needs, like survival, safety, love, etc.\n\nLooking at the options, none of them seem to directly relate to basic needs. So, perhaps the correct answer is C because it's a general belief about risk and reward, which might be more instinctual.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the correct answer is D because respecting literature and martial arts are more learned, but respecting teachers and education is also learned.\n\nWait, I'm stuck. Let me try to think of it as the original motivation being more about basic needs, so perhaps the answer is C because it's a general principle that might be instinctual in terms of taking risks, but I'm not sure.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the correct answer is D because respecting literature and martial arts are more learned.\n\nWait, I'm going to make a decision. I think the correct answer is C because it's a general belief about risk and reward, which might be more instinctual, but I'm not entirely sure. Alternatively, maybe it's D because respecting literature and martial arts are more learned.\n\nWait, no, I think the correct answer is C because it's a general principle that might be instinctual in terms of taking risks, but I'm not certain. Alternatively, perhaps the correct answer is D because respecting literature and martial arts are more learned.\n\nWait, I'm going to go with C as the answer because it's a general belief about risk and reward, which might be more instinctual.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "C",
