  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_605",
    "context": "After the plant leaves are injured, green juice will emerge, and the fragrance of the leaves will become stronger. This green fragrance can lure the natural enemies of pests to eliminate pests. Researchers use transgenic methods to introduce the gene of green pepper aroma synthesis enzyme into Arabidopsis thaliana of Cruciferae. Once the larvae of Pieris rapae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis thaliana will be enhanced, The parasitoid lays its eggs on the larvae of Pieris rapae, which can eat all the larvae before they form pupae. Therefore, the researchers conclude that if this research result is applied to the formula of vegetables, the use of pesticides can be greatly reduced",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following premises must be added in order to make the researcher's conclusion valid",
    "options": [
      "A) At present, pesticides used in vegetable cultivation are mainly used to eliminate pests",
      "B) Fragrance of transgenic vegetable leaves will be enhanced after injury",
      "C)  green fragrance of plants is their weapon of self-defense",
      "D) At present, china's current law allows the planting of genetically modified vegetables"
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nAfter the plant leaves are injured, green juice will emerge, and the fragrance of the leaves will become stronger. This green fragrance can lure the natural enemies of pests to eliminate pests. Researchers use transgenic methods to introduce the gene of green pepper aroma synthesis enzyme into Arabidopsis thaliana of Cruciferae. Once the larvae of Pieris rapae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis thaliana will be enhanced, The parasitoid lays its eggs on the larvae of Pieris rapae, which can eat all the larvae before they form pupae. Therefore, the researchers conclude that if this research result is applied to the formula of vegetables, the use of pesticides can be greatly reduced\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following premises must be added in order to make the researcher's conclusion valid\n# Options:\nA) At present, pesticides used in vegetable cultivation are mainly used to eliminate pests\nB) Fragrance of transgenic vegetable leaves will be enhanced after injury\nC)  green fragrance of plants is their weapon of self-defense\nD) At present, china's current law allows the planting of genetically modified vegetables\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe reasoning is that the premise explaining why the green fragrance acts as a defense mechanism (option C) is necessary to validate the researcher's conclusion that increased fragrances will attract natural enemies, thereby reducing the need for pesticides. Without this premise, the link between the experiment's success and real-world application isn't established.",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which premise is needed to make the researcher's conclusion valid. The context says that when the leaves of Arabidopsis thaliana are injured, green juice comes out and the fragrance becomes stronger. This strong fragrance attracts natural enemies of pests, which then eliminate the pests. They used transgenic methods to introduce a gene for green pepper aroma synthesis enzyme into Arabidopsis. When the Pieris rapae larvae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis increases. The parasitoid lays eggs on these larvae, and before they become pupae, the larvae are eaten, reducing the pest population. The researchers conclude that applying this to vegetables can reduce the use of pesticides.\n\nThe question is asking which premise must be added to make this conclusion valid. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nI need to think about what the conclusion is. The conclusion is that using transgenic vegetables with increased green fragrance can reduce the need for pesticides because the natural enemies of the pests will be attracted to the fragrant leaves, thus controlling the pests without chemical pesticides.\n\nSo, to make this conclusion valid, we need a premise that connects the use of transgenic vegetables to the effectiveness of natural enemies. The researcher's experiment shows that in the lab, the transgenic Arabidopsis with stronger fragrance attracts natural enemies, leading to pest reduction. But to apply this to real vegetables, we need to assume that the same mechanism works in real-world conditions.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Current pesticides are mainly used to eliminate pests. This might be relevant because if the main use is to eliminate pests, then reducing their effectiveness would be beneficial. But does this directly support the conclusion? Maybe, but perhaps not the most critical link.\n\nB) Fragrance of transgenic leaves will be enhanced after injury. The context already mentions that when leaves are injured, green juice emerges and fragrance is stronger. The transgenic method enhances this. So this seems to be a given from the context, so maybe it's already established and doesn't need to be added as a premise.\n\nC) Green fragrance is a plant's self-defense weapon. This is an important point because if the plant's own defense mechanism is effective, then it's a reason why the natural enemies would be attracted. So this premise would support the conclusion because it explains why the increased fragrance would lead to more natural enemies.\n\nD) China's current law allows planting of GM vegetables. This is about regulations. While important for practical application, it doesn't directly link the effectiveness of the transgenic trait to the reduction of pesticides. It's more about whether it's allowed, not about the biological effect.\n\nSo, the key is that the plant's green fragrance acts as a defense, attracting natural enemies. Without this premise, the conclusion that increased fragrance would lead to more natural enemies might not hold. So option C is necessary to make the conclusion valid because it explains the mechanism behind why the increased fragrance would be effective.\n\nWait, but the context already talks about the green juice and stronger fragrance when injured, and the transgenic method enhances it. So maybe the premise that the plant's own defense mechanism (green fragrance) is effective is already implied. Hmm, but the question is about adding a premise to make the conclusion valid. The conclusion is about applying this to vegetables, so perhaps the premise that the plant's defense mechanism (green fragrance) is effective is necessary to link the experiment's success to the real-world application.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the premise that the transgenic method enhances the fragrance is already in the context, so B is already established. So the missing premise is that the green fragrance is a defense mechanism, making option C necessary.\n\nI think that's the case. So the correct premise is C.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_4678",
    "context": "The older a tree is, the more its rings are. The locust trees in Lao Zhang's yard are older than those in Lao Li's house. Therefore, the locust trees in Lao Zhang's house have more rings than those in Lao Li's house,",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following reasonings is most similar to the problem stem??",
    "options": [
      "A) The older a man is, the more mature he becomes. lao zhang is older than his son, so lao zhang is more mature than his son",
      "B) The higher the altitude, the thinner the air. because the altitude of xining is higher than xi'an, the air in xining is thinner than xi'an",
      "C) This year's marathon champion's performance is better than the year before last. zhang hua is this year's marathon champion, so his marathon performance this year is better than that of the year before last",
      "D) In the highly competitive market, the higher the product quality and the more advertising investment, the greater the product demand. company a invests more advertising expenses than company b. therefore, the demand for company a's products is greater than that of company b"
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nThe older a tree is, the more its rings are. The locust trees in Lao Zhang's yard are older than those in Lao Li's house. Therefore, the locust trees in Lao Zhang's house have more rings than those in Lao Li's house,\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following reasonings is most similar to the problem stem??\n# Options:\nA) The older a man is, the more mature he becomes. lao zhang is older than his son, so lao zhang is more mature than his son\nB) The higher the altitude, the thinner the air. because the altitude of xining is higher than xi'an, the air in xining is thinner than xi'an\nC) This year's marathon champion's performance is better than the year before last. zhang hua is this year's marathon champion, so his marathon performance this year is better than that of the year before last\nD) In the highly competitive market, the higher the product quality and the more advertising investment, the greater the product demand. company a invests more advertising expenses than company b. therefore, the demand for company a's products is greater than that of company b\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\nThe problem stem uses a cause-effect relationship where the age of trees (cause) leads to more rings (effect). Option B follows the same structure with altitude (cause) leading to thinner air (effect). Both are direct cause-effect relationships, making B the most similar.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D is most similar to the problem stem. Let me start by understanding the problem stem.\n\nThe problem says that the older a tree is, the more its rings are. So, older trees have more rings. Then it mentions that the locust trees in Lao Zhang's yard are older than those in Lao Li's house. From that, it concludes that the trees in Lao Zhang's house have more rings than those in Lao Li's house. \n\nSo, the reasoning here is that since Lao Zhang's trees are older, they have more rings, which is a direct cause-effect relationship. It's a straightforward example of transitivity: if A is older than B, and older trees have more rings, then A has more rings than B.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nOption A: Talks about a man's age and maturity. It says the older a man is, the more mature he becomes. Then it states that since Lao Zhang is older than his son, he's more mature. This is similar to the problem stem because it's using a cause-effect relationship (age leading to maturity) and then applying it to two individuals.\n\nOption B: Relates altitude to air thickness. It says higher altitude means thinner air. Then it compares Xining and Xi'an, stating Xining's air is thinner because it's higher. This is also a cause-effect relationship, similar to the tree example.\n\nOption C: Discusses a marathon champion's performance over the years. It says this year's champion is better than last year's. Then it attributes this to the fact that Lao Zhang is this year's champion. This seems more like correlation without a direct cause-effect relationship, as performance can be influenced by many factors, not just being a champion.\n\nOption D: Talks about market competition, product quality, advertising investment, and demand. It states that higher quality and more advertising lead to higher demand. Then it compares two companies, A and B, where A invests more in advertising, so A's demand is higher. This is a more complex cause-effect chain involving multiple factors.\n\nComparing all options, the problem stem is a simple transitive cause-effect relationship. Options A and B both use a cause-effect relationship to draw a conclusion. However, the problem stem specifically links age to rings, which is a direct cause. Option B links altitude to air thickness, which is also direct. \n\nBut the problem stem is about the age of trees and their ring count, which is a clear cause-effect relationship. Option A is about age and maturity, which is also a cause-effect, but it's about a person's age leading to their maturity, whereas the problem is about trees. Option B is about altitude leading to air thickness, which is a different scenario but structurally similar.\n\nWait, but the problem stem is about trees, so the reasoning is about growth over time. Option A is about aging a person, which is a different context but similar in structure. Option B is about a different natural phenomenon, altitude and air. Both are cause-effect, but the problem is about aging trees.\n\nI think the key is the structure of the reasoning, not the context. The problem stem uses a premise (older trees have more rings) and then applies it to two entities (Lao Zhang's and Lao Li's trees) to conclude a comparison. \n\nOption A does the same: older people (cause) are more mature (effect), applied to two individuals. Option B also does the same with altitude and air. So both A and B are similar in structure.\n\nBut the problem stem is about trees, so maybe the answer is A because it's about aging, which is a similar concept to the tree's age. However, B is about a different phenomenon but still a cause-effect.\n\nWait, the problem stem is about the number of rings in trees, which is a direct measure of age. Option A is about age leading to maturity, which is a different measure. Option B is about altitude leading to air thickness, which is a direct measure like rings.\n\nSo, the problem stem is about a measurable attribute (number of rings) increasing with age. Option B is about a measurable attribute (air thickness) decreasing with altitude. Both are direct cause-effect relationships.\n\nBut the problem stem is about comparing two entities based on their age, leading to their ring count. Option B is about comparing two places based on altitude, leading to air thickness. Both are similar in structure.\n\nWait, but the problem stem is about the number of rings, which is a direct result of age. Option B is about air thickness, which is a direct result of altitude. So both are direct cause-effect relationships, but the problem stem is about trees, so maybe the answer is A because it's about aging, which is a similar concept to the tree's age.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the answer is B because it's a different scenario but the same structure. Hmm.\n\nWait, the problem stem is about the number of rings in trees, which is a direct measure of age. Option A is about age leading to maturity, which is a different measure. Option B is about altitude leading to air thickness, which is a direct measure. So both are direct cause-effect, but the problem stem is about a measurable attribute increasing with age, while B is about a measurable attribute decreasing with altitude.\n\nSo, the structure is similar, but the contexts are different. The problem stem is about trees, so maybe the answer is A because it's about aging, which is a similar concept to the tree's age.\n\nWait, but the problem stem is about the number of rings, which is a direct result of age. Option A is about age leading to maturity, which is a different attribute. So maybe the answer is B because it's about a direct cause-effect relationship, similar to the tree's rings.\n\nI'm a bit confused. Let me think again.\n\nThe problem stem: older trees (cause) have more rings (effect). Therefore, since Lao Zhang's trees are older than Lao Li's, Lao Zhang's have more rings.\n\nOption A: older people (cause) are more mature (effect). Therefore, since Lao Zhang is older, he's more mature.\n\nOption B: higher altitude (cause) leads to thinner air (effect). Therefore, since Xining is higher, its air is thinner.\n\nOption C: this year's champion (cause) is better (effect). Therefore, since Lao Zhang is this year's champion, his performance is better.\n\nOption D: higher quality and more advertising (causes) lead to higher demand (effect). Therefore, since A has more advertising, A's demand is higher.\n\nSo, the problem stem is a transitive application of a cause-effect relationship. All options except C use this structure. C is more about correlation without a direct cause-effect.\n\nSo, the correct answer should be either A, B, or D, as they all use cause-effect reasoning. But the problem stem is about trees, so maybe the answer is A because it's about aging, which is a similar concept to the tree's age.\n\nAlternatively, B is about altitude, which is a different context but similar structure.\n\nWait, the problem stem is about the number of rings, which is a direct measure of age. Option A is about age leading to maturity, which is a different measure. So, maybe the answer is B because it's about a direct cause-effect relationship, similar to the tree's rings.\n\nI think the answer is B because it's a direct cause-effect relationship, just like the problem stem. The problem stem is about trees, but the reasoning structure is the same as B, which is about altitude leading to air thickness.\n\nWait, but the problem stem is about comparing two trees based on their age, leading to rings. Option B is about comparing two cities based on altitude, leading to air thickness. Both are cause-effect, so both are similar. But the problem stem is about a measurable attribute (rings) increasing with age, while B is about a measurable attribute (air thickness) decreasing with altitude.\n\nSo, the structure is similar, but the direction is opposite. The problem stem is cause (age) leading to effect (more rings), while B is cause (altitude) leading to effect (thinner air). Both are direct cause-effect, so both are similar.\n\nBut the problem stem is about trees, so maybe the answer is A because it's about aging, which is a similar concept to the tree's age.\n\nWait, but the problem stem is about the number of rings, which is a direct result of age. Option A is about age leading to maturity, which is a different attribute. So, maybe the answer is B because it's about a direct cause-effect relationship, similar to the tree's rings.\n\nI think I'm overcomplicating this. The correct answer is B because it's a direct cause-effect relationship, just like the problem stem. The problem stem is about older trees having more rings, so it's a cause-effect. Option B is about higher altitude leading to thinner air, which is also cause-effect. So, both are similar, but the problem stem is about trees, so maybe the answer is A because it's about aging, which is a similar concept.\n\nWait, no, the problem stem is about the number of rings, which is a direct measure of age. Option A is about age leading to maturity, which is a different measure. So, maybe the answer is B because it's about a direct cause-effect relationship, similar to the tree's rings.\n\nI think I'm stuck. Let me try to summarize:\n\nProblem stem: older trees (cause) \u2192 more rings (effect). Therefore, since Lao Zhang's trees are older, they have more rings.\n\nOptions:\n\nA) older people (cause) \u2192 more mature (effect). Therefore, Lao Zhang is more mature.\n\nB) higher altitude (cause) \u2192 thinner air (effect). Therefore, Xining's air is thinner.\n\nC) this year's champion (cause) \u2192 better performance (effect). Therefore, Lao Zhang's performance is better.\n\nD) higher quality and more advertising (causes) \u2192 higher demand (effect). Therefore, A's demand is higher.\n\nSo, all except C use cause-effect. The problem stem is about a direct cause (age) leading to a direct effect (more rings). Option B is about a direct cause (altitude) leading to a direct effect (thinner air). So, both are similar in structure.\n\nBut the problem stem is about trees, so maybe the answer is A because it's about aging, which is a similar concept to the tree's age.\n\nAlternatively, B is about a different phenomenon but similar structure.\n\nI think the answer is B because it's a direct cause-effect relationship, just like the problem stem. The problem stem is about older trees having more rings, so it's a cause-effect. Option B is about higher altitude leading to thinner air, which is also cause-effect. So, both are similar, but the problem stem is about trees, so maybe the answer is A because it's about aging, which is a similar concept.\n\nWait, but the problem stem is about the number of rings, which is a direct measure of age. Option A is about age leading to maturity, which is a different measure. So, maybe the answer is B because it's about a direct cause-effect relationship, similar to the tree's rings.\n\nI think I'm going in circles. Let me make a decision.\n\nThe correct answer is B because it's a direct cause-effect relationship, similar to the problem stem. The problem stem is about older trees having more rings, so it's a cause-effect. Option B is about higher altitude leading to thinner air, which is also cause-effect. Both are similar in structure, so B is the answer.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
