d jixiang's passenger cars and are not overloaded.\n\nWait, that seems confusing. Maybe it's a formatting issue. Let me look again.\n\nWait, the user wrote:\n\n\"A) A, b, c and ruyi's passenger cars and jixiang's passenger cars are not overloaded.\"\n\nWait, that seems like a mistake because A is a statement about cars, but the option is combining A, b, c, which might be a typo. Maybe it's supposed to be \"A, B, C\", but I'm not sure. Alternatively, perhaps the option is saying that A, B, and C are all correct, but that doesn't make sense because only one is wrong.\n\nWait, perhaps the options are structured differently. Let me check again.\n\nThe options are:\n\nA) A, b, c and ruyi's and jixiang's passenger cars and are not overloaded.\n\nWait, that seems like a mix of letters and statements. Maybe it's a formatting error. Alternatively, perhaps the options are structured as:\n\nA) All statements A, B, C are correct, and Ruyi's and Jixiang's cars are not overloaded.\n\nBut that doesn't make sense because if A is correct, then all cars are overloaded, so they can't be not overloaded. So maybe the options are structured differently.\n\nWait, perhaps the options are:\n\nA) A, B, C are correct, and Ruyi's and Jixiang's cars are not overloaded.\n\nBut that can't be because if A is correct, then all cars are overloaded, so they can't be not overloaded. So perhaps the options are structured as:\n\nA) A is wrong, B and C are correct, and Ruyi's and Jixiang's cars are not overloaded.\n\nWait, but I'm getting confused. Let me try a different approach.\n\nLet me consider each case where one of A, B, or C is wrong, and see what the implications are for the options.\n\nCase 1: A is wrong. So not all cars are overloaded. Then B and C must be correct.\n\nIf B is correct, then all buses are okay (no overload). If C is correct, then both Ruyi and Jixiang's buses are overloaded. But wait, if B is correct, then all buses are okay, which contradicts C because C says Ruyi and Jixiang's