l options again.\n\nWait, the options are:\n\nA) A, b, c and ruyi's passenger cars and jixiang's passenger cars are not overloaded.\n\nB) Both ruyi's passenger cars and jixiang's passenger cars were overloaded.\n\nC) The passenger cars of ruyi company are overloaded, but the passenger cars of jixiang company are not overloaded.\n\nD) There is an overloading problem on the passenger cars of jixiang, but there is no overloading problem on the passenger cars of ruyi.\n\nWait, now I see that the options are about cars, not buses. So perhaps the question is about cars, and the buses are part of the statements but the options are about cars. That might be a mistake, but I'll proceed.\n\nWait, the original statements are:\n\nA: All the passenger cars are overloaded.\n\nB: There is no overload problem on all the city's passenger buses.\n\nC: Both Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses are overloaded.\n\nSo the options are about cars, but the statements are about both cars and buses. So perhaps the options are only about cars, and the buses are part of the statements but not the options. That might be a confusion.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps the options are about both cars and buses, but the way they're written is unclear.\n\nWait, perhaps the options are structured as:\n\nA) A, B, C are correct, and Ruyi's and Jixiang's cars are not overloaded.\n\nBut that can't be because if A is correct, all cars are overloaded.\n\nOption B: Both Ruyi's and Jixiang's passenger cars were overloaded.\n\nWait, but if B is wrong, then A and C are correct, meaning Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses are overloaded, but their cars are all overloaded as well. So in that case, both Ruyi's and Jixiang's cars are overloaded.\n\nIf C is wrong, then A and B are correct, meaning Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses are not overloaded, but their cars are all overloaded.\n\nSo in both cases where only one statement is wrong, either B or C is wrong, and in both cases, Ruyi's and Jixiang's cars are overloaded.\n\nWait, let me clarify:\n\nIf B is wrong (so B is wrong, A and C are correct):\n\n- All cars are overloaded (A correct).\n\n- Both Ruyi and Jixiang's buses are overloaded (C correct).\n\nSo Ruyi's and Jixiang's cars are all overloaded.\n\nIf C is wrong (so C is wrong, A and B are correct):\n\n- All cars are overloaded (A correct).\n\n- All buses are okay (B correct).\n\nSo Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses are not overloaded, but their cars are all overloaded.\n\nSo in both scenarios, Ruyi's and Jixiang's cars are overloaded. Therefore, the correct option must be that both Ruyi's and Jixiang's cars are overloaded.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption B: Both Ruyi's and Jixiang's passenger cars were overloaded.\n\nBut wait, the options are about cars, not buses. So in both cases, Ruyi's and Jixiang's cars are overloaded, so Option B would be correct.\n\nWait, but let me check the options again:\n\nA) A, B, C are correct, and Ruyi's and Jixiang's cars are not overloaded. \u2192 Impossible because if A is correct, all cars are overloaded.\n\nB) Both Ruyi's and Jixiang's passenger cars were overloaded. \u2192 This seems correct because in both cases, their cars are overloaded.\n\nC) Ruyi's cars are overloaded, Jixiang's not. \u2192 This would only be true if C is wrong, but in that case, their buses are not overloaded, but their cars are. However, in the other case where B is wrong, both their cars are overloaded. So this option is not necessarily true in all cases.\n\nD) Jixiang's cars have a problem, Ruyi's don't. \u2192 This would only be true if C is wrong, but in that case, their cars are all overloaded. So this is not necessarily true.\n\nWait, but in both scenarios, Ruyi's and Jixiang's cars are overloaded. So Option B is correct in both cases.\n\nWait, but let me think again. If B is wrong, then A and C are correct, so Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses are overloaded, but their cars are all overloaded. So their cars are definitely overloaded.\n\nIf C is wrong, then A and B are correct, so Ruyi's and Jixiang's buses are not overloaded, but their cars are all overloaded. So again, their cars are overloaded.\n\nTherefore, regardless of whether B or C is wrong, Ruyi's and Jixiang's cars are overloaded. So the correct option is B.\n\nWait, but looking back at the options, Option B says both Ruyi's and Jixiang's passenger cars were overloaded. So that's correct.\n\nBut wait, the options are:\n\nA) A, B, C and ruyi's and jixiang's cars are not overloaded.\n\nB) Both ruyi's and jixiang's passenger cars were overloaded.\n\nC) Ruyi's cars are overloaded, Jixiang's not.\n\nD) Jixiang's cars have a problem, Ruyi's don't.\n\nSo the correct answer is B.\n\nWait, but I'm a bit confused because the options are a bit unclear. Let me make sure.\n\nYes, in both cases where only one statement is wrong, Ruyi's and Jixiang's cars are overloaded. So the correct option is B.\n",
