happen. So, this might mean that the environment is not as disrupted as experts say. It actually contradicts the expert's point because it shows that the environment isn't as bad as it should be, as rats are still being controlled when the water rises. So this option doesn't support the expert's view.\n\nOption D: Large-scale drainage and planting of poplars turned the tidal wetland into a woodland where rats can live. If they drained water and changed the environment, maybe the natural water levels that controlled rats are gone. So, now rats have more space to live without the natural predators or controls. This supports the expert's point because it shows that human actions have altered the environment, leading to rat proliferation.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the expert is saying that Dongting Lake's environment is destroyed, leading to rats. Option C suggests that even when the water rises, rats can drown, which would help control their numbers. But last year, without the drought, the water didn't rise, so rats weren't drowned. This means the environment isn't as disrupted as experts say, so it doesn't support the expert's point.\n",
