  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_2110",
    "context": "No matter how rich and wonderful the original works are, the adaptation of literary classics into TV dramas is a re-creation. TV dramas should not only present the complex and heavy themes of the original as much as possible, but also integrate the stories into the fate of characters through adaptation. The historical changes of the ancient land, the life reflection of national survival, the glory and decline of Confucian culture, the suppression and publicity of human desire. The motif of literary classics must be reinterpreted through adaptation. Moreover, no matter how to change, the advance of the plot, the shaping of the characters and the development of the plot should conform to the inherent behavior logic of the characters.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, regarding the principle of adapting literary classics into TV series, which of the following articles is not covered?",
    "options": [
      "A) To highlight the themes of literary classics",
      "B) There must be a clearer thread",
      "C) Should have reasonable internal logic",
      "D) Use necessary exaggeration"
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nNo matter how rich and wonderful the original works are, the adaptation of literary classics into TV dramas is a re-creation. TV dramas should not only present the complex and heavy themes of the original as much as possible, but also integrate the stories into the fate of characters through adaptation. The historical changes of the ancient land, the life reflection of national survival, the glory and decline of Confucian culture, the suppression and publicity of human desire. The motif of literary classics must be reinterpreted through adaptation. Moreover, no matter how to change, the advance of the plot, the shaping of the characters and the development of the plot should conform to the inherent behavior logic of the characters.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, regarding the principle of adapting literary classics into TV series, which of the following articles is not covered?\n# Options:\nA) To highlight the themes of literary classics\nB) There must be a clearer thread\nC) Should have reasonable internal logic\nD) Use necessary exaggeration\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I need to figure out which article is not covered based on the given context about adapting literary classics into TV series. Let me start by reading the context carefully.\n\nThe context talks about how TV dramas are a re-creation of literary classics. It mentions that TV dramas should present complex and heavy themes as much as possible, integrate stories into character fates, and adapt historical changes, life reflections, Confucian culture, and human desires. It also says that literary motifs must be reinterpreted through adaptation. Additionally, the plot, character shaping, and plot development should follow the inherent behavior logic of the characters.\n\nNow, looking at the question, it asks which article is not covered. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nOption A: \"To highlight the themes of literary classics.\" The context does mention that TV dramas should present complex themes, so this seems covered.\n\nOption B: \"There must be a clearer thread.\" The context talks about the plot and character development following inherent logic, which implies a clear thread. So B is covered.\n\nOption C: \"Should have reasonable internal logic.\" The context emphasizes that the plot and character behavior should conform to their inherent logic, meaning the internal logic must be reasonable. So C is covered.\n\nOption D: \"Use necessary exaggeration.\" The context doesn't mention anything about exaggeration. It talks about reinterpreting motifs and maintaining the original's essence but doesn't specify using exaggeration. So D is not covered.\n\nTherefore, the answer should be D.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_3812",
    "context": "In country A, more complicated civil trials often exceed the comprehension of the jury. As a result, the jury's decision on this is often wrong. Therefore, some people suggest that the more complicated civil trials should be decided by the judge rather than the jury, which will improve the service quality of the judicial department.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following options is correct according to the above definition?",
    "options": [
      "A) Even when it comes to uncomplicated civil trials, the jury's decisions are often wrong",
      "B) When judges decide on complex civil trials, they have a better understanding of the complexity of those trials than the jury",
      "C) The complexity of most civil trials exceeds the comprehension of the jury",
      "D) Some countries with the same legal system other than country a have also made similar proposals for a long time, and have records of their implementation"
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nIn country A, more complicated civil trials often exceed the comprehension of the jury. As a result, the jury's decision on this is often wrong. Therefore, some people suggest that the more complicated civil trials should be decided by the judge rather than the jury, which will improve the service quality of the judicial department.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following options is correct according to the above definition?\n# Options:\nA) Even when it comes to uncomplicated civil trials, the jury's decisions are often wrong\nB) When judges decide on complex civil trials, they have a better understanding of the complexity of those trials than the jury\nC) The complexity of most civil trials exceeds the comprehension of the jury\nD) Some countries with the same legal system other than country a have also made similar proposals for a long time, and have records of their implementation\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n1. The context states that in country A, juries often make wrong decisions on complicated civil trials.\n2. This leads to the suggestion that judges should handle such cases instead.\n3. Option B aligns with this reasoning, as judges would have a better understanding of complex cases.\n4. Options A, C, and D are not supported by the given information.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option is correct based on the given context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it properly.\n\nThe context says that in country A, when civil trials are more complicated, the jury's decisions often end up being wrong. Because of this, some people think that complex civil trials should be decided by judges instead of the jury to improve the quality of judicial service.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options A to D is correct based on this information. Let me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"Even when it comes to uncomplicated civil trials, the jury's decisions are often wrong.\" Hmm, the context doesn't mention anything about uncomplicated trials. It only talks about complicated ones. So I don't think this is necessarily true. The argument is about complexity leading to wrong decisions, not about simplicity.\n\nOption B: \"When judges decide on complex civil trials, they have a better understanding of the complexity of those trials than the jury.\" This seems plausible. The context suggests that the jury's decisions on complicated cases are wrong, implying that judges, who presumably have more expertise, would make better decisions. So this could be correct.\n\nOption C: \"The complexity of most civil trials exceeds the comprehension of the jury.\" The context says that more complicated trials exceed the jury's comprehension, but it doesn't say \"most.\" It could be that some are complicated, but not necessarily most. So this might not be accurate.\n\nOption D: \"Some countries with the same legal system other than country A have also made similar proposals for a long time, and have records of their implementation.\" The context doesn't mention anything about other countries or their experiences with similar proposals. It only talks about country A. So I don't think this is supported by the given information.\n\nPutting it all together, the correct answer should be option B because it logically follows from the context that judges have a better understanding of complex trials, leading to correct decisions, whereas the jury's decisions on complicated cases are wrong.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_13306",
    "context": "Lance: If experience teaches us nothing else, it teaches us that every general rule has at least one exception. Frank: What you conclude is itself a general rule. If we assume that it is true, then there is at least one general rule that has no exceptions. Ttherefore, you must withdraw your conclusion.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, frank's argument is an attempt to counter Lance's conclusion by",
    "options": [
      "A) Demonstrating that lance assumes the very thing he sets out to prove",
      "B) Establishing that experience teaches us the opposite of what lance concludes",
      "C) Showing that lance's conclusion involves him in a contradiction",
      "D) Showing that it has no implications for any real cases"
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nLance: If experience teaches us nothing else, it teaches us that every general rule has at least one exception. Frank: What you conclude is itself a general rule. If we assume that it is true, then there is at least one general rule that has no exceptions. Ttherefore, you must withdraw your conclusion.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, frank's argument is an attempt to counter Lance's conclusion by\n# Options:\nA) Demonstrating that lance assumes the very thing he sets out to prove\nB) Establishing that experience teaches us the opposite of what lance concludes\nC) Showing that lance's conclusion involves him in a contradiction\nD) Showing that it has no implications for any real cases\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nFrank's argument is attempting to show that Lance's conclusion leads to a contradiction. By assuming Lance's conclusion is true, Frank demonstrates that it implies the existence of a general rule without exceptions, which directly contradicts Lance's original statement. Therefore, Frank is effectively showing that Lance's conclusion is self-contradictory.\n\nThe correct option is: C) Showing that lance's conclusion involves him in a contradiction.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me start by reading the context and the question carefully.\n\nThe context is a conversation between Lance and Frank. Lance says, \"If experience teaches us nothing else, it teaches us that every general rule has at least one exception.\" Frank responds, \"What you conclude is itself a general rule. If we assume that it is true, then there is at least one general rule that has no exceptions. Therefore, you must withdraw your conclusion.\"\n\nThe question is asking what Frank's argument is trying to do in response to Lance's conclusion. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nFirst, I need to break down what each person is saying.\n\nLance's statement: \"If experience teaches us nothing else, it teaches us that every general rule has at least one exception.\" So, Lance is saying that experience will show that all general rules have exceptions. In logical terms, this is like saying if we accept that experience is reliable, then every rule has an exception.\n\nFrank's response: He starts by pointing out that Lance's conclusion is itself a general rule. Then he says that if we assume Lance's conclusion is true, it leads to the idea that there's at least one general rule without exceptions. Therefore, Frank concludes that Lance must withdraw his conclusion.\n\nSo, Frank is saying that Lance's conclusion creates a contradiction because if every rule has an exception, then there must be a rule without an exception, which contradicts Lance's conclusion. Therefore, Lance's conclusion can't be true.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Demonstrating that Lance assumes the very thing he sets out to prove. Hmm, that doesn't seem right. Lance is trying to prove that every rule has an exception. Frank isn't saying Lance is assuming what he's trying to prove; instead, Frank is challenging the conclusion by creating a contradiction.\n\nB) Establishing that experience teaches us the opposite of what Lance concludes. Frank isn't directly saying experience teaches the opposite; he's arguing against Lance's conclusion by showing it leads to a contradiction, not necessarily that experience teaches the opposite.\n\nC) Showing that Lance's conclusion involves him in a contradiction. This seems to fit. Frank is pointing out that if Lance's conclusion is true, it leads to a contradiction because it would imply there's a rule without an exception, which contradicts the conclusion that every rule has an exception. So, this is a contradiction.\n\nD) Showing that it has no implications for any real cases. I don't think Frank is arguing about implications for real cases. He's more about the logical structure of the argument leading to a contradiction.\n\nSo, the correct answer should be C because Frank is demonstrating a contradiction in Lance's argument.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_9299",
    "context": "Rare earths are a general term for a class of metals with less reserves, which are widely used in cutting-edge technology and military industries. Most of the world's rare earths are produced in China. Since 1998, China has begun to reduce the amount of rare earth mining, control the rare earth export quota, and strengthen the concentration of the rare earth industry. In this regard, some countries accuse China of monopolizing resources, which is extremely destructive to other countries in the world, and demand that China relax its control over the rare earth industry.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following statements, if true, is most appropriate to refute these countries' accusations of China?",
    "options": [
      "A) Rare earths are non-renewable and important strategic resources.",
      "B) At present, on a per capita basis, china is already a country with relatively scarce rare earth resources.",
      "C) Since the 1980s, rare earths have been exploited indiscriminately in some parts of china, causing serious waste of resources and great damage to the environment.",
      "D) In 2009, china's rare earth reserves accounted for 36% of the national reserves, the united states accounted for 13%, russia accounted for 19%, australia accounted for 5.4%; china's production accounted for 97% of the world's production, and the other three countries were all zero."
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nRare earths are a general term for a class of metals with less reserves, which are widely used in cutting-edge technology and military industries. Most of the world's rare earths are produced in China. Since 1998, China has begun to reduce the amount of rare earth mining, control the rare earth export quota, and strengthen the concentration of the rare earth industry. In this regard, some countries accuse China of monopolizing resources, which is extremely destructive to other countries in the world, and demand that China relax its control over the rare earth industry.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following statements, if true, is most appropriate to refute these countries' accusations of China?\n# Options:\nA) Rare earths are non-renewable and important strategic resources.\nB) At present, on a per capita basis, china is already a country with relatively scarce rare earth resources.\nC) Since the 1980s, rare earths have been exploited indiscriminately in some parts of china, causing serious waste of resources and great damage to the environment.\nD) In 2009, china's rare earth reserves accounted for 36% of the national reserves, the united states accounted for 13%, russia accounted for 19%, australia accounted for 5.4%; china's production accounted for 97% of the world's production, and the other three countries were all zero.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nThe reasoning is that option D provides specific data showing China's significant control over rare earth resources, both in production and reserves, which directly refutes the accusation of monopolization. \n\nThe correct option is: D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option refutes the countries' accusations about China monopolizing rare earth resources. Let me start by understanding the context given.\n\nThe context says that rare earths are a class of metals with limited reserves, used in advanced tech and military. Most of the world's rare earths come from China. Since 1998, China has been reducing its rare earth mining, controlling exports, and concentrating the industry. Some countries are accusing China of monopolizing resources, which is harmful to other countries. These countries want China to relax its control.\n\nSo the question is asking which statement, if true, would best refute these accusations. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLet me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"Rare earths are non-renewable and important strategic resources.\" This is a general statement about rare earths. It's true that they're non-renewable and strategic, but how does that refute the accusation? The accusation is about China controlling the industry, not necessarily about the scarcity of resources. So while A is true, it might not directly address the issue of concentration or control.\n\nOption B: \"At present, on a per capita basis, China is already a country with relatively scarce rare earth resources.\" This talks about China's per capita resources being scarce. If China is relatively scarce, then it's not that they have a monopoly because they have limited resources. But wait, the context says China has the most reserves, so if they're per capita scarce, it might mean they have to import a lot, which could imply they control a lot of the supply. I'm not sure if this directly refutes the accusation. It might actually support the idea that China is a major player because they have limited per capita resources but control a large portion of the reserves.\n\nOption C: \"Since the 1800s, rare earths have been exploited indiscriminately in some parts of China, causing serious waste of resources and great damage to the environment.\" This talks about past environmental and resource misuse. While this is a problem, it doesn't directly address the accusation of monopolizing resources. It's more about environmental issues than control over resources.\n\nOption D: \"In 2009, China's rare earth reserves accounted for 36% of the national reserves, the United States accounted for 13%, Russia 19%, Australia 5.4%; China's production accounted for 97% of the world's production, and the other three countries were all zero.\" This is interesting. It shows that China has a large portion of the reserves (36%) and almost all the production (97%). The other countries have zero production but their reserves are lower. This would mean that China is controlling a significant portion of the rare earth resources, both in terms of production and reserves. If China is controlling 97% of production and has 36% of the reserves, it strengthens the idea that China is a major player and not a user. Therefore, this would refute the accusation that China is monopolizing resources because it shows China is both producing and controlling a large share, making it a dominant force.\n\nWait, but the accusation is that China is monopolizing, so if China is actually controlling a large portion, it would support the idea that they are not being accused wrongly. So D directly shows that China is a major player, which would refute the idea that they're being accused of monopolizing because they actually are.\n\nSo, putting it together, the best option is D because it provides concrete data showing China's control over rare earth resources, which would refute the accusation that they're being monopolized.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_5842",
    "context": "Intellectual property is the right of people to enjoy the wealth created by intellectual labor in the fields of science, technology, culture, education and art.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following is not an intellectual property?",
    "options": [
      "A) Trademark right.",
      "B) Right of invention.",
      "C) Portrait rights.",
      "D) Copyright."
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nIntellectual property is the right of people to enjoy the wealth created by intellectual labor in the fields of science, technology, culture, education and art.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following is not an intellectual property?\n# Options:\nA) Trademark right.\nB) Right of invention.\nC) Portrait rights.\nD) Copyright.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I need to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D is not an intellectual property based on the given definition. Let me start by understanding the context provided.\n\nThe context says that intellectual property is the right of people to enjoy the wealth created by intellectual labor in fields like science, technology, culture, education, and art. So, intellectual property is about protecting the fruits of human creativity and effort in these areas.\n\nNow, looking at the question: it asks which of the options is NOT an intellectual property. The options are Trademark right, Right of invention, Portrait rights, and Copyright.\n\nLet me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Trademark right. I know that trademarks are used to protect brands, logos, and other distinctive signs that identify goods or services. They are a form of intellectual property because they protect creative designs and logos, which are intangible assets. So, A is an intellectual property.\n\nOption B: Right of invention. This refers to the legal protection granted for new and useful\u53d1\u660es. In many countries, inventors are granted patents, which are a type of intellectual property. So, B is also an intellectual property.\n\nOption C: Portrait rights. This is a bit trickier. Portrait rights typically refer to the legal rights a person has to control the use of their own photograph or image. This is more about personal rights and privacy rather than intellectual creation. It's not about creating something new but about the rights to one's own image. So, portrait rights might not fall under intellectual property.\n\nOption D: Copyright. Copyrights protect original works of authorship, like books, music, paintings, and software. They are definitely a form of intellectual property because they protect creative works. So, D is an intellectual property.\n\nPutting it all together, the odd one out is C) Portrait rights because they are more about personal rights and privacy rather than protecting intellectual creation.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_6322",
    "context": "From politics, economy to daily life, from globalization to regional revival, from practical technology to discourse expression, many problems and crises are closely related to the factor of space, which is manifested  in the from of earthquake and tsunami disaster, the border armed conflict , the collapse of the real estate industry, road congestion, and increasingly fierce space and other various forms.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following options is meant to illustrate?",
    "options": [
      "A) Economic globalization has brought many spatial crises.",
      "B) The crisis of natural and man-made space has become increasingly prominent.",
      "C) Nowadays, space is becoming more and more important.",
      "D) The increase in population brings many spatial problems."
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nFrom politics, economy to daily life, from globalization to regional revival, from practical technology to discourse expression, many problems and crises are closely related to the factor of space, which is manifested  in the from of earthquake and tsunami disaster, the border armed conflict , the collapse of the real estate industry, road congestion, and increasingly fierce space and other various forms.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following options is meant to illustrate?\n# Options:\nA) Economic globalization has brought many spatial crises.\nB) The crisis of natural and man-made space has become increasingly prominent.\nC) Nowadays, space is becoming more and more important.\nD) The increase in population brings many spatial problems.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe correct option is: B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option correctly illustrates the information given in the context. Let me start by reading the context carefully. The context talks about various problems and crises related to space, mentioning earthquakes and tsunamis, border armed conflicts, real estate industry collapse, road congestion, and other forms of space-related issues. It seems like the context is highlighting that space is a significant factor leading to these crises.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nA) Economic globalization has brought many spatial crises.\nHmm, the context doesn't mention economic globalization directly causing spatial crises. It talks about natural and man-made space issues, so maybe this isn't the best fit.\n\nB) The crisis of natural and man-made space has become increasingly prominent.\nThis seems to align well with the context. The context mentions natural disasters like earthquakes and tsunamis, as well as man-made issues like border conflicts and real estate problems. So this option is directly related to what's described.\n\nC) Nowadays, space is becoming more and more important.\nWhile the context does talk about space being a factor, this option is more about space becoming important rather than illustrating the crises it causes. So maybe not the best choice.\n\nD) The increase in population brings many spatial problems.\nThe context does mention road congestion and real estate issues, which could be related to population growth. However, it doesn't specifically focus on population as the main cause but rather on the various crises space causes. So this might not be the best answer.\n\nPutting it all together, option B directly addresses the crises related to natural and man-made space, which is exactly what the context is about. The other options either don't fit as well or focus on different aspects that aren't as central to the context provided.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_605",
    "context": "After the plant leaves are injured, green juice will emerge, and the fragrance of the leaves will become stronger. This green fragrance can lure the natural enemies of pests to eliminate pests. Researchers use transgenic methods to introduce the gene of green pepper aroma synthesis enzyme into Arabidopsis thaliana of Cruciferae. Once the larvae of Pieris rapae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis thaliana will be enhanced, The parasitoid lays its eggs on the larvae of Pieris rapae, which can eat all the larvae before they form pupae. Therefore, the researchers conclude that if this research result is applied to the formula of vegetables, the use of pesticides can be greatly reduced",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following premises must be added in order to make the researcher's conclusion valid",
    "options": [
      "A) At present, pesticides used in vegetable cultivation are mainly used to eliminate pests",
      "B) Fragrance of transgenic vegetable leaves will be enhanced after injury",
      "C)  green fragrance of plants is their weapon of self-defense",
      "D) At present, china's current law allows the planting of genetically modified vegetables"
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nAfter the plant leaves are injured, green juice will emerge, and the fragrance of the leaves will become stronger. This green fragrance can lure the natural enemies of pests to eliminate pests. Researchers use transgenic methods to introduce the gene of green pepper aroma synthesis enzyme into Arabidopsis thaliana of Cruciferae. Once the larvae of Pieris rapae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis thaliana will be enhanced, The parasitoid lays its eggs on the larvae of Pieris rapae, which can eat all the larvae before they form pupae. Therefore, the researchers conclude that if this research result is applied to the formula of vegetables, the use of pesticides can be greatly reduced\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following premises must be added in order to make the researcher's conclusion valid\n# Options:\nA) At present, pesticides used in vegetable cultivation are mainly used to eliminate pests\nB) Fragrance of transgenic vegetable leaves will be enhanced after injury\nC)  green fragrance of plants is their weapon of self-defense\nD) At present, china's current law allows the planting of genetically modified vegetables\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe reasoning is that premise B directly supports the effectiveness of the increased fragrance in attracting natural enemies, which is crucial for the conclusion that using transgenic vegetables can reduce pesticide use.",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which premise is needed to make the researcher's conclusion valid. The context says that when the leaves of Arabidopsis thaliana are injured, green juice comes out and the fragrance becomes stronger. This strong fragrance attracts natural enemies of pests, which then eliminate the pests. They used transgenic methods to introduce a gene for green pepper aroma synthesis into Arabidopsis. When the Pieris rapae larvae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis increases. The parasitoid lays eggs on these larvae, and before they become pupae, the larvae are eaten, reducing the pest population. The researchers conclude that applying this to vegetables can reduce the use of pesticides.\n\nThe question is asking which premise must be added to make this conclusion valid. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nI need to think about what the conclusion is. The conclusion is that using transgenic vegetables with increased green fragrance can reduce pesticide use because the natural enemies will take care of the pests. So, the reasoning is that the increased fragrance attracts predators, which then eat the pests before they can harm the crops.\n\nNow, looking at the premises in the context, they mention that when the leaves are injured, the fragrance increases, which lures natural enemies. They also mention that the transgenic method introduces the gene, so the plant produces more of the aroma enzyme, leading to more fragrance. The parasitoid eats the larvae, which are the pests, so the natural enemies are effectively used.\n\nBut to make the conclusion valid, we need to ensure that the increased fragrance actually works as intended. So, the researcher's conclusion is that this method will reduce pesticide use. Therefore, the premise must link the increased fragrance to the effectiveness of the natural enemies.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Current pesticides are mainly used for eliminating pests. This might be relevant because if most pesticides are used for this purpose, then reducing their use would be significant. But does this directly support the conclusion? Maybe not directly, because the conclusion is about the effectiveness of natural enemies, not just the type of pesticides used.\n\nB) Fragrance of transgenic leaves will be enhanced after injury. This seems relevant because the context mentions that when leaves are injured, the fragrance increases. So, if the transgenic leaves are modified to produce more fragrance when injured, that supports the idea that the increased fragrance will attract natural enemies. So this premise would be necessary to validate the conclusion.\n\nC) Green fragrance is a plant's self-defense weapon. This is more of a general statement about plants. While it's true that plants use defense mechanisms, the context specifically talks about the transgenic modification leading to increased fragrance. So, this might not directly support the conclusion unless it's assumed that the plant's natural defense mechanisms are being enhanced, which is what the premise B is about.\n\nD) China's current law allows planting of genetically modified vegetables. This is about legal aspects. While it's important for the practicality of the research, it doesn't directly affect the validity of the conclusion regarding the reduction of pesticides. The conclusion is about the effectiveness of the method, not whether it's legally feasible.\n\nSo, the key point is that the increased fragrance must be effective in attracting natural enemies. The context already mentions that when leaves are injured, the fragrance increases, which lures natural enemies. However, the premise B specifically states that the transgenic leaves' fragrance will be enhanced after injury, which directly supports the effectiveness needed for the conclusion.\n\nTherefore, the correct premise to add is B.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_10375",
    "context": "Scientists, just like Earth, Venus also has a hot lava core inside. With the autobiography and rotation of Venus, huge heat is released. The earth releases internal heat through volcanic eruptions caused by plate tectonics. It is puzzling that there is no volcanic eruption like plate tectonics in Venus.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following options, if true, gives the best explanation for the confusion of the scientist?",
    "options": [
      "A) The autobiography of venus is slow and its outer shell is much thinner than that of earth, which makes it easy to release internal heat to the outside.",
      "B) Greenhouse effect, caused by carbon dioxide in venus's atmosphere, makes its surface temperature as high as 485 degrees celsius.",
      "C) Due to the action of high temperature and high pressure, the rocks on the surface of venus are harder than those on the earth's surface.",
      "D) The movement of the lava in the core of venus once had more violent temperature fluctuations than the movement of the earth's rocks."
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nScientists, just like Earth, Venus also has a hot lava core inside. With the autobiography and rotation of Venus, huge heat is released. The earth releases internal heat through volcanic eruptions caused by plate tectonics. It is puzzling that there is no volcanic eruption like plate tectonics in Venus.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following options, if true, gives the best explanation for the confusion of the scientist?\n# Options:\nA) The autobiography of venus is slow and its outer shell is much thinner than that of earth, which makes it easy to release internal heat to the outside.\nB) Greenhouse effect, caused by carbon dioxide in venus's atmosphere, makes its surface temperature as high as 485 degrees celsius.\nC) Due to the action of high temperature and high pressure, the rocks on the surface of venus are harder than those on the earth's surface.\nD) The movement of the lava in the core of venus once had more violent temperature fluctuations than the movement of the earth's rocks.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe reasoning is that Venus's rocks are harder due to high temperature and pressure, preventing volcanic eruptions despite a hot core.",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this question about Venus and why it doesn't have volcanic eruptions like Earth does, despite having a hot core. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that Venus, like Earth, has a hot lava core. But unlike Earth, Venus doesn't have volcanic eruptions caused by plate tectonics. The question is asking which option best explains this confusion.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A says Venus's core's autobiography is slow, and its outer shell is thinner, making it easier to release heat. Wait, \"autobiography\" doesn't make sense here. Maybe it's a typo for \"autobiography\" or perhaps it's supposed to be \"activity.\" If the core's activity is slow, that might mean less heat is released, but the question is about why there's no volcanic activity. Also, a thinner shell might allow heat to escape, but that's more about surface temperature than volcanic eruptions.\n\nOption B talks about the greenhouse effect causing high surface temperatures. Venus's atmosphere is thick and dense with CO2, which traps heat, making it extremely hot. But the question is about volcanic eruptions, not surface temperature. So while this explains Venus's high temperature, it doesn't directly address why there's no lava.\n\nOption C mentions high temperature and pressure making Venus's rocks harder. Harder rocks might mean less movement and thus less volcanic activity. That makes sense because if the rocks are too hard, they don't melt or flow, so no eruptions. This could be a good explanation.\n\nOption D says Venus's lava core had more violent temperature fluctuations. If the core's temperature fluctuates more, it might cause more frequent or intense volcanic eruptions. But the question states that there are no eruptions, so this seems contradictory. Unless it's saying that the fluctuations were so violent that they caused melting, but that doesn't explain the lack of eruptions.\n\nPutting it together, the best explanation is probably Option C. The hard rocks prevent lava from erupting, so even though there's a hot core, it doesn't result in volcanic activity. This aligns with how some planets, like Mars, have a hot core but no active volcanoes because their crust is too hard.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_250",
    "context": "People generally think that the thinking of scientists is based on strict logical reasoning, rather than Image thinking. However, research shows that Nobel Prize winners use image thinking methods more than ordinary scientists, because image thinking helps make a major scientific breakthroughs.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, the above conclusion is based on which of the following assumptions?",
    "options": [
      "A) Structured, step-by-step reasoning is essential for general scientific research",
      "B) Nobel prize winners have the ability to make creative thinking through image thinking",
      "C) Nobel prize winners have made major scientific breakthroughs",
      "D) Nobel prize winners are smarter and work harder than ordinary scientists"
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nPeople generally think that the thinking of scientists is based on strict logical reasoning, rather than Image thinking. However, research shows that Nobel Prize winners use image thinking methods more than ordinary scientists, because image thinking helps make a major scientific breakthroughs.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, the above conclusion is based on which of the following assumptions?\n# Options:\nA) Structured, step-by-step reasoning is essential for general scientific research\nB) Nobel prize winners have the ability to make creative thinking through image thinking\nC) Nobel prize winners have made major scientific breakthroughs\nD) Nobel prize winners are smarter and work harder than ordinary scientists\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe conclusion is based on the assumption that image thinking enables creative and significant scientific advancements, as stated in the context. This aligns with option B, which directly addresses the role of image thinking in creativity and breakthroughs.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this question based on the given context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context says that people generally believe scientists think strictly using logic, but research shows that Nobel Prize winners use image thinking more than ordinary scientists. It also mentions that image thinking helps make major scientific breakthroughs.\n\nNow, the question is asking which assumption the conclusion is based on. The conclusion is that the above information supports the idea that image thinking is used by Nobel Prize winners more than ordinary scientists because of its help in major breakthroughs.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Structured, step-by-step reasoning is essential for general scientific research.\nHmm, this is about structured reasoning being important. But the context talks about image thinking, not structured reasoning. So maybe A isn't the right assumption.\n\nB) Nobel prize winners have the ability to make creative thinking through image thinking.\nThis seems relevant because the context says image thinking helps make breakthroughs, implying that it's a creative method. So B might be the assumption.\n\nC) Nobel prize winners have made major scientific breakthroughs.\nThe context mentions that image thinking helps make breakthroughs, not that they have made them. So C might be a conclusion rather than an assumption.\n\nD) Nobel prize winners are smarter and work harder than ordinary scientists.\nThis is about their intelligence and effort. The context doesn't provide information about their intelligence or work ethic, only about their thinking style. So D might not be the assumption.\n\nPutting it together, the conclusion is based on the assumption that image thinking leads to breakthroughs, which is option B.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
