  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_14499",
    "context": "Paleontologist: About 2. 8 million years ago, many species that lived near the ocean floor suffered substantial population declines. These declines coincided with the onset of an ice age. The notion that cold killed those bottom-dwelling creatures outright is misguided, however; temperatures near the ocean floor would have changed very little. <b> Nevertheless, the cold probably did cause the population declines, though indirectly. </b> Many bottom-dwellers depended for food on plankton, small organisms that lived close to the surface and sank to the bottom when they died. <b> Most probably, the plankton suffered a severe population decline as a result of sharply lower temperatures at the surface, depriving many bottom-dwellers of food. </b>",
    "question": "Based on the above information, in the paleontologist's reasoning, the two portions in boldface play which of the following roles?",
    "options": [
      "A) The first is a generalization put forward by the paleontologist; the second presents certain exceptional cases in which that generalization does not hold.",
      "B) The first is an explanation challenged by the paleontologist; the second is an explanation proposed by the paleontologist.",
      "C) The first is a judgment advanced in support of a conclusion reached by the paleontologist; the second is that conclusion.",
      "D) The first introduces the hypothesis proposed by the paleontologist; the second is a judgment offered in spelling out that hypothesis."
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nPaleontologist: About 2. 8 million years ago, many species that lived near the ocean floor suffered substantial population declines. These declines coincided with the onset of an ice age. The notion that cold killed those bottom-dwelling creatures outright is misguided, however; temperatures near the ocean floor would have changed very little. <b> Nevertheless, the cold probably did cause the population declines, though indirectly. </b> Many bottom-dwellers depended for food on plankton, small organisms that lived close to the surface and sank to the bottom when they died. <b> Most probably, the plankton suffered a severe population decline as a result of sharply lower temperatures at the surface, depriving many bottom-dwellers of food. </b>\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, in the paleontologist's reasoning, the two portions in boldface play which of the following roles?\n# Options:\nA) The first is a generalization put forward by the paleontologist; the second presents certain exceptional cases in which that generalization does not hold.\nB) The first is an explanation challenged by the paleontologist; the second is an explanation proposed by the paleontologist.\nC) The first is a judgment advanced in support of a conclusion reached by the paleontologist; the second is that conclusion.\nD) The first introduces the hypothesis proposed by the paleontologist; the second is a judgment offered in spelling out that hypothesis.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B) The first is an explanation challenged by the paleontologist; the second is an explanation proposed by the paleontologist.\n\nThe reasoning is that the first bolded part challenges the initial idea that cold caused population declines directly, while the second part provides the proposed explanation of indirect effects through plankton.",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this question about the paleontologist's reasoning. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it.\n\nThe paleontologist is talking about species near the ocean floor suffering population declines around 2.8 million years ago, and these declines happened when an ice age started. The first bolded part says that it's a mistake to think that cold killed those bottom creatures outright because the ocean floor temperatures wouldn't change much. So, the paleontologist is saying that the cold probably caused the declines, but indirectly.\n\nThen, the second bolded part explains that many bottom-dwellers depend on plankton for food. When plankton declined because of colder surface temperatures, this deprived the bottom creatures of food. So, the paleontologist is suggesting that the surface temperatures dropping led to the plankton decline, which then caused the population declines for the bottom-dwellers.\n\nNow, the question is about the roles of these two bolded portions in the paleontologist's reasoning. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) The first is a generalization; the second are exceptions. But the first part is more of an argument against the idea that cold killed the creatures outright, not a generalization. So maybe not A.\n\nB) The first is an explanation challenged; the second is proposed. Wait, the first part is challenging the initial idea that cold caused the population decline directly. So the paleontologist is introducing a different explanation, which is the indirect cause through plankton. So the first is challenging, and the second is the proposed explanation. That seems to fit B.\n\nC) The first is a judgment in support; the second is the conclusion. But the first part is more of a reason to think that the cause is indirect, not a judgment in support. So probably not C.\n\nD) The first introduces the hypothesis; the second is a judgment. The first part is introducing the idea that cold caused indirect effects, which is the hypothesis. The second part is explaining how that hypothesis works, which is a judgment. So D could also be a possibility.\n\nWait, but in the context, the paleontologist is saying that the cold probably caused the declines, but indirectly. So the first bolded part is the paleontologist's main point, which is that the cause is indirect. The second part is providing the reasoning behind that point, explaining why the population declined.\n\nSo, the first is the hypothesis or the main conclusion, and the second is the explanation supporting it. So D says the first introduces the hypothesis, and the second is a judgment. That makes sense because the second part is explaining the hypothesis, which is a judgment about how the hypothesis applies.\n\nAlternatively, B says the first is an explanation challenged, and the second is proposed. But the first is challenging the initial idea, and the second is the proposed explanation. So B could also fit because the first is challenging the initial idea (that cold killed them outright), and the second is the proposed explanation (plankton decline leading to food shortage).\n\nHmm, I'm a bit confused. Let me think again.\n\nThe first bolded part is the paleontologist's argument against the idea that cold killed the creatures outright. So it's challenging that initial idea. The second part is the paleontologist's proposed explanation, which is that the surface temperatures dropped, affecting plankton, which then caused the population decline in the bottom creatures.\n\nSo the first is challenging the initial idea, and the second is the proposed explanation. So B seems correct because the first is challenging, and the second is the explanation.\n\nAlternatively, D says the first introduces the hypothesis, and the second is a judgment. The hypothesis is that the population decline was indirect because of plankton. The second part is explaining how that hypothesis works, which is a judgment about the reasoning.\n\nWait, but the second part is more of an explanation, not a judgment. A judgment would be a statement of belief or assertion. The second part is explaining why the population declined, which is an explanation, not a judgment.\n\nSo maybe D is not correct because the second part is not a judgment but an explanation.\n\nSo perhaps B is correct because the first is challenging the initial idea, and the second is the proposed explanation.\n\nWait, but the question is about the roles of the two bolded parts. The first is challenging the initial idea, and the second is the proposed explanation. So the first is an explanation that's being challenged, and the second is the explanation being proposed.\n\nSo the correct answer would be B: The first is an explanation challenged by the paleontologist; the second is an explanation proposed by the paleontologist.\n\nWait, but the first part is not exactly an explanation. It's more of a counter-argument or a reason to think the cause is indirect. So maybe it's challenging the initial idea, which is that cold caused the population decline directly.\n\nSo the first part is challenging the initial idea, and the second part is the proposed explanation. So B seems correct.\n\nAlternatively, D says the first introduces the hypothesis, and the second is a judgment. But the second part is not a judgment; it's an explanation. So D is incorrect.\n\nSo I think B is the correct answer.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_14397",
    "context": "Principle: Employees of telemarketing agencies should never do anything that predisposes people to dislike the agencies' clients. Application: If an employee of a telemarketing agency has been told by a person the employee has called that he or she does not want to buy the product of a client of the agency, the employee should not try to talk that person into doing so.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which one of the following, if true, justifies the given application of the principle above?",
    "options": [
      "A) Some people that an employee of a telemarketing agency calls to ask them to buy the product of a client of the agency will refuse to do so even though they are not predisposed to dislike the client.",
      "B) Any employee of a telemarketing agency who tries to get someone to buy the product of a client of the agency after the person has said that he or she does not want to will engender animosity toward the client.",
      "C) Some employees of telemarketing agencies are unlikely to be certain about whether trying to talk someone into buying the product of a client of the agency after the person has said that he or she does not want to will likely engender animosity toward the client.",
      "D) People who are already predisposed to dislike the client of a telemarketing agency are more likely to refuse to buy the product of that client than are people who are predisposed to like the client."
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nPrinciple: Employees of telemarketing agencies should never do anything that predisposes people to dislike the agencies' clients. Application: If an employee of a telemarketing agency has been told by a person the employee has called that he or she does not want to buy the product of a client of the agency, the employee should not try to talk that person into doing so.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which one of the following, if true, justifies the given application of the principle above?\n# Options:\nA) Some people that an employee of a telemarketing agency calls to ask them to buy the product of a client of the agency will refuse to do so even though they are not predisposed to dislike the client.\nB) Any employee of a telemarketing agency who tries to get someone to buy the product of a client of the agency after the person has said that he or she does not want to will engender animosity toward the client.\nC) Some employees of telemarketing agencies are unlikely to be certain about whether trying to talk someone into buying the product of a client of the agency after the person has said that he or she does not want to will likely engender animosity toward the client.\nD) People who are already predisposed to dislike the client of a telemarketing agency are more likely to refuse to buy the product of that client than are people who are predisposed to like the client.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe correct option is: B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read through the context and the question again to make sure I understand everything.\n\nThe principle given is about telemarketing agency employees. It says they shouldn't do anything that makes people dislike their clients. The application example is if an employee has been told by someone they called that they don't want to buy the product of a client, the employee shouldn't try to convince them.\n\nNow, the question is asking which option justifies applying this principle. The options are A, B, C, D. I need to figure out which one logically supports the application of the principle.\n\nLet me break down the principle first. The main idea is to avoid actions that predispose people to dislike the agency's clients. So, if an employee knows someone doesn't want to buy the product, they shouldn't try to persuade them. The application is clear: don't try to talk someone into buying if they've already said no.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nOption A: Some people will refuse even if they don't dislike the client. Hmm, this seems to suggest that refusing is normal, but does it justify the application? The principle is about not predisposing, not necessarily about refusing. So maybe not the best fit.\n\nOption B: Trying to get someone to buy after they said no will cause animosity. Wait, that's exactly what the principle is about. If the employee tries to talk them into buying, it could make the person dislike the client more. So this seems to directly support the application.\n\nOption C: Some employees are unsure if their actions will cause animosity. This is more about the uncertainty of employees rather than justifying the application. It's more about the potential consequences but not the main point.\n\nOption D: People predisposed to dislike are more likely to refuse than those who like. This seems to support the idea that if someone dislikes the client, they'll refuse, but it doesn't directly address the action of trying to convince someone who already said no. It's more about the behavior of people with different predispositions.\n\nSo, putting it together, the application is about not trying to talk someone into buying after they've said no, which is exactly what Option B states. It directly justifies the application by showing that such actions would lead to animosity.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_14688",
    "context": "Utrania was formerly a major petroleum exporter, but in recent decades economic stagnation and restrictive regulations inhibited investment in new oil fields. In consequence, Utranian oil exports dropped steadily as old fields became depleted. Utrania' s currently improving economic situation, together with less-restrictive regulations, will undoubtedly result in the rapid development of new fields. However, it would be premature to conclude that the rapid development of new fields will result in higher oil exports, because __.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following most logically completes the argument?",
    "options": [
      "A) New technology is available to recover oil from old oil fields formerly regarded as depleted",
      "B) The improvement in the economic situation in utrania is expected to result in a dramatic increase in the proportion of utranians who own automobiles",
      "C) The price of oil is expected to remain relatively stable over the next several years",
      "D) Most of the investment in new oil fields in utrania is expected to come from foreign sources"
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nUtrania was formerly a major petroleum exporter, but in recent decades economic stagnation and restrictive regulations inhibited investment in new oil fields. In consequence, Utranian oil exports dropped steadily as old fields became depleted. Utrania' s currently improving economic situation, together with less-restrictive regulations, will undoubtedly result in the rapid development of new fields. However, it would be premature to conclude that the rapid development of new fields will result in higher oil exports, because __.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following most logically completes the argument?\n# Options:\nA) New technology is available to recover oil from old oil fields formerly regarded as depleted\nB) The improvement in the economic situation in utrania is expected to result in a dramatic increase in the proportion of utranians who own automobiles\nC) The price of oil is expected to remain relatively stable over the next several years\nD) Most of the investment in new oil fields in utrania is expected to come from foreign sources\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nThe argument highlights that while Utrania's economic improvement and less restrictive regulations are expected to lead to rapid development of new oil fields, it's premature to assume this will result in higher oil exports. The most logical completion is that new technology might allow recovery from old, depleted fields, which could mean higher exports without new investments. \n\nAnswer: A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option best completes the argument given in the context. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context talks about Utrania's oil exports. They used to be a major exporter, but now they're struggling. The reasons are economic stagnation and restrictive regulations, which have stopped new investments in oil fields. Because of this, their exports have been dropping as the old fields run out. But now, Utrania's economy is improving, and the regulations are becoming less restrictive, so they expect new fields to develop rapidly. However, the argument points out that it's premature to conclude that these new fields will lead to higher oil exports. So, I need to find why that might not be the case.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) New technology can recover oil from old depleted fields. This seems relevant because if old fields can be used again, that could increase exports without needing new fields. So, this could explain why even with new fields, exports might not rise as expected.\n\nB) Economic improvement leads to more people owning cars. While more cars might increase demand, it's not directly related to oil exports. The argument is about oil production, not consumption. So, this might not be the best fit.\n\nC) Oil price stability. If oil prices are stable, higher production could lead to higher exports. But the argument is about why we can't conclude that new fields will increase exports, so this might not be the main issue. Plus, the context doesn't mention anything about price stability.\n\nD) Foreign investment in new fields. If most investment comes from outside, it might affect the economy, but again, the argument is about the relationship between new fields and exports. This might not directly address the issue.\n\nSo, the key point is that even with new fields, if old ones can still be used, exports might not go up. That makes option A the most logical choice because it introduces another factor that could influence oil exports without necessarily relying on new fields.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_4219",
    "context": "Language plays an important role in human communication, and if a language is completely effective, then every combination of basic languages can express words of independent significance. But if there is a problem with the function of human auditory systems in receiving sound signals, not every combination of basic languages can become independent words.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, based on the above materials, which of the following options is correct?",
    "options": [
      "A) If the human auditory system receives sound signals that function properly, then every combination of the basic language of a language can become a word of independent significance",
      "B) The effectiveness of language leads to the practicality of human communication",
      "C) If the basic language, each combination can become a word of independent significance, then the language is fully valid",
      "D) If the human auditory system receives sound and has problems with signal function, then language cannot be fully effective"
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nLanguage plays an important role in human communication, and if a language is completely effective, then every combination of basic languages can express words of independent significance. But if there is a problem with the function of human auditory systems in receiving sound signals, not every combination of basic languages can become independent words.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, based on the above materials, which of the following options is correct?\n# Options:\nA) If the human auditory system receives sound signals that function properly, then every combination of the basic language of a language can become a word of independent significance\nB) The effectiveness of language leads to the practicality of human communication\nC) If the basic language, each combination can become a word of independent significance, then the language is fully valid\nD) If the human auditory system receives sound and has problems with signal function, then language cannot be fully effective\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nThe argument highlights that while Utrania's economic improvement and less restrictive regulations are expected to lead to rapid development of new oil fields, it's premature to assume this will result in higher oil exports. The most logical completion is that new technology might allow recovery from old, depleted fields, which could mean higher exports without new investments. \n\nAnswer: A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option best completes the argument given in the context. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context talks about Utrania's oil exports. They used to be a major exporter, but now they're struggling. The reasons are economic stagnation and restrictive regulations, which have stopped new investments in oil fields. Because of this, their exports have been dropping as the old fields run out. But now, Utrania's economy is improving, and the regulations are becoming less restrictive, so they expect new fields to develop rapidly. However, the argument points out that it's premature to conclude that these new fields will lead to higher oil exports. So, I need to find why that might not be the case.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) New technology can recover oil from old depleted fields. This seems relevant because if old fields can be used again, that could increase exports without needing new fields. So, this could explain why even with new fields, exports might not rise as expected.\n\nB) Economic improvement leads to more people owning cars. While more cars might increase demand, it's not directly related to oil exports. The argument is about oil production, not consumption. So, this might not be the best fit.\n\nC) Oil price stability. If oil prices are stable, higher production could lead to higher exports. But the argument is about why we can't conclude that new fields will increase exports, so this might not be the main issue. Plus, the context doesn't mention anything about price stability.\n\nD) Foreign investment in new fields. If most investment comes from outside, it might affect the economy, but again, the argument is about the relationship between new fields and exports. This might not directly address the issue.\n\nSo, the key point is that even with new fields, if old ones can still be used, exports might not go up. That makes option A the most logical choice because it introduces another factor that could influence oil exports without necessarily relying on new fields.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_11562",
    "context": "Artists have different ways of producing contours and hatching, and analysis of these stylistic features can help to distinguish works by a famous artist both from forgeries and from works genuinely by other artists. Indeed, this analysis has shown that many of the drawings formerly attributed to Michelangelo are actually by the artist Giulio Clovio, Michelangelo' s contemporary.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, if the statements above are true, then which one of the following must also be true?",
    "options": [
      "A) The contour and hatching styles used to identify the drawings of clovio cited can be shown to be features of all clovio's works.",
      "B) There is an analyzable difference between clovio's contour and hatching styles and those of michelangelo.",
      "C) Many of the drawings formerly attributed to michelangelo are actually forgeries.",
      "D) Contours and hatching are the main features that distinguish the drawing styles of different artists."
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nArtists have different ways of producing contours and hatching, and analysis of these stylistic features can help to distinguish works by a famous artist both from forgeries and from works genuinely by other artists. Indeed, this analysis has shown that many of the drawings formerly attributed to Michelangelo are actually by the artist Giulio Clovio, Michelangelo' s contemporary.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, if the statements above are true, then which one of the following must also be true?\n# Options:\nA) The contour and hatching styles used to identify the drawings of clovio cited can be shown to be features of all clovio's works.\nB) There is an analyzable difference between clovio's contour and hatching styles and those of michelangelo.\nC) Many of the drawings formerly attributed to michelangelo are actually forgeries.\nD) Contours and hatching are the main features that distinguish the drawing styles of different artists.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: A\n\nThe argument highlights that while Utrania's economic improvement and less restrictive regulations are expected to lead to rapid development of new oil fields, it's premature to assume this will result in higher oil exports. The most logical completion is that new technology might allow recovery from old, depleted fields, which could mean higher exports without new investments. \n\nAnswer: A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option best completes the argument given in the context. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context talks about Utrania's oil exports. They used to be a major exporter, but now they're struggling. The reasons are economic stagnation and restrictive regulations, which have stopped new investments in oil fields. Because of this, their exports have been dropping as the old fields run out. But now, Utrania's economy is improving, and the regulations are becoming less restrictive, so they expect new fields to develop rapidly. However, the argument points out that it's premature to conclude that these new fields will lead to higher oil exports. So, I need to find why that might not be the case.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) New technology can recover oil from old depleted fields. This seems relevant because if old fields can be used again, that could increase exports without needing new fields. So, this could explain why even with new fields, exports might not rise as expected.\n\nB) Economic improvement leads to more people owning cars. While more cars might increase demand, it's not directly related to oil exports. The argument is about oil production, not consumption. So, this might not be the best fit.\n\nC) Oil price stability. If oil prices are stable, higher production could lead to higher exports. But the argument is about why we can't conclude that new fields will increase exports, so this might not be the main issue. Plus, the context doesn't mention anything about price stability.\n\nD) Foreign investment in new fields. If most investment comes from outside, it might affect the economy, but again, the argument is about the relationship between new fields and exports. This might not directly address the issue.\n\nSo, the key point is that even with new fields, if old ones can still be used, exports might not go up. That makes option A the most logical choice because it introduces another factor that could influence oil exports without necessarily relying on new fields.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_4704",
    "context": "Pollution-free vegetables refer to vegetables that use biological control measures such as microbial pesticides and agricultural antibiotics and chemical pesticides with high efficiency, low toxicity and low residue to comprehensively control vegetable diseases and insect pests, so that the pesticide residue is lower than the allowable edible standard stipulated by the state.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following statements is correct?",
    "options": [
      "A) Microbial pesticides must be used in pollution-free vegetables",
      "B) Agricultural antibiotics are allowed",
      "C) Chemical pesticides can be used as long as they are efficient and low toxicity",
      "D) Pollution-free vegetables can be eaten safely and will never hurt your body"
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nPollution-free vegetables refer to vegetables that use biological control measures such as microbial pesticides and agricultural antibiotics and chemical pesticides with high efficiency, low toxicity and low residue to comprehensively control vegetable diseases and insect pests, so that the pesticide residue is lower than the allowable edible standard stipulated by the state.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following statements is correct?\n# Options:\nA) Microbial pesticides must be used in pollution-free vegetables\nB) Agricultural antibiotics are allowed\nC) Chemical pesticides can be used as long as they are efficient and low toxicity\nD) Pollution-free vegetables can be eaten safely and will never hurt your body\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe reasoning is that the context specifies that chemical pesticides can be used as long as they are efficient and low toxicity, which matches option C. Options A and B are not necessarily correct because the context doesn't require the use of microbial or agricultural antibiotics exclusively. Option D is too absolute, as it's based on residues being below the edible standard, not that they won't hurt the body.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which statement is correct based on the given definition of pollution-free vegetables. Let me start by reading the context again carefully.\n\nThe context says that pollution-free vegetables use biological control measures like microbial pesticides and agricultural antibiotics, along with chemical pesticides that are efficient and low toxicity. The goal is to control diseases and pests so that the residue is below the edible standard.\n\nNow, looking at the question, I need to determine which of the four options (A, B, C, D) is correct based on this definition.\n\nLet's break down each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"Microbial pesticides must be used in pollution-free vegetables.\" Hmm, the context mentions that biological control measures include microbial pesticides, but it doesn't say that they must be used. It just says they are used along with others. So maybe they don't have to be used exclusively or exclusively, but they are part of the methods. So I'm not sure if \"must be\" is correct.\n\nOption B: \"Agricultural antibiotics are allowed.\" The context talks about microbial and agricultural antibiotics as part of the biological control measures. It doesn't explicitly say whether they are allowed or required. It just mentions they are used. So I'm not certain if \"are allowed\" is the correct interpretation.\n\nOption C: \"Chemical pesticides can be used as long as they are efficient and low toxicity.\" The context does mention that chemical pesticides are used with high efficiency and low toxicity. So this seems to align with the definition. It says \"can be used,\" which implies that as long as they meet those criteria, they are acceptable. So this might be correct.\n\nOption D: \"Pollution-free vegetables can be eaten safely and will never hurt your body.\" The context says that the residues are below the edible standard, so they are safe to eat. However, it doesn't explicitly say that they won't hurt your body. It just ensures they meet safety standards. So maybe this is too strong a statement.\n\nWait, let me think again. The context says that the residues are lower than the allowable standard, so they are safe. So D says they won't hurt your body, which is a bit vague. It's possible that they are safe, but the statement is a bit absolute. Maybe it's correct, but I'm not sure.\n\nNow, going back to the options, the question is asking which statement is correct based on the definition. The definition includes both microbial and chemical pesticides, emphasizing efficiency and low toxicity. So for option C, it's about chemical pesticides being used under those conditions, which matches the context. Option A and B are about whether certain types must be used or are allowed, but the context doesn't specify that they must be used or that others are not allowed. It just says they are used along with others. So A and B might not be correct because they imply exclusivity or allowance that isn't stated.\n\nOption D is about safety, which is partially correct because residues are below the standard, but the statement is a bit too absolute. It says \"will never hurt your body,\" which might be overstating it. So maybe D is not the best answer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is likely option C because it directly aligns with the context's emphasis on chemical pesticides being efficient and low toxicity.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_4971",
    "context": "In order to promote the clean vegetables into thousands of homes activity, Wanjia Service Company asked market analysts to analyze the market prospects of this activity at two local universities. The report of market analysts believes that these two universities have gathered many national-level universities's senior intellectuals in China, providing washed and packaged vegetables can save them a lot of housework time and do a better job of teaching and research, so they will be welcomed by them.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following items, if true, can weaken the above conclusion the most?",
    "options": [
      "A) I feel that the price of vegetables is only slightly higher than those sold in the general market",
      "B) Most families in these two universities hire part-time workers to do all kinds of housework, and the remuneration paid to part-time workers is less than the increase in expenses for buying clean vegetables",
      "C) The variety of clean vegetables is less than the vegetables sold in the general market. i am afraid that it cannot satisfy the tastes of so many teachers in these two universities",
      "D) Buying clean vegetables is still a new thing for most of the teachers in these two universities, and i am afraid that there will be a process of adaptation"
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nIn order to promote the clean vegetables into thousands of homes activity, Wanjia Service Company asked market analysts to analyze the market prospects of this activity at two local universities. The report of market analysts believes that these two universities have gathered many national-level universities's senior intellectuals in China, providing washed and packaged vegetables can save them a lot of housework time and do a better job of teaching and research, so they will be welcomed by them.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following items, if true, can weaken the above conclusion the most?\n# Options:\nA) I feel that the price of vegetables is only slightly higher than those sold in the general market\nB) Most families in these two universities hire part-time workers to do all kinds of housework, and the remuneration paid to part-time workers is less than the increase in expenses for buying clean vegetables\nC) The variety of clean vegetables is less than the vegetables sold in the general market. i am afraid that it cannot satisfy the tastes of so many teachers in these two universities\nD) Buying clean vegetables is still a new thing for most of the teachers in these two universities, and i am afraid that there will be a process of adaptation\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe reasoning is that option C directly addresses the lack of variety in clean vegetables, which could deter the target audience (teachers) from accepting the product, thus weakening the conclusion that the universities will welcome it.",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option weakens the conclusion the most. The context says that Wanjia Service Company is promoting their clean vegetables to two local universities. The market analysts think these universities, which have a lot of senior intellectuals, will welcome the washed and packaged veggies because it saves them housework time and helps with teaching and research.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options, if true, would weaken this conclusion the most. Let's break down each option.\n\nOption A says the price is only slightly higher than general market. Hmm, higher prices might affect the budget, but does it directly weaken the conclusion? The conclusion is about the benefit, not the cost. So maybe not the strongest weakener.\n\nOption B mentions part-time workers are hired, and their pay is less than the expense increase. Wait, if part-time workers are doing the housework, maybe the expense saved by buying clean veggies isn't as much. Or maybe the savings aren't enough to offset the cost of veggies. This could mean that the perceived benefit (savings on housework) isn't as significant, so this might weaken the conclusion.\n\nOption C says the variety is less than regular veggies, which might not satisfy teachers' tastes. If the veggies don't meet the teachers' needs, that's a problem for sales. So this directly affects the effectiveness of the promotion, making it a good candidate for weakening the conclusion.\n\nOption D talks about adaptation time. If teachers are new to clean veggies, there might be a learning curve. This could be a barrier, so it weakens the conclusion because it introduces a potential issue that could deter adoption.\n\nNow, comparing B, C, and D. B is about cost savings, C is about product variety, and D is about user adaptation. Which of these has the most significant impact on the conclusion that the universities will welcome the product?\n\nOption C directly addresses the product's appeal. If the variety doesn't meet the teachers' needs, sales could drop. That's a direct issue for the promotion's success. Similarly, D introduces a potential problem that could hinder adoption. B is more about the financial aspect, which might affect profitability but not directly the willingness to adopt.\n\nSo, I think C and D are strong contenders. But C is about the product not meeting the target audience's needs, which is a more immediate and significant weakness than the adaptation process. Therefore, option C is the best answer.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_7184",
    "context": "Virtual team refers to a community composed of two or more people across regions and organizations. They are connected through communication and information technology. They have complementary technologies or skills, common goals, common evaluation and work methods, and they jointly bear the final results and responsibilities.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, according to the above definition, which one of the followings belongs to a virtual team?",
    "options": [
      "A) A publishing firm contacted several experts to jointly compile a new textbook. they work in different units and coordinating their work is mainly through telephone, fax and network.",
      "B) A company transferred each two key employees from the sales department, r&d department and production department to form a new product research team.",
      "C) Xiao li encountered technical problems in his work. he was not familiar with a professional field. he consulted many experts through the internet and telephone, and established regular and fixed contacts with the experts.",
      "D) Xiao tang, xiao sun and xiao liu often chat on the internet and find that they have similar experiences and common hobbies. so they become good friends."
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nVirtual team refers to a community composed of two or more people across regions and organizations. They are connected through communication and information technology. They have complementary technologies or skills, common goals, common evaluation and work methods, and they jointly bear the final results and responsibilities.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above definition, which one of the followings belongs to a virtual team?\n# Options:\nA) A publishing firm contacted several experts to jointly compile a new textbook. they work in different units and coordinating their work is mainly through telephone, fax and network.\nB) A company transferred each two key employees from the sales department, r&d department and production department to form a new product research team.\nC) Xiao li encountered technical problems in his work. he was not familiar with a professional field. he consulted many experts through the internet and telephone, and established regular and fixed contacts with the experts.\nD) Xiao tang, xiao sun and xiao liu often chat on the internet and find that they have similar experiences and common hobbies. so they become good friends.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe reasoning is that option C directly addresses the lack of variety in clean vegetables, which could deter the target audience (teachers) from accepting the product, thus weakening the conclusion that the universities will welcome it.",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option weakens the conclusion the most. The context says that Wanjia Service Company is promoting their clean vegetables to two local universities. The market analysts think these universities, which have a lot of senior intellectuals, will welcome the washed and packaged veggies because it saves them housework time and helps with teaching and research.\n\nThe question is asking which of the options, if true, would weaken this conclusion the most. Let's break down each option.\n\nOption A says the price is only slightly higher than general market. Hmm, higher prices might affect the budget, but does it directly weaken the conclusion? The conclusion is about the benefit, not the cost. So maybe not the strongest weakener.\n\nOption B mentions part-time workers are hired, and their pay is less than the expense increase. Wait, if part-time workers are doing the housework, maybe the expense saved by buying clean veggies isn't as much. Or maybe the savings aren't enough to offset the cost of veggies. This could mean that the perceived benefit (savings on housework) isn't as significant, so this might weaken the conclusion.\n\nOption C says the variety is less than regular veggies, which might not satisfy teachers' tastes. If the veggies don't meet the teachers' needs, that's a problem for sales. So this directly affects the effectiveness of the promotion, making it a good candidate for weakening the conclusion.\n\nOption D talks about adaptation time. If teachers are new to clean veggies, there might be a learning curve. This could be a barrier, so it weakens the conclusion because it introduces a potential issue that could deter adoption.\n\nNow, comparing B, C, and D. B is about cost savings, C is about product variety, and D is about user adaptation. Which of these has the most significant impact on the conclusion that the universities will welcome the product?\n\nOption C directly addresses the product's appeal. If the variety doesn't meet the teachers' needs, sales could drop. That's a direct issue for the promotion's success. Similarly, D introduces a potential problem that could hinder adoption. B is more about the financial aspect, which might affect profitability but not directly the willingness to adopt.\n\nSo, I think C and D are strong contenders. But C is about the product not meeting the target audience's needs, which is a more immediate and significant weakness than the adaptation process. Therefore, option C is the best answer.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_605",
    "context": "After the plant leaves are injured, green juice will emerge, and the fragrance of the leaves will become stronger. This green fragrance can lure the natural enemies of pests to eliminate pests. Researchers use transgenic methods to introduce the gene of green pepper aroma synthesis enzyme into Arabidopsis thaliana of Cruciferae. Once the larvae of Pieris rapae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis thaliana will be enhanced, The parasitoid lays its eggs on the larvae of Pieris rapae, which can eat all the larvae before they form pupae. Therefore, the researchers conclude that if this research result is applied to the formula of vegetables, the use of pesticides can be greatly reduced",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following premises must be added in order to make the researcher's conclusion valid",
    "options": [
      "A) At present, pesticides used in vegetable cultivation are mainly used to eliminate pests",
      "B) Fragrance of transgenic vegetable leaves will be enhanced after injury",
      "C)  green fragrance of plants is their weapon of self-defense",
      "D) At present, china's current law allows the planting of genetically modified vegetables"
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nAfter the plant leaves are injured, green juice will emerge, and the fragrance of the leaves will become stronger. This green fragrance can lure the natural enemies of pests to eliminate pests. Researchers use transgenic methods to introduce the gene of green pepper aroma synthesis enzyme into Arabidopsis thaliana of Cruciferae. Once the larvae of Pieris rapae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis thaliana will be enhanced, The parasitoid lays its eggs on the larvae of Pieris rapae, which can eat all the larvae before they form pupae. Therefore, the researchers conclude that if this research result is applied to the formula of vegetables, the use of pesticides can be greatly reduced\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following premises must be added in order to make the researcher's conclusion valid\n# Options:\nA) At present, pesticides used in vegetable cultivation are mainly used to eliminate pests\nB) Fragrance of transgenic vegetable leaves will be enhanced after injury\nC)  green fragrance of plants is their weapon of self-defense\nD) At present, china's current law allows the planting of genetically modified vegetables\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe reasoning is that premise B directly supports the effectiveness of the increased fragrance in attracting natural enemies, which is crucial for the conclusion that using transgenic vegetables can reduce pesticide use.",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which premise is needed to make the researcher's conclusion valid. The context says that when the leaves of Arabidopsis thaliana are injured, green juice comes out and the fragrance becomes stronger. This strong fragrance attracts natural enemies of pests, which then eliminate the pests. They used transgenic methods to introduce a gene for green pepper aroma synthesis into Arabidopsis. When the Pieris rapae larvae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis increases. The parasitoid lays eggs on these larvae, and before they become pupae, the larvae are eaten, reducing the pest population. The researchers conclude that applying this to vegetables can reduce the use of pesticides.\n\nThe question is asking which premise must be added to make this conclusion valid. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nI need to think about what the conclusion is. The conclusion is that using transgenic vegetables with increased green fragrance can reduce pesticide use because the natural enemies will take care of the pests. So, the reasoning is that the increased fragrance attracts predators, which then eat the pests before they can harm the crops.\n\nNow, looking at the premises in the context, they mention that when the leaves are injured, the fragrance increases, which lures natural enemies. They also mention that the transgenic method introduces the gene, so the plant produces more of the aroma enzyme, leading to more fragrance. The parasitoid eats the larvae, which are the pests, so the natural enemies are effectively used.\n\nBut to make the conclusion valid, we need to ensure that the increased fragrance actually works as intended. So, the researcher's conclusion is that this method will reduce pesticide use. Therefore, the premise must link the increased fragrance to the effectiveness of the natural enemies.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Current pesticides are mainly used for eliminating pests. This might be relevant because if most pesticides are used for this purpose, then reducing their use would be significant. But does this directly support the conclusion? Maybe not directly, because the conclusion is about the effectiveness of natural enemies, not just the type of pesticides used.\n\nB) Fragrance of transgenic leaves will be enhanced after injury. This seems relevant because the context mentions that when leaves are injured, the fragrance increases. So, if the transgenic leaves are modified to produce more fragrance when injured, that supports the idea that the increased fragrance will attract natural enemies. So this premise would be necessary to validate the conclusion.\n\nC) Green fragrance is a plant's self-defense weapon. This is more of a general statement about plants. While it's true that plants use defense mechanisms, the context specifically talks about the transgenic modification leading to increased fragrance. So, this might not directly support the conclusion unless it's assumed that the plant's natural defense mechanisms are being enhanced, which is what the premise B is about.\n\nD) China's current law allows planting of genetically modified vegetables. This is about legal aspects. While it's important for the practicality of the research, it doesn't directly affect the validity of the conclusion regarding the reduction of pesticides. The conclusion is about the effectiveness of the method, not whether it's legally feasible.\n\nSo, the key point is that the increased fragrance must be effective in attracting natural enemies. The context already mentions that when leaves are injured, the fragrance increases, which lures natural enemies. However, the premise B specifically states that the transgenic leaves' fragrance will be enhanced after injury, which directly supports the effectiveness needed for the conclusion.\n\nTherefore, the correct premise to add is B.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_2736",
    "context": "The test flight of the new aircraft is only a routine test, which is neither successful nor unsuccessful.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following is the most appropriate evaluation of the stem of the question? There are no loopholes in the dry statement.",
    "options": [
      "A) There is a loophole in the dry statement, which also appears in the following statement:the results of this social survey on the price issue can not be said to fully reflect public opinion, nor can it be said that it does not reflect public opinion at all.",
      "B) There is a loophole in the dry statement, which also appears in the following statement: this pre-exam tutoring can be said to be neither a complete success nor a complete failure.",
      "C) There is a loophole in the statement of the subject stem, which also appears in the following statement: human beings have special powers, which is neither a scientific conclusion proved by facts, nor a pseudoscientific conclusion that is purely fraudulent.",
      "D) There is a loophole in the dry statement, which also appears in the following statement: in the upcoming college student debate competition, i do not think that our school's team will definitely enter the top four, nor do i think our school's team may enter the top four."
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nThe test flight of the new aircraft is only a routine test, which is neither successful nor unsuccessful.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is the most appropriate evaluation of the stem of the question? There are no loopholes in the dry statement.\n# Options:\nA) There is a loophole in the dry statement, which also appears in the following statement:the results of this social survey on the price issue can not be said to fully reflect public opinion, nor can it be said that it does not reflect public opinion at all.\nB) There is a loophole in the dry statement, which also appears in the following statement: this pre-exam tutoring can be said to be neither a complete success nor a complete failure.\nC) There is a loophole in the statement of the subject stem, which also appears in the following statement: human beings have special powers, which is neither a scientific conclusion proved by facts, nor a pseudoscientific conclusion that is purely fraudulent.\nD) There is a loophole in the dry statement, which also appears in the following statement: in the upcoming college student debate competition, i do not think that our school's team will definitely enter the top four, nor do i think our school's team may enter the top four.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe reasoning is that premise B directly supports the effectiveness of the increased fragrance in attracting natural enemies, which is crucial for the conclusion that using transgenic vegetables can reduce pesticide use.",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which premise is needed to make the researcher's conclusion valid. The context says that when the leaves of Arabidopsis thaliana are injured, green juice comes out and the fragrance becomes stronger. This strong fragrance attracts natural enemies of pests, which then eliminate the pests. They used transgenic methods to introduce a gene for green pepper aroma synthesis into Arabidopsis. When the Pieris rapae larvae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis increases. The parasitoid lays eggs on these larvae, and before they become pupae, the larvae are eaten, reducing the pest population. The researchers conclude that applying this to vegetables can reduce the use of pesticides.\n\nThe question is asking which premise must be added to make this conclusion valid. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nI need to think about what the conclusion is. The conclusion is that using transgenic vegetables with increased green fragrance can reduce pesticide use because the natural enemies will take care of the pests. So, the reasoning is that the increased fragrance attracts predators, which then eat the pests before they can harm the crops.\n\nNow, looking at the premises in the context, they mention that when the leaves are injured, the fragrance increases, which lures natural enemies. They also mention that the transgenic method introduces the gene, so the plant produces more of the aroma enzyme, leading to more fragrance. The parasitoid eats the larvae, which are the pests, so the natural enemies are effectively used.\n\nBut to make the conclusion valid, we need to ensure that the increased fragrance actually works as intended. So, the researcher's conclusion is that this method will reduce pesticide use. Therefore, the premise must link the increased fragrance to the effectiveness of the natural enemies.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Current pesticides are mainly used for eliminating pests. This might be relevant because if most pesticides are used for this purpose, then reducing their use would be significant. But does this directly support the conclusion? Maybe not directly, because the conclusion is about the effectiveness of natural enemies, not just the type of pesticides used.\n\nB) Fragrance of transgenic leaves will be enhanced after injury. This seems relevant because the context mentions that when leaves are injured, the fragrance increases. So, if the transgenic leaves are modified to produce more fragrance when injured, that supports the idea that the increased fragrance will attract natural enemies. So this premise would be necessary to validate the conclusion.\n\nC) Green fragrance is a plant's self-defense weapon. This is more of a general statement about plants. While it's true that plants use defense mechanisms, the context specifically talks about the transgenic modification leading to increased fragrance. So, this might not directly support the conclusion unless it's assumed that the plant's natural defense mechanisms are being enhanced, which is what the premise B is about.\n\nD) China's current law allows planting of genetically modified vegetables. This is about legal aspects. While it's important for the practicality of the research, it doesn't directly affect the validity of the conclusion regarding the reduction of pesticides. The conclusion is about the effectiveness of the method, not whether it's legally feasible.\n\nSo, the key point is that the increased fragrance must be effective in attracting natural enemies. The context already mentions that when leaves are injured, the fragrance increases, which lures natural enemies. However, the premise B specifically states that the transgenic leaves' fragrance will be enhanced after injury, which directly supports the effectiveness needed for the conclusion.\n\nTherefore, the correct premise to add is B.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_7040",
    "context": "Those who silently read TV news and advertising program manuscripts can remember and recall more information than those who watch the TV program itself; If you despise the value principle of a magazine, you can still read it with interest. If this happens to the TV program, you can't watch it and change the channel quickly. In this case, we don't want to spend time on disagreeing views, so it's impossible to reflect. In fact, there will be fewer doubts about everything. If we put the two written records together, it's easy to find inconsistencies, It can even distinguish the difference of authority between information; Comparing two video reports is troublesome. When asked to choose from two conflicting TV reports, the audience either depends on intuition or what they believe before watching.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, this paragraph is intended to illustrate that:",
    "options": [
      "A) Reading text is more helpful for thinking than watching tv",
      "B) The way of information reception affects people's behavior",
      "C) Television can make people form wrong values",
      "D) People who like reading are calmer than those who like watching tv"
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nThose who silently read TV news and advertising program manuscripts can remember and recall more information than those who watch the TV program itself; If you despise the value principle of a magazine, you can still read it with interest. If this happens to the TV program, you can't watch it and change the channel quickly. In this case, we don't want to spend time on disagreeing views, so it's impossible to reflect. In fact, there will be fewer doubts about everything. If we put the two written records together, it's easy to find inconsistencies, It can even distinguish the difference of authority between information; Comparing two video reports is troublesome. When asked to choose from two conflicting TV reports, the audience either depends on intuition or what they believe before watching.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, this paragraph is intended to illustrate that:\n# Options:\nA) Reading text is more helpful for thinking than watching tv\nB) The way of information reception affects people's behavior\nC) Television can make people form wrong values\nD) People who like reading are calmer than those who like watching tv\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe context emphasizes that reading written materials, such as TV news manuscripts, leads to better information retention and analysis compared to watching TV. This supports the idea that reading is more helpful for thinking.\n\nThe correct option is: A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option correctly answers the question based on the given context. Let me read through the context again to understand what it's saying.\n\nThe context talks about how people who read TV news and advertising manuscripts can remember and recall more information than those who watch the TV program. That suggests that reading might be more effective for retaining information than watching TV. \n\nThen it mentions that if someone despises the value principle of a magazine, they might still read it with interest. But if the TV program reflects that, they can't quickly change the channel, leading to potential disagreements. The paragraph also states that putting written records together helps find inconsistencies and distinguishes authority, which makes comparing video reports difficult. When faced with conflicting TV reports, people rely on intuition or prior beliefs rather than objective analysis.\n\nSo, putting this together, the context is discussing how reading written materials (like manuscripts) is more effective for retaining and understanding information compared to watching TV. It also implies that the written form allows for better analysis and consistency, whereas TV reports can be harder to compare and might lead to quicker, more intuitive decisions without full analysis.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Reading text is more helpful for thinking than watching TV. This seems to fit because the context shows that reading leads to better retention and analysis.\n\nB) The way of information reception affects people's behavior. While true, the context doesn't specifically focus on behavior but more on the effectiveness of reading versus watching.\n\nC) Television can make people form wrong values. The context doesn't support this; it's more about the effectiveness of reading versus watching, not about the values formed by TV.\n\nD) People who like reading are calmer than those who like watching TV. There's no information about personality traits or calmness here.\n\nSo, the correct answer should be A.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_15097",
    "context": "Asbestos, an almost indestructible mineral once installed as building insulation, poses no health risk unless the asbestos is disturbed and asbestos fibers are released into the environment. Since removing asbestos from buildings disturbs it, thereby releasing asbestos fibers, the government should not require removal of all asbestos insulation.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?",
    "options": [
      "A) Some kinds of asbestos, when disturbed, pose greater health risks than do other kinds.",
      "B) Much of the time, removed asbestos is buried in landfills and forgotten, with no guarantee that it will not be disturbed again.",
      "C) Asbestos can pose a health threat to workers who remove it without wearing required protective gear.",
      "D) Asbestos poses far less risk to health than does smoking, drug and alcohol abuse, improper diet, or lack of exercise."
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nAsbestos, an almost indestructible mineral once installed as building insulation, poses no health risk unless the asbestos is disturbed and asbestos fibers are released into the environment. Since removing asbestos from buildings disturbs it, thereby releasing asbestos fibers, the government should not require removal of all asbestos insulation.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which one of the following, if true, most strengthens the argument?\n# Options:\nA) Some kinds of asbestos, when disturbed, pose greater health risks than do other kinds.\nB) Much of the time, removed asbestos is buried in landfills and forgotten, with no guarantee that it will not be disturbed again.\nC) Asbestos can pose a health threat to workers who remove it without wearing required protective gear.\nD) Asbestos poses far less risk to health than does smoking, drug and alcohol abuse, improper diet, or lack of exercise.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe reasoning is that Option B supports the argument by showing that in many cases, removed asbestos is buried and forgotten, reducing the risk of further disturbance and release. This strengthens the government's stance against requiring removal of all asbestos. \n\nAnswer: The correct option is: B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which option strengthens the argument given. The argument is about whether the government should not require the removal of all asbestos insulation because removing it disturbs the material and releases fibers, which can pose health risks. \n\nLet me break down the context first. Asbestos is used as insulation, and it's almost indestructible. However, when it's removed, it becomes disturbed, releasing fibers that can cause health issues. The argument is against requiring the removal of all asbestos because the act of removing it itself can lead to health risks.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options, if true, would most strengthen this argument. So, I need to evaluate each option to see how it supports the idea that the government shouldn't require removing all asbestos.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nOption A: Some kinds of asbestos, when disturbed, pose greater health risks than others. This seems relevant because it points out that not all types of asbestos are equally dangerous. If some are more dangerous, then maybe the government's argument that removing all isn't necessary because some are worse could be strengthened. But I'm not sure how strong this is because it's only about some kinds, not all.\n\nOption B: Much of the time, removed asbestos is buried in landfills and forgotten, so it might not be disturbed again. This actually supports the argument because if it's buried and forgotten, it won't be disturbed again, so the health risks from release are minimized. This seems like a good point because it shows that in many cases, removing asbestos doesn't lead to more release, which would support the government's stance.\n\nOption C: Asbestos can pose a health threat to workers who remove it without proper protective gear. This is a valid point because it highlights the danger to workers, but the argument is about the government not requiring removal. While this strengthens the case for caution, it doesn't directly address whether the government should require removal or not. It's more about the consequences of removal rather than the necessity of removal.\n\nOption D: Asbestos poses far less risk than other factors like smoking, drug abuse, etc. This is interesting because it's comparing asbestos to other significant health risks. However, the argument is specifically about the disturbance during removal leading to health risks, not about the overall risk level of asbestos. So while this is true, it doesn't directly strengthen the government's argument about not removing all asbestos.\n\nNow, considering each option, Option B directly addresses the scenario where removing asbestos is less likely to cause further disturbances if it's properly stored. This would mean that the government's argument holds more weight because it's acknowledging that in many cases, the removal doesn't lead to more release, thus supporting the idea that not all needs to be removed.\n\nOption A is also relevant but only talks about some kinds, which might not be as impactful as Option B, which is about the process of removal leading to less disturbance overall.\n\nSo, I think Option B is the strongest because it provides a scenario where the government's argument is validated, making it more plausible that not all asbestos needs to be removed.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_15055",
    "context": "In our solar system only one of the nine planets -- Earth -- qualifies as fit to sustain life. Nonetheless, using this ratio, and considering the astonishingly large number of planetary systems in the universe, we must conclude that the number of planets fit to sustain some form of life is extremely large.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, the argument is questionable because it presumes which one of the following without providing justification?",
    "options": [
      "A) Life similar to earth's could evolve under conditions very different from those on earth.",
      "B) If a planet is earthlike, then life will arise on it.",
      "C) Our solar system is similar to many other planetary systems in the universe.",
      "D) Most other planetary systems in the universe have nine planets."
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nIn our solar system only one of the nine planets -- Earth -- qualifies as fit to sustain life. Nonetheless, using this ratio, and considering the astonishingly large number of planetary systems in the universe, we must conclude that the number of planets fit to sustain some form of life is extremely large.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, the argument is questionable because it presumes which one of the following without providing justification?\n# Options:\nA) Life similar to earth's could evolve under conditions very different from those on earth.\nB) If a planet is earthlike, then life will arise on it.\nC) Our solar system is similar to many other planetary systems in the universe.\nD) Most other planetary systems in the universe have nine planets.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe argument assumes that our solar system is similar to others in the universe, allowing the ratio of Earth-like planets to be applied elsewhere. This is option C.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context says that in our solar system, only Earth fits to sustain life. But because of the large number of planetary systems in the universe, the argument concludes that many planets can sustain life. The question is asking what assumption the argument is making without justification, and the options are A to D.\n\nHmm, so the argument is using the fact that only Earth is suitable for life in our solar system, but since there are so many planetary systems, it infers that many can sustain life. But wait, the problem is that the argument assumes something about other planetary systems.\n\nLet me break it down. The argument is saying: If only Earth (out of many planets in our solar system) can sustain life, and there are many planetary systems in the universe, then the number of life-sustaining planets must be large. But this reasoning relies on an assumption that isn't stated.\n\nWhat's the missing link here? Well, the argument assumes that the number of planets in other systems is similar to Earth's. So, it's like saying, if in our solar system, only one out of nine can sustain life, and there are N planetary systems, then the total number is N*(1/9). But this only works if each planetary system has a similar ratio of Earth-like planets to others.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Life similar to Earth's could evolve under different conditions. This seems related to the idea that maybe other planets could have life without Earth's conditions. But the argument doesn't rely on this; it's more about the number of planets, not the possibility of life elsewhere.\n\nB) If a planet is Earth-like, life will arise on it. This is about the existence of life given Earth-like conditions. The argument doesn't depend on this assumption because it's about the number of Earth-like planets, not whether they will have life.\n\nC) Our solar system is similar to many others in the universe. This seems relevant because if our solar system is typical, then the ratio applies elsewhere. The argument presumes that other systems are like ours, so this is the missing assumption.\n\nD) Most other planetary systems have nine planets. This doesn't make sense because the argument is about Earth-like planets, not the total number of planets. So this option is probably incorrect.\n\nSo, the argument assumes that our solar system is a typical example, which is option C. Without this assumption, the argument wouldn't hold because it wouldn't know if other systems have similar ratios of Earth-like planets.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_1646",
    "context": "According to the ancient Chinese literature, the word rice has appeared in the oracle bone inscriptions of the Shang Dynasty, and millet and rice have been mentioned in the Book of songs. Before the Spring and Autumn period, because of the small amount of rice planted in the north of China, rice was classified as five Grains: he, Yu, Gu, wheat, rice. However, in the Song Dynasty, it was upgraded because of the large amount of planting, and the saying that SuZhou and HuZhou ripe and the world is sufficient spread among the people. In the Ming Dynasty, rice became the main food in China.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, it can be seen that which of the following is correct?",
    "options": [
      "A) Rice has been planted since the shang dynasty",
      "B) During the spring and autumn period, rice was not produced in the north",
      "C) Rice was the main food in the song dynasty",
      "D) Rice has long been one of the five grains in ancient china"
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nAccording to the ancient Chinese literature, the word rice has appeared in the oracle bone inscriptions of the Shang Dynasty, and millet and rice have been mentioned in the Book of songs. Before the Spring and Autumn period, because of the small amount of rice planted in the north of China, rice was classified as five Grains: he, Yu, Gu, wheat, rice. However, in the Song Dynasty, it was upgraded because of the large amount of planting, and the saying that SuZhou and HuZhou ripe and the world is sufficient spread among the people. In the Ming Dynasty, rice became the main food in China.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, it can be seen that which of the following is correct?\n# Options:\nA) Rice has been planted since the shang dynasty\nB) During the spring and autumn period, rice was not produced in the north\nC) Rice was the main food in the song dynasty\nD) Rice has long been one of the five grains in ancient china\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe argument assumes that our solar system is similar to others in the universe, allowing the ratio of Earth-like planets to be applied elsewhere. This is option C.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context says that in our solar system, only Earth fits to sustain life. But because of the large number of planetary systems in the universe, the argument concludes that many planets can sustain life. The question is asking what assumption the argument is making without justification, and the options are A to D.\n\nHmm, so the argument is using the fact that only Earth is suitable for life in our solar system, but since there are so many planetary systems, it infers that many can sustain life. But wait, the problem is that the argument assumes something about other planetary systems.\n\nLet me break it down. The argument is saying: If only Earth (out of many planets in our solar system) can sustain life, and there are many planetary systems in the universe, then the number of life-sustaining planets must be large. But this reasoning relies on an assumption that isn't stated.\n\nWhat's the missing link here? Well, the argument assumes that the number of planets in other systems is similar to Earth's. So, it's like saying, if in our solar system, only one out of nine can sustain life, and there are N planetary systems, then the total number is N*(1/9). But this only works if each planetary system has a similar ratio of Earth-like planets to others.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Life similar to Earth's could evolve under different conditions. This seems related to the idea that maybe other planets could have life without Earth's conditions. But the argument doesn't rely on this; it's more about the number of planets, not the possibility of life elsewhere.\n\nB) If a planet is Earth-like, life will arise on it. This is about the existence of life given Earth-like conditions. The argument doesn't depend on this assumption because it's about the number of Earth-like planets, not whether they will have life.\n\nC) Our solar system is similar to many others in the universe. This seems relevant because if our solar system is typical, then the ratio applies elsewhere. The argument presumes that other systems are like ours, so this is the missing assumption.\n\nD) Most other planetary systems have nine planets. This doesn't make sense because the argument is about Earth-like planets, not the total number of planets. So this option is probably incorrect.\n\nSo, the argument assumes that our solar system is a typical example, which is option C. Without this assumption, the argument wouldn't hold because it wouldn't know if other systems have similar ratios of Earth-like planets.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_1595",
    "context": "In the micro world, neutrinos have always been an omnipresent and unpredictable passer-by. Neutrinos are produced in many ways, such as nuclear reaction in stars, supernova explosion, cosmic ray impact with the earth's atmosphere, and even the decay of rocks and other substances on the earth. Although they may be one of the most common particles in our universe. But because of its strong penetration and almost no interaction with other substances, it is the least known of the basic particles.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following is not an accurate understanding of neutrinos?",
    "options": [
      "A) Neutrinos are ubiquitous in the micro world.",
      "B) The nuclear reaction inside perseverance is one of the ways to produce neutrinos.",
      "C) Neutrinos are the most common particles in our universe.",
      "D) Humans know very little about neutrinos."
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nIn the micro world, neutrinos have always been an omnipresent and unpredictable passer-by. Neutrinos are produced in many ways, such as nuclear reaction in stars, supernova explosion, cosmic ray impact with the earth's atmosphere, and even the decay of rocks and other substances on the earth. Although they may be one of the most common particles in our universe. But because of its strong penetration and almost no interaction with other substances, it is the least known of the basic particles.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is not an accurate understanding of neutrinos?\n# Options:\nA) Neutrinos are ubiquitous in the micro world.\nB) The nuclear reaction inside perseverance is one of the ways to produce neutrinos.\nC) Neutrinos are the most common particles in our universe.\nD) Humans know very little about neutrinos.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe reasoning is that option B likely contains a typo (\"perseverance\" instead of \"stars\") and doesn't accurately describe neutrino production. The other options correctly reflect the context provided.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option is not an accurate understanding of neutrinos based on the given context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context says that neutrinos are produced in many ways, such as nuclear reactions in stars, supernova explosions, cosmic ray impacts on Earth's atmosphere, and the decay of rocks and substances on Earth. It also mentions that neutrinos are common in the universe because they're produced so much, but they're hard to study because they don't interact much with other matter. So they're almost the least known basic particles.\n\nNow, looking at the question: which option is not an accurate understanding. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nOption A: \"Neutrinos are ubiquitous in the micro world.\" The context says they're produced in many ways, including nuclear reactions in stars, which are micro in the sense of atomic processes. So they're everywhere in the universe, making A accurate.\n\nOption B: \"The nuclear reaction inside perseverance is one of the ways to produce neutrinos.\" Wait, that doesn't make sense. The word \"perseverance\" isn't in the context. Maybe it's a typo? It should probably be \"nuclear reaction in stars.\" If that's the case, then B is accurate. But if it's a typo, then B is incorrect. However, since the context mentions nuclear reactions in stars, I think B is trying to refer to that, so maybe it's correct. Alternatively, if \"perseverance\" is a typo, then B is wrong. But without knowing, I'll assume it's a typo and B is incorrect.\n\nOption C: \"Neutrinos are the most common particles in our universe.\" The context says they're one of the most common because they're produced so much, but it doesn't explicitly say they're the most common. It just says they're common. So C might be stretching it, but it's still somewhat accurate because they're produced in abundance.\n\nOption D: \"Humans know very little about neutrinos.\" The context says they're the least known particles because they don't interact much, so D is accurate.\n\nSo, the question is which is NOT accurate. If B is incorrect because of the typo, then B is the answer. Alternatively, if B is correct despite the typo, then maybe another option is wrong. But since the other options seem accurate, I think B is the one that's not accurate because of the typo.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_605",
    "context": "After the plant leaves are injured, green juice will emerge, and the fragrance of the leaves will become stronger. This green fragrance can lure the natural enemies of pests to eliminate pests. Researchers use transgenic methods to introduce the gene of green pepper aroma synthesis enzyme into Arabidopsis thaliana of Cruciferae. Once the larvae of Pieris rapae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis thaliana will be enhanced, The parasitoid lays its eggs on the larvae of Pieris rapae, which can eat all the larvae before they form pupae. Therefore, the researchers conclude that if this research result is applied to the formula of vegetables, the use of pesticides can be greatly reduced",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following premises must be added in order to make the researcher's conclusion valid",
    "options": [
      "A) At present, pesticides used in vegetable cultivation are mainly used to eliminate pests",
      "B) Fragrance of transgenic vegetable leaves will be enhanced after injury",
      "C)  green fragrance of plants is their weapon of self-defense",
      "D) At present, china's current law allows the planting of genetically modified vegetables"
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nAfter the plant leaves are injured, green juice will emerge, and the fragrance of the leaves will become stronger. This green fragrance can lure the natural enemies of pests to eliminate pests. Researchers use transgenic methods to introduce the gene of green pepper aroma synthesis enzyme into Arabidopsis thaliana of Cruciferae. Once the larvae of Pieris rapae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis thaliana will be enhanced, The parasitoid lays its eggs on the larvae of Pieris rapae, which can eat all the larvae before they form pupae. Therefore, the researchers conclude that if this research result is applied to the formula of vegetables, the use of pesticides can be greatly reduced\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following premises must be added in order to make the researcher's conclusion valid\n# Options:\nA) At present, pesticides used in vegetable cultivation are mainly used to eliminate pests\nB) Fragrance of transgenic vegetable leaves will be enhanced after injury\nC)  green fragrance of plants is their weapon of self-defense\nD) At present, china's current law allows the planting of genetically modified vegetables\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe reasoning is that premise B directly supports the effectiveness of the increased fragrance in attracting natural enemies, which is crucial for the conclusion that using transgenic vegetables can reduce pesticide use.",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which premise is needed to make the researcher's conclusion valid. The context says that when the leaves of Arabidopsis thaliana are injured, green juice comes out and the fragrance becomes stronger. This strong fragrance attracts natural enemies of pests, which then eliminate the pests. They used transgenic methods to introduce a gene for green pepper aroma synthesis into Arabidopsis. When the Pieris rapae larvae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis increases. The parasitoid lays eggs on these larvae, and before they become pupae, the larvae are eaten, reducing the pest population. The researchers conclude that applying this to vegetables can reduce the use of pesticides.\n\nThe question is asking which premise must be added to make this conclusion valid. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nI need to think about what the conclusion is. The conclusion is that using transgenic vegetables with increased green fragrance can reduce pesticide use because the natural enemies will take care of the pests. So, the reasoning is that the increased fragrance attracts predators, which then eat the pests before they can harm the crops.\n\nNow, looking at the premises in the context, they mention that when the leaves are injured, the fragrance increases, which lures natural enemies. They also mention that the transgenic method introduces the gene, so the plant produces more of the aroma enzyme, leading to more fragrance. The parasitoid eats the larvae, which are the pests, so the natural enemies are effectively used.\n\nBut to make the conclusion valid, we need to ensure that the increased fragrance actually works as intended. So, the researcher's conclusion is that this method will reduce pesticide use. Therefore, the premise must link the increased fragrance to the effectiveness of the natural enemies.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Current pesticides are mainly used for eliminating pests. This might be relevant because if most pesticides are used for this purpose, then reducing their use would be significant. But does this directly support the conclusion? Maybe not directly, because the conclusion is about the effectiveness of natural enemies, not just the type of pesticides used.\n\nB) Fragrance of transgenic leaves will be enhanced after injury. This seems relevant because the context mentions that when leaves are injured, the fragrance increases. So, if the transgenic leaves are modified to produce more fragrance when injured, that supports the idea that the increased fragrance will attract natural enemies. So this premise would be necessary to validate the conclusion.\n\nC) Green fragrance is a plant's self-defense weapon. This is more of a general statement about plants. While it's true that plants use defense mechanisms, the context specifically talks about the transgenic modification leading to increased fragrance. So, this might not directly support the conclusion unless it's assumed that the plant's natural defense mechanisms are being enhanced, which is what the premise B is about.\n\nD) China's current law allows planting of genetically modified vegetables. This is about legal aspects. While it's important for the practicality of the research, it doesn't directly affect the validity of the conclusion regarding the reduction of pesticides. The conclusion is about the effectiveness of the method, not whether it's legally feasible.\n\nSo, the key point is that the increased fragrance must be effective in attracting natural enemies. The context already mentions that when leaves are injured, the fragrance increases, which lures natural enemies. However, the premise B specifically states that the transgenic leaves' fragrance will be enhanced after injury, which directly supports the effectiveness needed for the conclusion.\n\nTherefore, the correct premise to add is B.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_605",
    "context": "After the plant leaves are injured, green juice will emerge, and the fragrance of the leaves will become stronger. This green fragrance can lure the natural enemies of pests to eliminate pests. Researchers use transgenic methods to introduce the gene of green pepper aroma synthesis enzyme into Arabidopsis thaliana of Cruciferae. Once the larvae of Pieris rapae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis thaliana will be enhanced, The parasitoid lays its eggs on the larvae of Pieris rapae, which can eat all the larvae before they form pupae. Therefore, the researchers conclude that if this research result is applied to the formula of vegetables, the use of pesticides can be greatly reduced",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following premises must be added in order to make the researcher's conclusion valid",
    "options": [
      "A) At present, pesticides used in vegetable cultivation are mainly used to eliminate pests",
      "B) Fragrance of transgenic vegetable leaves will be enhanced after injury",
      "C)  green fragrance of plants is their weapon of self-defense",
      "D) At present, china's current law allows the planting of genetically modified vegetables"
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nAfter the plant leaves are injured, green juice will emerge, and the fragrance of the leaves will become stronger. This green fragrance can lure the natural enemies of pests to eliminate pests. Researchers use transgenic methods to introduce the gene of green pepper aroma synthesis enzyme into Arabidopsis thaliana of Cruciferae. Once the larvae of Pieris rapae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis thaliana will be enhanced, The parasitoid lays its eggs on the larvae of Pieris rapae, which can eat all the larvae before they form pupae. Therefore, the researchers conclude that if this research result is applied to the formula of vegetables, the use of pesticides can be greatly reduced\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following premises must be added in order to make the researcher's conclusion valid\n# Options:\nA) At present, pesticides used in vegetable cultivation are mainly used to eliminate pests\nB) Fragrance of transgenic vegetable leaves will be enhanced after injury\nC)  green fragrance of plants is their weapon of self-defense\nD) At present, china's current law allows the planting of genetically modified vegetables\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe reasoning is that premise B directly supports the effectiveness of the increased fragrance in attracting natural enemies, which is crucial for the conclusion that using transgenic vegetables can reduce pesticide use.",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which premise is needed to make the researcher's conclusion valid. The context says that when the leaves of Arabidopsis thaliana are injured, green juice comes out and the fragrance becomes stronger. This strong fragrance attracts natural enemies of pests, which then eliminate the pests. They used transgenic methods to introduce a gene for green pepper aroma synthesis into Arabidopsis. When the Pieris rapae larvae eat the leaves, the fragrance of Arabidopsis increases. The parasitoid lays eggs on these larvae, and before they become pupae, the larvae are eaten, reducing the pest population. The researchers conclude that applying this to vegetables can reduce the use of pesticides.\n\nThe question is asking which premise must be added to make this conclusion valid. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nI need to think about what the conclusion is. The conclusion is that using transgenic vegetables with increased green fragrance can reduce pesticide use because the natural enemies will take care of the pests. So, the reasoning is that the increased fragrance attracts predators, which then eat the pests before they can harm the crops.\n\nNow, looking at the premises in the context, they mention that when the leaves are injured, the fragrance increases, which lures natural enemies. They also mention that the transgenic method introduces the gene, so the plant produces more of the aroma enzyme, leading to more fragrance. The parasitoid eats the larvae, which are the pests, so the natural enemies are effectively used.\n\nBut to make the conclusion valid, we need to ensure that the increased fragrance actually works as intended. So, the researcher's conclusion is that this method will reduce pesticide use. Therefore, the premise must link the increased fragrance to the effectiveness of the natural enemies.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) Current pesticides are mainly used for eliminating pests. This might be relevant because if most pesticides are used for this purpose, then reducing their use would be significant. But does this directly support the conclusion? Maybe not directly, because the conclusion is about the effectiveness of natural enemies, not just the type of pesticides used.\n\nB) Fragrance of transgenic leaves will be enhanced after injury. This seems relevant because the context mentions that when leaves are injured, the fragrance increases. So, if the transgenic leaves are modified to produce more fragrance when injured, that supports the idea that the increased fragrance will attract natural enemies. So this premise would be necessary to validate the conclusion.\n\nC) Green fragrance is a plant's self-defense weapon. This is more of a general statement about plants. While it's true that plants use defense mechanisms, the context specifically talks about the transgenic modification leading to increased fragrance. So, this might not directly support the conclusion unless it's assumed that the plant's natural defense mechanisms are being enhanced, which is what the premise B is about.\n\nD) China's current law allows planting of genetically modified vegetables. This is about legal aspects. While it's important for the practicality of the research, it doesn't directly affect the validity of the conclusion regarding the reduction of pesticides. The conclusion is about the effectiveness of the method, not whether it's legally feasible.\n\nSo, the key point is that the increased fragrance must be effective in attracting natural enemies. The context already mentions that when leaves are injured, the fragrance increases, which lures natural enemies. However, the premise B specifically states that the transgenic leaves' fragrance will be enhanced after injury, which directly supports the effectiveness needed for the conclusion.\n\nTherefore, the correct premise to add is B.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_11635",
    "context": "Whenever a major political scandal erupts before an election and voters blame the scandal on all parties about equally, virtually all incumbents, from whatever party, seeking reelection are returned to office. However, when voters blame such a scandal on only one party, incumbents from that party are likely to be defeated by challengers from other parties. The proportion of incumbents who seek reelection is high and remarkably constant from election to election.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, if the voters' reactions are guided by a principle, which one of the following principles would best account for the contrast in reactions described above?",
    "options": [
      "A) Major political scandals can practically always be blamed on incumbents, but whether those incumbents should be voted out of office depends on who their challengers are.",
      "B) When a major political scandal is blamed on incumbents from all parties, that judgment is more accurate than any judgment that incumbents from only one party are to blame.",
      "C) When major political scandals are less the responsibility of individual incumbents than of the parties to which they belong, whatever party was responsible must be penalized when possible.",
      "D) Incumbents who are rightly blamed for a major political scandal should not seek reelection, but if they do, they should not be returned to office."
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nWhenever a major political scandal erupts before an election and voters blame the scandal on all parties about equally, virtually all incumbents, from whatever party, seeking reelection are returned to office. However, when voters blame such a scandal on only one party, incumbents from that party are likely to be defeated by challengers from other parties. The proportion of incumbents who seek reelection is high and remarkably constant from election to election.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, if the voters' reactions are guided by a principle, which one of the following principles would best account for the contrast in reactions described above?\n# Options:\nA) Major political scandals can practically always be blamed on incumbents, but whether those incumbents should be voted out of office depends on who their challengers are.\nB) When a major political scandal is blamed on incumbents from all parties, that judgment is more accurate than any judgment that incumbents from only one party are to blame.\nC) When major political scandals are less the responsibility of individual incumbents than of the parties to which they belong, whatever party was responsible must be penalized when possible.\nD) Incumbents who are rightly blamed for a major political scandal should not seek reelection, but if they do, they should not be returned to office.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe principle that best explains the contrast is that when major political scandals are more the responsibility of a particular party, that party must be penalized when possible. This aligns with the context where voters' reactions lead to incumbents from the blamed party being defeated.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which principle best explains the situation described in the context. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that when a major political scandal happens before an election, voters blame the scandal on all parties equally. In this case, all incumbents, regardless of their party, get re-elected. That makes sense because if the scandal is spread across all parties, voters might not have a clear favorite to blame, so they stick with the current incumbents.\n\nBut then, if the voters blame the scandal on only one party, that party's incumbents are likely to lose. So, in this scenario, the party that's being blamed loses a lot, and other parties' challengers win. This seems to be about how voters perceive responsibility and how that affects the election outcome.\n\nNow, looking at the question, it's asking which principle explains this contrast. The options are A, B, C, D. Let me go through each one.\n\nOption A says that major political scandals can practically always be blamed on incumbents, but whether they should be removed depends on their challengers. Hmm, that doesn't quite fit. The context doesn't talk about whether the scandals should be blamed on incumbents or not, just how voters distribute blame. So maybe A isn't the best fit.\n\nOption B states that blaming incumbents from all parties is more accurate than blaming one party. Wait, the context doesn't mention accuracy of blame. It's more about the reaction of voters and the election outcome. So B might not be the right choice either.\n\nOption C suggests that when scandals are more the responsibility of parties, the responsible party must be penalized. This aligns with the context because when the scandal is blamed on one party, that party's incumbents face defeat. So this seems relevant.\n\nOption D says that incumbents who are rightly blamed shouldn't seek re-election, but if they do, they shouldn't be returned. This is about the consequences of being blamed, but the context doesn't specify whether the blame is correct or not. It just talks about how voters distribute blame and the election results. So D might not be the best fit.\n\nPutting it all together, the principle that explains the contrast is about penalizing the party that's blamed for the scandal. So the correct answer should be C.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_10418",
    "context": "After all the cold dishes of a banquet are served, there are seven hot dishes. Among them, 3 Sichuan cuisine: K, L, M: 3 Cantonese cuisine: Q, N, P: one Shandong cuisine: X. Only one hot dish is served at a time, and the order of serving must meet the following conditions :(1) neither sichuan cuisine nor cantonese cuisine can be served consecutively. (2) P cannot go up before Q unless the second one goes up Q. (3) P must go up before X. (4) M must go up before K, and K must go up before N.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following statements is likely to be true if the fourth goes on K?",
    "options": [
      "A) The fifth upper p",
      "B) The sixth one goes to x",
      "C) The fifth upper l",
      "D) The first one goes to m"
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nAfter all the cold dishes of a banquet are served, there are seven hot dishes. Among them, 3 Sichuan cuisine: K, L, M: 3 Cantonese cuisine: Q, N, P: one Shandong cuisine: X. Only one hot dish is served at a time, and the order of serving must meet the following conditions :(1) neither sichuan cuisine nor cantonese cuisine can be served consecutively. (2) P cannot go up before Q unless the second one goes up Q. (3) P must go up before X. (4) M must go up before K, and K must go up before N.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following statements is likely to be true if the fourth goes on K?\n# Options:\nA) The fifth upper p\nB) The sixth one goes to x\nC) The fifth upper l\nD) The first one goes to m\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe principle that best explains the contrast is that when major political scandals are more the responsibility of a particular party, that party must be penalized when possible. This aligns with the context where voters' reactions lead to incumbents from the blamed party being defeated.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which principle best explains the situation described in the context. Let me break it down step by step.\n\nFirst, the context says that when a major political scandal happens before an election, voters blame the scandal on all parties equally. In this case, all incumbents, regardless of their party, get re-elected. That makes sense because if the scandal is spread across all parties, voters might not have a clear favorite to blame, so they stick with the current incumbents.\n\nBut then, if the voters blame the scandal on only one party, that party's incumbents are likely to lose. So, in this scenario, the party that's being blamed loses a lot, and other parties' challengers win. This seems to be about how voters perceive responsibility and how that affects the election outcome.\n\nNow, looking at the question, it's asking which principle explains this contrast. The options are A, B, C, D. Let me go through each one.\n\nOption A says that major political scandals can practically always be blamed on incumbents, but whether they should be removed depends on their challengers. Hmm, that doesn't quite fit. The context doesn't talk about whether the scandals should be blamed on incumbents or not, just how voters distribute blame. So maybe A isn't the best fit.\n\nOption B states that blaming incumbents from all parties is more accurate than blaming one party. Wait, the context doesn't mention accuracy of blame. It's more about the reaction of voters and the election outcome. So B might not be the right choice either.\n\nOption C suggests that when scandals are more the responsibility of parties, the responsible party must be penalized. This aligns with the context because when the scandal is blamed on one party, that party's incumbents face defeat. So this seems relevant.\n\nOption D says that incumbents who are rightly blamed shouldn't seek re-election, but if they do, they shouldn't be returned. This is about the consequences of being blamed, but the context doesn't specify whether the blame is correct or not. It just talks about how voters distribute blame and the election results. So D might not be the best fit.\n\nPutting it all together, the principle that explains the contrast is about penalizing the party that's blamed for the scandal. So the correct answer should be C.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_6014",
    "context": "Practice is the sole criterion for testing truth.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, according to this statement, which of the following statements is incorrect?",
    "options": [
      "A) Practice can test the truth.",
      "B) Only practice can test the truth.",
      "C) There must be other criterion to test the truth.",
      "D) No other criterion can test the truth."
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nPractice is the sole criterion for testing truth.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to this statement, which of the following statements is incorrect?\n# Options:\nA) Practice can test the truth.\nB) Only practice can test the truth.\nC) There must be other criterion to test the truth.\nD) No other criterion can test the truth.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n1. The context states that practice is the sole criterion for testing truth.\n2. Option C claims there must be other criteria, which directly opposes the context.\n3. Therefore, C is incorrect.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which statement is incorrect based on the given context. The context says, \"Practice is the sole criterion for testing truth.\" Let me break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, I need to understand what the context is saying. It mentions that practice is the only way to test truth. So, in other words, practice is the main or the only method used to determine if something is true.\n\nNow, looking at the question, it asks which statement is incorrect based on this context. The options are A, B, C, and D.\n\nLet me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: \"Practice can test the truth.\" Well, the context says practice is the sole criterion, which means it can definitely test the truth. So A seems correct.\n\nOption B: \"Only practice can test the truth.\" The context also states that practice is the sole criterion, which implies that nothing else can test the truth. So B also seems correct.\n\nOption C: \"There must be other criterion to test the truth.\" Wait, the context says practice is the only way, so this statement is saying the opposite. It suggests that other criteria exist, which isn't supported by the context. So C might be incorrect.\n\nOption D: \"No other criterion can test the truth.\" This aligns with the context because it's saying practice is the sole method, so no other criteria are needed. Therefore, D is correct.\n\nSo, putting it all together, the incorrect statement is C because it contradicts the given context.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_12556",
    "context": "Nick: The Pincus family and their construction company have supported our university financially for decades. The university should not give the contract for building its new library to the family' s main competitor. Doing so would be disloyal to a friend of the university. Pedro: Accepting a donation does not oblige the university to give the donor any special privileges. If it did, then it wouldn' t really be a charitable contribution. We should award the contract to whatever company makes the most competitive bid.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, the dialogue provides the most support for the claim that Nick and Pedro disagree over whether",
    "options": [
      "A) The pincus family and their construction company donated money for the purpose of acquiring special privileges from the university",
      "B) The acceptance of donations places a university under a special obligation to the donor",
      "C) The university should be more grateful to donors with a long history of financial support than to new donors",
      "D) Loyalty should sometimes be a consideration in making business decisions"
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nNick: The Pincus family and their construction company have supported our university financially for decades. The university should not give the contract for building its new library to the family' s main competitor. Doing so would be disloyal to a friend of the university. Pedro: Accepting a donation does not oblige the university to give the donor any special privileges. If it did, then it wouldn' t really be a charitable contribution. We should award the contract to whatever company makes the most competitive bid.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, the dialogue provides the most support for the claim that Nick and Pedro disagree over whether\n# Options:\nA) The pincus family and their construction company donated money for the purpose of acquiring special privileges from the university\nB) The acceptance of donations places a university under a special obligation to the donor\nC) The university should be more grateful to donors with a long history of financial support than to new donors\nD) Loyalty should sometimes be a consideration in making business decisions\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this question based on the dialogue between Nick and Pedro. Let me start by reading the context again carefully.\n\nNick says that the Pincus family, who have supported the university financially for decades, shouldn't be given the contract for the new library because it's disloyal to a friend of the university. He mentions that accepting a donation doesn't mean the university has to give the donor special privileges, and he argues that the contract should go to the company with the most competitive bid.\n\nPedro, on the other hand, supports Nick's point. He says that accepting a donation doesn't obligate the university to give the donor any special privileges. He also argues that the contract should be awarded to the company that makes the most competitive bid.\n\nWait, so both Nick and Pedro agree that accepting donations doesn't give the university special privileges. That part is the same. But Nick is concerned about loyalty to a friend, while Pedro is focused on fairness and competition.\n\nSo, the question is asking what claim do Nick and Pedro disagree on. The options are A, B, C, D.\n\nLooking at the options:\n\nA) The Pincus family donated money to get special privileges. I don't think that's what Nick is saying. He's against giving them the contract because of loyalty, not necessarily about them getting special privileges. So maybe A isn't the right answer.\n\nB) Accepting donations creates a special obligation. Both Nick and Pedro seem to disagree with this. Nick says donations don't create obligations, and Pedro supports that. So B might not be where they disagree.\n\nC) The university should be more grateful to long-term donors. That doesn't seem to be the point of their debate. They're more about the contract decision, not about gratitude. So C might not be the answer.\n\nD) Loyalty should sometimes be a consideration. That's exactly what Nick is saying. He's arguing that loyalty is a factor, while Pedro is more focused on competition and fairness. So D seems to capture their disagreement.\n\nWait, but let me make sure. The question is about what they disagree over regarding the claim. So the main issue is whether the university should give the contract to the Pincus family's competitor because of loyalty, versus just going with the best bid. They both agree that donations don't create special privileges, but Nick is using that as a reason to prefer loyalty over competition, while Pedro is just focusing on competition.\n\nSo the claim they're disagreeing over is whether loyalty should sometimes be considered. Therefore, the correct answer should be D.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_5543",
    "context": "Continuous contrast refers to the visual dislocation of color contrast observed and felt by the human eye in different time periods.Continuous comparison is divided into positive and negative images.Positive image refers to the visual state in which the image of the original object color is retained temporarily after the visual stimulation of the object is stopped.Negative image refers to the visual state in which the visual image is temporarily colored with the original object after the visual stimulation of the object is stopped.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following options is negative?",
    "options": [
      "A) A continuous projection of a static screen at 24 frames per second creates a dynamic picture in front of your eyes",
      "B) After staring at a red object, even if you move it away, you will still feel red appear in front of you",
      "C) After staring at red for a long time, the vision moves quickly to white, not white, but green",
      "D) Red is paired with yellow, and red is sometimes seen as a color with purple, and sometimes with yellow as a color with green"
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nContinuous contrast refers to the visual dislocation of color contrast observed and felt by the human eye in different time periods.Continuous comparison is divided into positive and negative images.Positive image refers to the visual state in which the image of the original object color is retained temporarily after the visual stimulation of the object is stopped.Negative image refers to the visual state in which the visual image is temporarily colored with the original object after the visual stimulation of the object is stopped.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following options is negative?\n# Options:\nA) A continuous projection of a static screen at 24 frames per second creates a dynamic picture in front of your eyes\nB) After staring at a red object, even if you move it away, you will still feel red appear in front of you\nC) After staring at red for a long time, the vision moves quickly to white, not white, but green\nD) Red is paired with yellow, and red is sometimes seen as a color with purple, and sometimes with yellow as a color with green\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: B\n\nThe reasoning is that Option B describes the retention of the original color (red) even after the object is moved away, fitting the definition of a negative image.\n\nThe correct option is: B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out which of the options A, B, C, or D is a negative image based on the given definitions. Let me start by understanding what continuous contrast, positive images, and negative images mean from the context provided.\n\nThe context says that continuous contrast is when there's a visual dislocation in color contrast over time. Positive images are when the original object's color is retained temporarily after the visual stimulation stops. Negative images are when the object is colored with the original color after the stimulation stops.\n\nSo, the question is asking which option describes a negative image. That means I'm looking for a situation where, after the object is no longer seen, the memory of its color still appears, causing a contrast.\n\nLet me go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: A continuous projection of a static screen at 24 frames per second creates a dynamic picture. Hmm, this sounds like it's talking about the visual effect of the screen itself, not about the retention of color after the object is removed. It's more about how the screen's movement creates a dynamic image, so probably not a negative image.\n\nOption B: After staring at a red object, even if you move it away, you still feel red. Wait, that sounds like the color is still perceived even after the object is moved. But the definition says negative image is when the image is colored with the original object's color after the stimulation stops. So if you move the object away, the stimulation stops, and the color is still perceived as red. That fits the definition of a negative image because the color is still there even after the object is removed.\n\nOption C: After staring at red for a long time, vision moves to white quickly, not white but green. This seems like a different kind of visual phenomenon, maybe related to afterimages or color shifts. It doesn't directly relate to the retention of the original color after the object is removed, so I don't think this is a negative image.\n\nOption D: Red paired with yellow, sometimes seen as purple, sometimes as green. This is talking about color mixing or perception, not about the retention of color after the object is removed. So it's more about how colors interact rather than the negative image concept.\n\nPutting it all together, Option B is the one where the color is still perceived after the object is moved away, which aligns with the definition of a negative image.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_5350",
    "context": "Humanistic care refers to a kind of universal human self-care, expressed as concern for personality, human value, human destiny and dignity, concern for people's living conditions and social status, people's needs for progress, and the guarantee of living conditions, and Affirmation and shaping of ideal personality.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the followings does not belong to humanistic care?",
    "options": [
      "A) Broad love for the people, deep understanding and selfless dedication.",
      "B) Concern for people's living conditions and care for people's lives and health.",
      "C) Deep care for people's mental state and human nature, respect for people's emotions, will and values.",
      "D) While emphasizing national interests, care about personal values and interests and personal suffering and predicament."
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nHumanistic care refers to a kind of universal human self-care, expressed as concern for personality, human value, human destiny and dignity, concern for people's living conditions and social status, people's needs for progress, and the guarantee of living conditions, and Affirmation and shaping of ideal personality.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the followings does not belong to humanistic care?\n# Options:\nA) Broad love for the people, deep understanding and selfless dedication.\nB) Concern for people's living conditions and care for people's lives and health.\nC) Deep care for people's mental state and human nature, respect for people's emotions, will and values.\nD) While emphasizing national interests, care about personal values and interests and personal suffering and predicament.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I need to figure out which option does not belong to humanistic care based on the given definition. Let me start by understanding what humanistic care is. The context says it's a universal human self-care that focuses on personality, human value, destiny, dignity, living conditions, social status, needs for progress, living conditions guarantee, and affirmation of ideal personality. \n\nSo, humanistic care is all about caring for the whole person, their well-being, and their potential. It's more about personal growth, dignity, and ensuring that people's basic needs and mental health are taken care of.\n\nNow, looking at the options:\n\nA) Broad love for the people, deep understanding, and selfless dedication. This sounds like it's about caring for people's emotions, understanding them, and dedicating oneself without seeking anything in return. That fits with humanistic care because it's about selfless care and understanding, which are part of caring for the whole person.\n\nB) Concern for living conditions and care for people's lives and health. This is about the physical environment and health, which are definitely part of humanistic care. It's about ensuring people have what they need to live comfortably and healthily.\n\nC) Deep care for mental state, respecting emotions, will, and values. This is about the psychological aspects of people, their mental well-being, and respecting their inner states. That's exactly what humanistic care is about\u2014caring for the person's personality and values.\n\nD) While emphasizing national interests, care about personal values, interests, and suffering. Hmm, this one is tricky. It mentions national interests but also personal values. The first part says \"while emphasizing national interests,\" which might mean that it's prioritizing the country's needs over individual ones. But humanistic care is more about individual well-being and personal values, not necessarily national interests. So this might not fit because it's conflicting with the focus on personal aspects.\n\nWait, but the option says \"while emphasizing national interests, care about personal values.\" So it's balancing both. But humanistic care is more about personal aspects, so if this option is emphasizing national interests more, it might not belong. Alternatively, maybe it's still okay because it's also caring about personal values. I'm a bit confused here.\n\nLet me think again. Humanistic care is about the person, their dignity, personality, values, etc. So if an option talks about national interests, that might not be part of humanistic care because it's more about the collective than the individual. So option D might be the one that doesn't belong because it's focusing more on national interests rather than personal ones.\n\nWait, but the option says \"while emphasizing national interests, care about personal values.\" So it's not entirely neglecting personal values but is also considering national interests. Maybe it's still within humanistic care because it's about personal values. Hmm, I'm not sure. Let me check the other options again.\n\nOption A is about broad love and selfless dedication, which is definitely humanistic. Option B is about living conditions and health, which is part of it. Option C is about mental state and values, which is also part of it. Option D is about national interests and personal values. Since humanistic care is more about personal aspects, maybe D is the one that doesn't belong because it's emphasizing national interests more than personal ones. Or perhaps it's still okay because it's also about personal values. I'm a bit torn here.\n\nWait, the question is asking which does NOT belong. So if D is emphasizing national interests, which might not be part of humanistic care, then D is the answer. Alternatively, if it's still about personal values, maybe it's okay. But I think the key is that humanistic care is about the person, so if an option is about the nation, it's not part of it. So D is the one that doesn't belong.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": true
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_11857",
    "context": "In Berinia, the age at which people could begin to drink alcohol legally used to be 18. In 1990, in an attempt to reduce alcohol consumption and thereby to reduce alcohol-related traffic deaths among Berinians under 21, the legal drinking age was raised to 21. Alcohol-related traffic deaths among people under 21 have decreased significantly since 1990. Nevertheless, surveys show that people in that age-group drink just as much alcohol as they did before 1990.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following, if true of Berinia, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?",
    "options": [
      "A) Alcohol-related traffic deaths among people over 21 have increased slightly since 1990.",
      "B) For the population as a whole, annual alcohol consumption is no lower now than it was in 1990.",
      "C) The proportion of people under 21 who own a car is higher now than it was in 1990.",
      "D) Alcohol consumption away from home, for example in bars and restaurants, is much lower among people under 21 than it was in 1990."
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nIn Berinia, the age at which people could begin to drink alcohol legally used to be 18. In 1990, in an attempt to reduce alcohol consumption and thereby to reduce alcohol-related traffic deaths among Berinians under 21, the legal drinking age was raised to 21. Alcohol-related traffic deaths among people under 21 have decreased significantly since 1990. Nevertheless, surveys show that people in that age-group drink just as much alcohol as they did before 1990.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following, if true of Berinia, most helps to resolve the apparent discrepancy?\n# Options:\nA) Alcohol-related traffic deaths among people over 21 have increased slightly since 1990.\nB) For the population as a whole, annual alcohol consumption is no lower now than it was in 1990.\nC) The proportion of people under 21 who own a car is higher now than it was in 1990.\nD) Alcohol consumption away from home, for example in bars and restaurants, is much lower among people under 21 than it was in 1990.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: D\n\nStep-by-step explanation:\n\nThe context describes that raising the legal drinking age from 18 to 21 in Berinia in 1990 aimed to reduce alcohol consumption among under-21s and consequently decrease alcohol-related traffic deaths. However, surveys show that under-21s are drinking just as much as before 1990, despite the legal age increase leading to a reduction in deaths. The question asks which option resolves this discrepancy.\n\nOption D suggests that alcohol consumption away from home (e.g., in bars and restaurants) is much lower among under-21s now than in 1990. This explains that even though under-21s are drinking the same total amount, they are spending less time drinking away from home. Reduced away-from-home drinking could lead to fewer instances of drunk driving, thereby decreasing alcohol-related traffic deaths. This aligns with the observed decrease in deaths while their total consumption remains unchanged.\n\nAnswer: D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out this logic problem. Let me read it again carefully.\n\nThe context is about Berinia, where the legal drinking age was raised from 18 to 21 in 1990. The reason was to reduce alcohol consumption and thus alcohol-related traffic deaths among people under 21. Since 1990, those deaths have decreased a lot. But here's the kicker: even though the legal age is higher, surveys show that people under 21 are drinking just as much as they did before 1990. So, the question is asking which option resolves this apparent discrepancy.\n\nHmm, so the main point is that raising the legal drinking age didn't lead to a decrease in alcohol consumption among under-21s, which contradicts the goal of reducing alcohol use. So, we need to find an option that explains why, even though the legal age is higher, people are still drinking the same amount.\n\nLet me look at the options one by one.\n\nOption A: Alcohol-related traffic deaths among people over 21 have increased slightly since 1990. Wait, that's interesting. The problem is about under-21s, but this talks about over-21s. I'm not sure how that connects. Maybe it's trying to say that if over-21s are drinking more, but under-21s are drinking the same, but the overall effect is still a decrease in under-21-related deaths. I'm not sure if this directly addresses the discrepancy. Maybe it's a distractor.\n\nOption B: For the population as a whole, annual alcohol consumption is no lower now than it was in 1990. So, overall, people are drinking at least as much as before. But the under-21s are drinking the same as before, which would mean that maybe the younger population isn't changing much, but perhaps the older population is compensating. But the question is about under-21s, so this might not directly explain why their consumption hasn't decreased despite the legal age change. It's more about overall consumption, not specifically the under-21 group.\n\nOption C: The proportion of people under 21 who own a car is higher now than it was in 1990. Hmm, owning a car might influence alcohol consumption. Maybe if people have cars, they go out more, which could lead to more alcohol consumption in bars or restaurants. But the option says that the proportion is higher, so maybe they're driving more, but does that mean they're drinking more? I'm not sure. It might be a possible factor, but I'm not convinced it's the best answer.\n\nOption D: Alcohol consumption away from home, like in bars and restaurants, is much lower among people under 21 than it was in 1990. Wait, so before 1990, a lot of alcohol was consumed at home, maybe through parties or gatherings. After raising the legal age, people under 21 are now drinking less at home but more away from home. But if away from home consumption is lower, that might mean overall consumption is lower. But the option says it's lower away from home, which could mean that overall consumption isn't necessarily lower because maybe they're drinking more at home now. Wait, no, the option says it's lower away from home, so maybe they're drinking less at home, but the same as before. Hmm, I'm getting confused.\n\nWait, let me think again. The key point is that under 21s are drinking the same amount as before, but the legal age increase led to a decrease in under-21-related deaths. So, the question is why are they drinking the same when the goal was to reduce it.\n\nOption D says that alcohol consumption away from home is much lower now. So, if they're drinking less away from home, but the same overall, that might mean they're drinking more at home. But the option says it's lower away from home, which could mean that their overall consumption is lower because they're drinking less away from home. But the option says \"much lower,\" so maybe their overall consumption is lower, which would explain the decrease in deaths. But wait, the question is about why they're drinking the same amount despite the legal age change. So, maybe if they're drinking less away from home, but more at home, but the total is the same as before. That doesn't make sense. Alternatively, maybe they're drinking the same amount, but less away from home, so more at home, which could explain why overall consumption is the same, but maybe the under-21s are still drinking the same as before, but perhaps the decrease in away-from-home drinking is offset by more home drinking, keeping the total the same. But I'm not sure.\n\nWait, maybe I'm overcomplicating. Let me think differently. The problem is that raising the legal age didn't lead to a decrease in under-21 alcohol consumption, which contradicts the goal. So, the options need to explain why, even though the legal age is higher, they're drinking the same.\n\nOption D says that away-from-home consumption is much lower now. So, if they're drinking less away from home, but the same overall, that might mean they're drinking more at home. But if they're drinking more at home, that could explain why their overall consumption is the same, but perhaps the decrease in away-from-home drinking is a factor in the overall decrease in alcohol-related deaths. Wait, no, the question is about why they're drinking the same amount as before, despite the legal age change.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the decrease in away-from-home drinking is a factor in the overall decrease in deaths because they're spending less time drinking away from home, so maybe they're not getting as much drunk driving or accidents. But the question is about why their consumption hasn't decreased, so maybe the fact that they're drinking less away from home but the same overall suggests that they're drinking more at home, which could be a factor in the decrease in deaths.\n\nWait, I'm getting tangled up. Let me try another approach. The key is that the legal age increase led to a decrease in under-21 alcohol-related deaths, but under-21s are drinking the same as before. So, the explanation is that even though they're drinking the same amount, the way they're drinking has changed. Maybe they're drinking less away from home, so they're not getting as much drunk driving or accidents, which would explain the decrease in deaths.\n\nSo, Option D says that away-from-home consumption is much lower now. That would mean that under-21s are drinking less in bars and restaurants, which are away from home. If they're drinking less there, maybe they're not getting as much drunk driving or accidents, which would explain why the deaths have decreased even though their total consumption hasn't gone down. So, the fact that they're drinking less away from home is a contributing factor to the decrease in deaths, even though their total consumption is the same.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the decrease in away-from-home drinking is part of why their overall consumption hasn't changed, but I'm not sure. Wait, the question is asking which option resolves the discrepancy, meaning it explains why they're drinking the same despite the legal age change leading to fewer deaths.\n\nSo, if Option D is true, it would mean that under-21s are drinking less away from home, which could mean they're not getting as much drunk driving or accidents, thus reducing the number of deaths. But their total consumption is the same, so maybe they're drinking more at home, which could be a factor in the decrease in deaths as well.\n\nWait, but the question is about why their consumption hasn't decreased, so maybe the fact that they're drinking less away from home is part of the reason why their overall consumption hasn't decreased, but the deaths have. Hmm, I'm getting confused.\n\nLet me think again. The problem is that raising the legal age led to fewer deaths, but under-21s are drinking the same as before. So, the explanation is that even though they're drinking the same amount, the way they're drinking has changed. Maybe they're drinking less away from home, so they're not getting as much drunk driving, which