  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_11932",
    "context": "It takes 365. 25 days for the Earth to make one complete revolution around the Sun. Long-standing convention makes a year 365 days long, with an extra day added every fourth year, and the year is divided into 52 seven-day weeks. But since 52 times 7 is only 364, anniversaries do not fall on the same day of the week each year. Many scheduling problems could be avoided if the last day of each year and an additional day every fourth year belonged to no week, so that January 1 would be a Sunday every year.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, the proposal above, once put into effect, would be most likely to result in continued scheduling conflicts for which one of the following groups?",
    "options": [
      "A) Employed people whose strict religious observances require that they refrain from working every seventh day",
      "B) People who have birthdays or other anniversaries on december 30 or 31",
      "C) People who have to plan events several years before those events occur",
      "D) Employed people who have three-day breaks from work when holidays are celebrated on mondays or fridays"
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nIt takes 365. 25 days for the Earth to make one complete revolution around the Sun. Long-standing convention makes a year 365 days long, with an extra day added every fourth year, and the year is divided into 52 seven-day weeks. But since 52 times 7 is only 364, anniversaries do not fall on the same day of the week each year. Many scheduling problems could be avoided if the last day of each year and an additional day every fourth year belonged to no week, so that January 1 would be a Sunday every year.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, the proposal above, once put into effect, would be most likely to result in continued scheduling conflicts for which one of the following groups?\n# Options:\nA) Employed people whose strict religious observances require that they refrain from working every seventh day\nB) People who have birthdays or other anniversaries on december 30 or 31\nC) People who have to plan events several years before those events occur\nD) Employed people who have three-day breaks from work when holidays are celebrated on mondays or fridays\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe reasoning is that Pancake Man is described as having a broad knowledge base but not requiring a deep explanation, which aligns with Option C.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out the answer to this question about Pancake Man based on the given context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context talks about \"Pancake people,\" who are described as intellectuals but with a different approach. Instead of focusing deeply on a particular area, they have a broad knowledge base but not in-depth expertise. They love money reading, which I think refers to consuming a lot of information, possibly through search engines. They don't really think critically or use their imagination much. Their \"noodles\" are as big as pancakes, which is a metaphor for their knowledge being broad but not necessarily deep. They can quickly obtain scarce information, especially the positive aspects, but this makes them reluctant to dive deep into any subject.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options A to D is true about Pancake Man. Let's go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Be good at learning new knowledge. The context says they have a basic knowledge in various fields but aren't proficient in any particular area. So, they might not be great at learning new knowledge deeply, but they can learn a bit about many things. I'm not sure if this is the best fit.\n\nOption B: Ability to learn by analogy. The context doesn't mention anything about learning through analogy. It talks more about their broad but shallow knowledge and their reluctance to delve into a subject deeply. So, I don't think this is correct.\n\nOption C: Keen to cover a wide range, but does not require a good explanation. The context does say they have a love for money reading, implying a wide range of knowledge, and they don't require a good explanation because they don't think deeply. This seems to align with the description.\n\nOption D: Occupy a large number of scarce information resources. The context mentions that they can obtain scarce information quickly, especially the positive side, but it doesn't say they occupy a lot of resources. Instead, it's more about the ease of access rather than occupying resources.\n\nPutting it all together, Option C seems to fit best because it captures both their broad knowledge and their lack of deep understanding or critical thinking.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_13560",
    "context": "Editorial: It has been suggested that private, for-profit companies should be hired to supply clean drinking water to areas of the world where it is unavailable now. But water should not be supplied by private companies. After all, clean water is essential for human health, and the purpose of a private company is to produce profit, not to promote health.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which one of the following principles, if valid, would most help to justify the reasoning in the editorial?",
    "options": [
      "A) The mere fact that something actually promotes human health is not sufficient to show that its purpose is to promote health.",
      "B) Drinking water should never be supplied by an organization that is not able to consistently supply clean, safe water.",
      "C) If something is necessary for human health, then it should be provided by an organization whose primary purpose is the promotion of health.",
      "D) A private company should not be allowed to supply a commodity that is essential to human health unless that commodity is also supplied by a government agency."
    ],
    "answer": "C",
