  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_11622",
    "context": "We ought to pay attention only to the intrinsic properties of a work of art. Its other, extrinsic properties are irrelevant to our aesthetic interactions with it. For example, when we look at a painting we should consider only what is directly presented in our experience of it. What is really aesthetically relevant, ttherefore, is not what a painting symbolizes, but what it directly presents to experience.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, the conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is added to the premises?",
    "options": [
      "A) There are certain properties of our experiences of artworks that can be distinguished as symbolic properties.",
      "B) Only an artwork's intrinsic properties are relevant to our aesthetic interactions with it.",
      "C) It is possible in theory for an artwork to symbolize nothing.",
      "D) What an artwork symbolizes involves only extrinsic properties of that work."
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nWe ought to pay attention only to the intrinsic properties of a work of art. Its other, extrinsic properties are irrelevant to our aesthetic interactions with it. For example, when we look at a painting we should consider only what is directly presented in our experience of it. What is really aesthetically relevant, ttherefore, is not what a painting symbolizes, but what it directly presents to experience.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, the conclusion follows logically if which one of the following is added to the premises?\n# Options:\nA) There are certain properties of our experiences of artworks that can be distinguished as symbolic properties.\nB) Only an artwork's intrinsic properties are relevant to our aesthetic interactions with it.\nC) It is possible in theory for an artwork to symbolize nothing.\nD) What an artwork symbolizes involves only extrinsic properties of that work.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe reasoning is that Pancake Man is described as having a broad knowledge base but not requiring a deep explanation, which aligns with Option C.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out the answer to this question about Pancake Man based on the given context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context talks about \"Pancake people,\" who are described as intellectuals but with a different approach. Instead of focusing deeply on a particular area, they have a broad knowledge base but not in-depth expertise. They love money reading, which I think refers to consuming a lot of information, possibly through search engines. They don't really think critically or use their imagination much. Their \"noodles\" are as big as pancakes, which is a metaphor for their knowledge being broad but not necessarily deep. They can quickly obtain scarce information, especially the positive aspects, but this makes them reluctant to dive deep into any subject.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options A to D is true about Pancake Man. Let's go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Be good at learning new knowledge. The context says they have a basic knowledge in various fields but aren't proficient in any particular area. So, they might not be great at learning new knowledge deeply, but they can learn a bit about many things. I'm not sure if this is the best fit.\n\nOption B: Ability to learn by analogy. The context doesn't mention anything about learning through analogy. It talks more about their broad but shallow knowledge and their reluctance to delve into a subject deeply. So, I don't think this is correct.\n\nOption C: Keen to cover a wide range, but does not require a good explanation. The context does say they have a love for money reading, implying a wide range of knowledge, and they don't require a good explanation because they don't think deeply. This seems to align with the description.\n\nOption D: Occupy a large number of scarce information resources. The context mentions that they can obtain scarce information quickly, especially the positive side, but it doesn't say they occupy a lot of resources. Instead, it's more about the ease of access rather than occupying resources.\n\nPutting it all together, Option C seems to fit best because it captures both their broad knowledge and their lack of deep understanding or critical thinking.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_12776",
    "context": "Myrna: People should follow diets in which fat represents no more than 30 percent of total calories, not the 37 percent the average diet in this country contains. Roland: If everyone in the country followed your recommendation during his or her entire life, just 0. 2 percent would lengthen their lives at all, and then only by an average of 3 months. Modifying our diet is not worthwhile. A lifetime of sacrifice spent eating an unappealing low-fat diet is too high a price to pay for the chance of extending that sacrifice for 3 months. Myrna: But for everyone who dies early from a high-fat diet, many more people suffer from serious chronic diseases because they followed such diets.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, myrna responds to Roland by",
    "options": [
      "A) Showing that the factors considered by roland are not the only ones relevant in evaluating her recommendation",
      "B) Demonstrating that the statistics used by roland to dispute her recommendation are inaccurate",
      "C) Pointing out that roland's argument assumes the very proposition it sets out to prove",
      "D) Disputing the correctness of the facts cited by roland and offering facts that she considers correct"
    ],
    "answer": "A",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nMyrna: People should follow diets in which fat represents no more than 30 percent of total calories, not the 37 percent the average diet in this country contains. Roland: If everyone in the country followed your recommendation during his or her entire life, just 0. 2 percent would lengthen their lives at all, and then only by an average of 3 months. Modifying our diet is not worthwhile. A lifetime of sacrifice spent eating an unappealing low-fat diet is too high a price to pay for the chance of extending that sacrifice for 3 months. Myrna: But for everyone who dies early from a high-fat diet, many more people suffer from serious chronic diseases because they followed such diets.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, myrna responds to Roland by\n# Options:\nA) Showing that the factors considered by roland are not the only ones relevant in evaluating her recommendation\nB) Demonstrating that the statistics used by roland to dispute her recommendation are inaccurate\nC) Pointing out that roland's argument assumes the very proposition it sets out to prove\nD) Disputing the correctness of the facts cited by roland and offering facts that she considers correct\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe reasoning is that Pancake Man is described as having a broad knowledge base but not requiring a deep explanation, which aligns with Option C.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
