  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_9133",
    "context": "The first bicycle in the world was invented in 1847. Bicycles disappeared only a short time after the emergence of bicycles and did not reappear until 1860. why did this happen? A new technology will be accepted only when it is consistent with the concept of social values. So there must have been some change in social values between 1817 and 1860.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following options is the cause of the defect in the above reasoning?",
    "options": [
      "A) Ignoring the possibility that bicycles will be accepted again.",
      "B) This paper puts forward a question which has little to do with the conclusion of the argument.",
      "C) The mistaken belief that the bicycle reappeared in 1860 shows that it has been re-accepted.",
      "D) It is assumed that it has been truly accepted without giving a reasonable explanation."
    ],
    "answer": "B",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nThe first bicycle in the world was invented in 1847. Bicycles disappeared only a short time after the emergence of bicycles and did not reappear until 1860. why did this happen? A new technology will be accepted only when it is consistent with the concept of social values. So there must have been some change in social values between 1817 and 1860.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following options is the cause of the defect in the above reasoning?\n# Options:\nA) Ignoring the possibility that bicycles will be accepted again.\nB) This paper puts forward a question which has little to do with the conclusion of the argument.\nC) The mistaken belief that the bicycle reappeared in 1860 shows that it has been re-accepted.\nD) It is assumed that it has been truly accepted without giving a reasonable explanation.\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
