    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_3258",
    "context": "Self depletion phenomenon is a phenomenon in which individuals' ability or willingness to conduct voluntary activities decline in the process of long-term work, including the decline in ability or willingness to control the environment, control themselves, make choices and initiate behaviors, and at the same time, job burnout, self depletion but no progress.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following is self depletion phenomenon?",
    "options": [
      "A) Lee did not finish his doctoral thesis and continued to postpone it. as a result, he did not graduate from school for eight years",
      "B) Chen has worked as a deputy director of the magazine for 20 years, and although he has published some personal works, he has not been promoted",
      "C) Song mou do sales work for nearly ten years, although he changed four or five companies, but because the performance is average, the income has not changed",
      "D) Wang has been working as an archive administrator for 15 years. he feels numb to his work and has no desire to change the status quo and get promoted"
    ],
    "answer": "D",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nSelf depletion phenomenon is a phenomenon in which individuals' ability or willingness to conduct voluntary activities decline in the process of long-term work, including the decline in ability or willingness to control the environment, control themselves, make choices and initiate behaviors, and at the same time, job burnout, self depletion but no progress.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, according to the above definition, which of the following is self depletion phenomenon?\n# Options:\nA) Lee did not finish his doctoral thesis and continued to postpone it. as a result, he did not graduate from school for eight years\nB) Chen has worked as a deputy director of the magazine for 20 years, and although he has published some personal works, he has not been promoted\nC) Song mou do sales work for nearly ten years, although he changed four or five companies, but because the performance is average, the income has not changed\nD) Wang has been working as an archive administrator for 15 years. he feels numb to his work and has no desire to change the status quo and get promoted\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe reasoning is that Pancake Man is described as having a broad knowledge base but not requiring a deep explanation, which aligns with Option C.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out the answer to this question about Pancake Man based on the given context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context talks about \"Pancake people,\" who are described as intellectuals but with a different approach. Instead of focusing deeply on a particular area, they have a broad knowledge base but not in-depth expertise. They love money reading, which I think refers to consuming a lot of information, possibly through search engines. They don't really think critically or use their imagination much. Their \"noodles\" are as big as pancakes, which is a metaphor for their knowledge being broad but not necessarily deep. They can quickly obtain scarce information, especially the positive aspects, but this makes them reluctant to dive deep into any subject.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options A to D is true about Pancake Man. Let's go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Be good at learning new knowledge. The context says they have a basic knowledge in various fields but aren't proficient in any particular area. So, they might not be great at learning new knowledge deeply, but they can learn a bit about many things. I'm not sure if this is the best fit.\n\nOption B: Ability to learn by analogy. The context doesn't mention anything about learning through analogy. It talks more about their broad but shallow knowledge and their reluctance to delve into a subject deeply. So, I don't think this is correct.\n\nOption C: Keen to cover a wide range, but does not require a good explanation. The context does say they have a love for money reading, implying a wide range of knowledge, and they don't require a good explanation because they don't think deeply. This seems to align with the description.\n\nOption D: Occupy a large number of scarce information resources. The context mentions that they can obtain scarce information quickly, especially the positive side, but it doesn't say they occupy a lot of resources. Instead, it's more about the ease of access rather than occupying resources.\n\nPutting it all together, Option C seems to fit best because it captures both their broad knowledge and their lack of deep understanding or critical thinking.\n",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_answer": "",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_result": false
  },
  {
    "id": "LOGIQA_dev_6980",
    "context": "I don't agree with you, but I will defend to the death your right to speak. this sentence is said to have been said by Voltaire, a French enlightenment thinker and known as French conscience, as a symbolic symbol of defending freedom of expression. this sentence has been spared no effort to promote it to the world by the European and American media and politicians. surprisingly, after textual research that Voltaire has not said this sentence. This seems to poke a hole in the Western argument of freedom of speech. According to a Chinese scholar who has lived in Europe and the United States for many years, there are 18 kinds of freedom of speech in the United States that are not protected, and the content of the European Convention on Human Rights restricting freedom of speech is twice as long as the content of freedom of speech.",
    "question": "Based on the above information, which of the following is the main emphasis of this text?",
    "options": [
      "A) There are double standards of freedom of speech in the west",
      "B) Freedom of speech does not exist in the west",
      "C) There is no absolute freedom of speech in the west",
      "D) Someone coined the famous saying of freedom of speech in the name of voltaire"
    ],
    "answer": "C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_input": "Please act as a math teacher and reason step by step to solve the logical reasoning problem. Given a context and a question, explain your reasoning process and give the answer with the format \"\\nThe correct option is: A/B/C/D\".\n####\n# Context:\nI don't agree with you, but I will defend to the death your right to speak. this sentence is said to have been said by Voltaire, a French enlightenment thinker and known as French conscience, as a symbolic symbol of defending freedom of expression. this sentence has been spared no effort to promote it to the world by the European and American media and politicians. surprisingly, after textual research that Voltaire has not said this sentence. This seems to poke a hole in the Western argument of freedom of speech. According to a Chinese scholar who has lived in Europe and the United States for many years, there are 18 kinds of freedom of speech in the United States that are not protected, and the content of the European Convention on Human Rights restricting freedom of speech is twice as long as the content of freedom of speech.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which of the following is the main emphasis of this text?\n# Options:\nA) There are double standards of freedom of speech in the west\nB) Freedom of speech does not exist in the west\nC) There is no absolute freedom of speech in the west\nD) Someone coined the famous saying of freedom of speech in the name of voltaire\n\n# Instruction:\n## Reasoning:",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_output": "\n\nThe correct option is: C\n\nThe reasoning is that Pancake Man is described as having a broad knowledge base but not requiring a deep explanation, which aligns with Option C.\n\nThe correct option is: C",
    "cot0shot.math teacher_thinking": "\nOkay, so I'm trying to figure out the answer to this question about Pancake Man based on the given context. Let me read through the context again to make sure I understand it correctly.\n\nThe context talks about \"Pancake people,\" who are described as intellectuals but with a different approach. Instead of focusing deeply on a particular area, they have a broad knowledge base but not in-depth expertise. They love money reading, which I think refers to consuming a lot of information, possibly through search engines. They don't really think critically or use their imagination much. Their \"noodles\" are as big as pancakes, which is a metaphor for their knowledge being broad but not necessarily deep. They can quickly obtain scarce information, especially the positive aspects, but this makes them reluctant to dive deep into any subject.\n\nNow, the question is asking which of the options A to D is true about Pancake Man. Let's go through each option one by one.\n\nOption A: Be good at learning new knowledge. The context says they have a basic knowledge in various fields but aren't proficient in any particular area. So, they might not be great at learning new knowledge deeply, but they can learn a bit about many things. I'm not sure if this is the best fit.\n\nOption B: Ability to learn by analogy. The context doesn't mention anything about learning through analogy. It talks more about their broad but shallow knowledge and their reluctance to delve into a subject deeply. So, I don't think this is correct.\n\nOption C: Keen to cover a wide range, but does not require a good explanation. The context does say they have a love for money reading, implying a wide range of knowledge, and they don't require a good explanation because they don't think deeply. This seems to align with the description.\n\nOption D: Occupy a large number of scarce information resources. The context mentions that they can obtain scarce information quickly, especially the positive side, but it doesn't say they occupy a lot of resources. Instead, it's more about the ease of access rather than occupying resources.\n\nPutting it all together, Option C seems to fit best because it captures both their broad knowledge and their lack of deep understanding or critical thinking.\n",
